      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>letttas In the</head>
        <p>letttas In the</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CI • Tllll EDITOR of "The Examiner" invites corre spondence on all subjectii of public interest Lrrrsas should be brief and pointed. Their object nrust be to impart or seek information, not abuse.</p>
        <p>RRESPONDENTS. LErrsgs must in all cases be accompanied by the name and address of the writer. Comm=Axiom received early in the week— except urgent matters—can always be given the best attention. '</p>
        <p>we ought not to expect too much, but set to work p 3 returk more to help them. To do what? Screw the weiker still tighter and tighter. No, brother workers, it is not more Labour members we want; it is not to bother about Parliament, but to concentrate al/ our energies in our trade union lodges and spread the idea for a more militant policy, and instead of discussing Par- liamentarr levies, discuss the taking over of our particular industry and running it along with others in other industries for the benefit of the whole community. And when that is done, all members of Parliament will be asking for jobs.</p>
        <p>. TRADE UNIONIST.</p>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES ADULT SCHOOL Sir,— I uee Mr. J. W. Bradley, in your last issue, takes of theakesex drink question ception gtuoasotn in a discussionatementnieu at a ht the above school, as reported in your columns a week previous. This he is quite entitled to do, We live in a comparatively free country. The statement which he criticises was made, but it was also vigorously combated at the time it was uttered by several members of the School. I I should like to state that in the five Men's Adult Schools in Warrington we have men of all shades of religious, political and other opinion, and in the discussions which arise out of the topics of the day, everyone is perfectly free to express his opinion, no matter whether it is orthodox or heterodox. The Adult School is not, however, responsible for the individual opinion of any member. The platform of the Adult School is broad enough and big enough for all. But if a man expresses an opinion, such as the one which Mr. Bradley criticised, he must take his chance and defend it. The individual concerned in this instance is quite capable of looking after himself; whether he will do so or not is for himself to decide. I am afraid Mr. „Bradley's challenge is not a very praCticable one: 'lt would be very difficult to fix such a test as he proposes, and if it were possible I do not feel sure that the results would prove much. But I think another way out may be found. Will Mr. Bradley be willing to come down to Sankey Bridges School some Sunday morning, and give us a lecturette upon his side of the subject, or on a Sunday afternoon to our Cur- rent Events Class and have a discussion? Our men would be most delighted to hear what he may have to say, he will be cordially welcomed, and will get a eoufteous and sym.pathetio hear- ing. The secretary's address is 17, Barnard-- street.</p>
        <p>THE PRESIDENT OF THE SCHOOL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>INCREASING RENTS.</head>
        <p>INCREASING RENTS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am not conscious of having referred to my success in this discussion, and cannot there- fore have written any eulogy in regard to it. Neither am I concerned with Mr. Smith's esti- mate of the measure of it, preferring to leave the matter to the more unbiased judgment of your readers. Mr. Smith should learn to keep to the point. I have tried to show him the way, and have refrained from side-tracking on to politics, as I was given to understand by him that they were not wanted. I Dever mentioned the People's Budget, except briefly to refer to it in response to a few remarks of his, and yet he says "the People's Budget has missed fire." Neither have I mentioned Free Trade or Tariff Reform, but if he desires to discuss any of these, and will state his case against either of the two former, or support the last-named policy, through your columns. I have no doubt he will meet with a ready response, not only from myself, but from others who have not changed their political colour through interested motives. A. HORNBY RATIIBONE.</p>
        <p>Thelwall New-road, Latchford Without, February 11th, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>FREEDOM FELLOWSHIP DISCUSSION.</head>
        <p>FREEDOM FELLOWSHIP DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>Sir.—Mr. Gilbert Manion has thought fit to address you respecting the conduct of the meet- ing held by the Freedom Fellowship on Monday night, February 2nd, and addressed by Guy Bowman. He protests against what he terms the deletion of discussion. It would, I think, be extremely difficult for Mr. Manion to get any unbiased person who was present at the meeting to support his pro- test. At the close of Guy Bowman's address, the chairman repeatedly invited questions, and after a number had been submitted and replied to, and when apparently no further questions were going to be asked, the meeting was declared, closed. Though the time allotted had been ex- ceeded, there was absolutely no desire to shirk discussion, neither was there a proverbial train nor an actual one to catch. Mr. Manion had ample opportunity to ask questions, and his ex- perience of meetings is sufficient to make him very familiar with the ease of transition from question to discussion. His charge against the Freedom Fellowship is entirely unfounded. If, however, he has still a desire to unburden him- self, another opportunity will be his next Friday night, the 20th inst., as he will see, from your advertising columns, that Nun Nicholas is to delivat in the Freedom Fellowship Rooms a lecture on "The Futility of Political Action," a subject kindred to Gu_y Bo_wman'a. -- The policy of -the Freedom Fellowship being, 'according to Mr, Manion, "vague and indeter- minate," surely he will see that it provides greater room for agreement in difference than does a defined, fixed and positive policy. Hence it is difficult for him to find a motive which would cause the Fellowship to discourage dis- cussion.</p>
        <p>THE CHAIRMAN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>STRIKES AND POLITICS.</head>
        <p>STRIKES AND POLITICS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—As one of the rank and file, with your permission, I would like to intervene at this Juncture on the above subject, for I think your readers will agree when I say that your two com- batants, Mr. Dukes and "Labor Omnia Vincit7 have had sufficient time to show how not to deal with it. Whether Mr. Dukes is anxious to be a Labour member of Parliament, or whether the Co-operative Society shall build houses, doesn't matter, for if one or both were estab- lished facts, we should still be wage-slaves, so what we might to be concerned with is ways and means of getting rid of wage-slavery or wagedom.</p>
        <p>H'</p>
        <p>,</p>
        <p>*</p>
        <p>b</p>
        <p>*1</p>
        <p>So I think it would serve a useful purpose to discuss the merits or demerits of industrial action v. political action. Now I daresay some people will say industrial action has been tried and failed, and will cite Dublin and South Africa to prove it. To those people who use this question, I would answer: It has failed be- cause it has never been tried. For to allow a small section of the workers' army to fight the well-organised forces of the capitalists, the rest of the workers aiding them, and then to have the effrontery to say that industrial action has been tried shows what little thought has been given to the matter. What I would call indus- trial action is that when one battalion of the Workers' army is attacked the rest of the army mobilise and get ready for the signal, and it is my opinion that is all that would he necessary to make the capitalists put up the white flag. Some will say that this is a tall order, but I don't consider it is if the workers in their lodges and meetings of the trades councils dis- cussed questions of how to meet the capitalists' attack, instead of passing pious resolutions and sending them on to their avowed opponents, Tory members of Parliament. This brings me to the other question, political action. We are continually having it drummed in our ears that what we want is more Labour men in Parliament if we desire a better state of things; that it is no use expecting a handful (40 of them) to bring about much change. You hear the Socialists always crying out from their platforms, as an excuse for the miserable at- tempt on 'the part of those already there, that</p>
        <p>endowed</p>
