      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>abitor.</head>
        <p>abitor.</p>
        <p>ELECTRICITY SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gough's Determined Opposition at the li.G.B:lnquiry.</p>
        <p>IS CURRENT SOLD TOO CHEAP?</p>
        <p>Our electricity works are inadequate for. the demands of the town, and the Corporation have applied for a loan of 114,550 for the extension of the undertaking. Mr. T. C. Ekin conducted an inquiry into the application at the Town Hall on Thursday morning. Practically no public interest was taken in the proceedings, and the only objector was Councillor Gough, whose objections Mr. Hallaway, the deputy town clerk, thought not worthy of reply. • There were present Mr. Hallaway (deputy town clerk), Mr. Spinks (assistant town clerk), Mr. J. Fair- hurst (borough treasurer), Mr. W. H. Wood- cock (borough collector), Councillor Dr. Joseph (chairman Electricity Committee), Alderman Smethurst, Alderman John Evans, Councillort Gough and Pemberton, Mi. Mathias • (engi- neer), and Mrs Silcock (architeCt). In answer to the Inspector, Mr. Mathias said the application was wholly in connection with the generating station. The work had not been commenced. There had been an increase of 200,000 units the previous year, although in 1912 there was a drop, due to slackness in trade. They had provisionally accepted the tender.f or the extensions. Mr. Mathias and Mr. Fairhurst having an- swered other formal questions, the Inspector said the only correspondence they had had was from the Town Clerk, appealing in very piteous tones for an early inquiry. Mr. Fairhurst said they had £756 left out of loans which they had not used, and the Inspec- tor suggested that they send the sanction back and get clear. The Inspector was also told that the Cor- poration were contemplating a loan for mains and service, and he said he would mention the matter. It was rather a pity, he said, that they had not had the amount in their present appli- cation. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallaway : We will make fortnal appli- cation this morning. We would ask for £3,000. The Inspector: What is the total amount of your profits since the undertaking was com- menced.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallway: £19,406. Mr. Fairhurst said that they lost in the first four years, but they made it up out of rates, and not out of profits. They lost on the aggregate £1,632. Of the net profit of £17,774, £9,358 had been placed to the reserve fund. The Inspector: §uch a happy result has been achieved by keeping down your capital charges. I should think, considering the output, your capital charges are as low as any in the coun- try. The true reserve fund is £9,358, and I believe £9,000 is invested. Mr. Fairhurst: That is so. The Inspector: Is there any opposition at this inquiry? Councillor Gough: Yes.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR GOUGH'S OPPOSITION. The Inspector: You oppose the application? Councillor Gough: I do. I ask, Is one of the reasons for this extension because of the appli- cations you have had from outside and inside the district? Mr. Hallaway : I answer that in another way. The principal reason for the loan is that the Corporation have not a sufficient margin of safety for their present demand. The maxi- mum capacity of the plant is 3,150 kilowats, and the maximum demand is 2,409 kilowats. It is obvious that if our one thousand kwt. motor broke down we should not be able to supply that demand, and since December we have made con- nections and. contracted for connections amount- ing to 422 kilowats, which bring us to our maximum capacity. The applications are from people within the borough. We have no powers to go outside the borough, with the exception of supplying the power for the new road bridge on the Liverpool boundary, which is being con- structed for the Lancashire County Council. It is about 20 yards over the boundary. We have no margin of safety and no business concern will carry- on business with the know- ledge that if anything happened to their motors or boilers that we should not be able to supply what we contracted to do. It will be impos- sible to take our consumers out of the borough, without special powers. It has been suggested that we should supply Stockton Heath. Over- tures were made, and the Corporation were pre- pared to supply on favourable terms, but acting as they were advised, and with a wisdom that has become notorious, they decided not to accept the offer, and came to terms with an- other power company, with the result that they tied out anyone else for three years. These three years have elapsed and they have no elec- tricity. Special powers and • a request will be required before we can take up a supply. The present application will increase our capacity by another 1,000 kilowats, and that is considered by the engineer to be a margin of safety for three years. If there was an extension to out- side districts—we have had application from Penketh. Our returns show that: we supply at a profit. We supply at an extremely low rate in individual cases.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gough : Too low. The Inspector: Still the'result is very happy.</p>
        <p>" BELOW COST PRICE." Councillor Gough: We supply below cost price. . .</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Hallaway : There is no case in which we supply below cost price. Councillor Gough referred to the report on the subject, and was quoting figures, when Mr. Hallaway said: The words of a well- known proverb, which were applied to me, I will refer to Councillor Gough, "A little knowledge." •That was said to Me when I went through the figures.</p>
        <p>_ vertising current at one half Councillor Gough: Are the Corporation ad- Mr. Hallaway : I have not the slightest idea. Councillor Gough: You have not the slightest idea? penny per unit?</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallway : There is a pamphlet drawn up on it by the committee, of which Councillor Pemberton is chairman. I am informed by the engineer that we are not advertising it at that price. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor Gough I was shown by the Town Clerk this week.</p>
        <p>Mr. Holloway: The book has not been pub- lished.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gough: Don't you think it is mis- leading manufacturers?</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallaway : I think you are misleading them if you say it was advertised.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gough: In a report of April 15th it is stated that the capacity was demonstrated. Could you give the result of that exhibition? Mr. Hallaway : Mr. Mathias demonstrated the facts I have put before you this morning. Councillor Gough: Can you give the names of those who have applied for current? .Mr. Mathias: I don't think it advisable to give names. I have had applications from firms —one asking for 200 kwts., rising to 1,000 kwts.</p>
        <p>The Inspector: The potential manufacturer wants to know if he can be guaranteed a supply? Mr. Mathias: We have no plant at our dis- posal, and we lost customers.</p>
        <p>' CRYING IT AT id. Councillor Gough: What price could you take it at? Mr. Mathias: There was no price quoted. W e are not in a position to. supply. , Councillor Gough: If we were in a• position to supply, would you be prepared to supply At one halfpenny a unit? Mr. Mathias: It is a good price: nuclei some conditions. -</p>
        <p>Councillor Gough: Are there three or four persons in Warrington who have instalments I for generating electricity, who made application for I)ower because you vrere crying it at a half- pehny? Mr. Itatlilas: We liasve 'applications from</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>HOW COSTS ARE MADE OUT.</head>
        <p>HOW COSTS ARE MADE OUT.</p>
        <p>The Inspector:. having explained to Councillor Gough how the costs were arrived at, Mr. Halls: way said Mr. Mathias invited Councillor Gough down to the works to show him how the costs were arrived at.</p>
        <p>_ The Inspector (to Councillor Gough) : You take my advice and accept it. Councillor Gough:' I say it is hard work to get information on the Council. We want to get in touch with these things. There are a lot of old hens on this committee who don't want to be removed. They don't want new blood .on this committee.'</p>
        <p>Councillor Gough also asked: Are you work- ing day and night to supply 40 firms at one penny per unit. Mr. Mathias: We are bound to run day and. night.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodcock: Some of the firms pay one penny, but twopence for the first quarter. Councillor Gough objected on the grounds that no Corporation ought to have the monopoly _ _</p>
        <p>in the supply of electriC en;;;;i..- The Corpor- ation were selling at a price less than cost, which was three farthings per unit. A gross injustice was being thine ratepayers, especially those who used electric light, for they were charged 4d. per unit. A further injustice was I being done to the Tramways Committee, for they were -charged lid. per unit. If the Cor- poration charged one penny, the committee could well afford to charge halfpenny fares for short stages. Private users for lighting Pur- poses were paying twopence too much. The Local Government Board ought not to suPPort that application unless the Corporation were willing to make a maximum charge of One penny per unit. Mr. Hallaway : I don't wish to pay Councillor Gough the compliment of assuming that there is anything in the statements he has made, so I will not reply, although I could do so. Alderman Smethurst supported the applica- tion. It was a most desirable extension. It had been difficult to keep going with the present</p>
        <p>facilities. Alderman John Evans also supported and Mr. Hallway urged upon the Board the necessity for expediting the sanction. They could rePiY to Councillor Gough, and would do so if tne Inspector wished it, but to anyone who had the slightest acquaintance with the subject it would be a waste of time answering such arguments. Councillor Gough: The object is to safeguard the ratepayers. ' Mr. Hallaway : We shall be pleased to give anyone particulars who want them. (near, hear.) The inquiry was closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
