      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>DISEASES OF ANIMALS.</head>
        <p>DISEASES OF ANIMALS.</p>
        <p>- In accordance with the notice issued by the Irish Department of Agriculture on Thursday night the Port of Dublin was re-opened on Fri- day morning for the shipment of cattle, sheep and pigs for immediate slaughter at the land- ing-places in Great Britain, and ;the first con- signment of cattle shipped under these condi- tions left for Birkenhead on Friday night. There were 57 outbreaks of swine fever in the week ended January 31s, and 378 pigs were slaughtered. All the outbreaks except three in Wales were 'reported. from 20 counties in England. Of anthrax 21 fresh cases were noti- fied, in which 20 cattle and one horse were attacked, and there were two cases of glanders attacking two animals. Fresh outbreaks of , sheep scab were notified from eight centres in five English counties, six centres in three coun- ties in Wales, and five localities in Inverness- shire, while of parasitic mange there were 86 fresh cases attacking 211 animals. In January the number of premises on which the existence of tuberculosis was notified was 472, and 494 animals were certified for slaughter.</p>
      </div>
