      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. R. T. Fairclough (in the chair), Alderman Tinnion, and Messrs. A. J. Jolley and T. Pemberton.</p>
        <p>A DEAR RAILWAY RlDE.—Silas Slater, alias Taylor, hawker, of 33, Durham-place, Hulme, Manchester, was charged with travelling on the Cheshire Lines Railway on February 6th with- out paying his fare and with intent to defraud. —P.C. Clarke, of the Cheshire Lines Police, said he was on the platform when the 12.30 , from Manchester drew up in the station. He saw prisoner and three other passengers alight from a third-class compartment. Later he saw, the ticket collector rush down the steps after the prisoner, who stoutly denied that he had travelled by the train.—P.C. Cooper, of the Manchester City Police Force, travelled by the same train, and stated that he' saw prisoner get into the train at Manchester.--prisoner said a man in Warrington owed him 1;3, and as his mother was ill he came over to try to get the money.—Alderman Tinnion : And you wanted the Cheshire Lines, to help you?—The Chief Constable stated that to use the prisoner's own words, he had come to Warrington to "pinch all he could lay his hands on." Prisoner ' originally denied that he had travelled by the train, and said he had been in Warrington for , the past three or four weeks and gave three addresses where he said he had slept during this period. Upon inquiry the police ascer- tained that prisoner had not stayed at the ad- dresses. The Chief Constable read a very long list of previous convictions, whereupon the pri- soner exclaimed: "If I am down I have to be kept down. They give me the full penalty of the law every time and don't, give me a chance." —The Chief Constable took exception to this saying that all the sentences he had received with the exception of one were nearly the mini- mum.—He was fined 40s. and costs.—Prisoner (sarcastically): Another chance I have got!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>MONDAY.</head>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Bol- ton, Smethurst and James Evans, Messrs. W. Roberts, S: Wright, G. Pilling, W. Furness</p>
        <p>and T. tacker.</p>
        <p>A -Cos= " ScsNe."—Alfred Green, Corpora- tion Cottages, Warrington, had to pay se. and costs for the scene he caused in Rylands-street on Saturday,night, when, in a drunken condi- tion, he wnted to fight the attendants at the Royal Court Theatre, and also kicked P.C. An- drews, who arrested him, in the leg. On the way to the Police Station he threw himself down. The constable named, with Sergt. Met- calfe and civilians, got him up and "down."</p>
        <p>" NOT Am THAT GLITTERS."—Patrick Postle- thwaite, 8, Morris-court, tried to trick Mrs. Shaw, shopkeeper, 37, Fennel-street, by tender- ing a polished farthing for one shilling's worth of goods. Mrs. Shaw gave him 9s. change, but later discovered her mistake, and prisoner was arrested.—Prisoner said he only came out of gaol that (Saturday) morning, and a drop of drink got over him.—The Chief Constable said the Police Court Missioner had the money to buy the an a " stock-in-trade "as a peg maker, but the man never came near Alr. Laws.—lie was sent to prison for three months.</p>
        <p>ON THE HANDCART.—Patrick Collins, 4, Black- hurst-street, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly, and also with assaulting P.C. Wise.—P.C. Clarke said the prisoner was lying across the footpath in 131ackhurst-street. Tie commenced kicking right and left, and said, "You are not going to take me down," He was carried to Warrington Bridge, where three officers put him in a handcart, on which he was conveyed to the Police Station.—The Chief Con- stable said Collins made his 26th appearance. He was mad when in drink. The repeated as- saults on the police were far from satisfactory, and the Corporation would be the sufferers if a man was so injured that he had to be pensioned. —Collins was sent to prison for two months, with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair), Alderman James Evans, Colonel Edelsten, and Messrs. T. Locker, J. Rymer Young and C.</p>
        <p>Jackson Holmes.</p>
        <p>- ------- RAILWAY CARRIAGE SCENE.—Richard Williams, a respectable-looking man, of 129, Bostock-street, was charged with interfering with the comfort of certain passengers on the L. and N.-W. Rail- way on December 23, and also with breaking a glass window of a railway carriage door on the same day.—Mr. Eady, solicitor, Of London, prosecuted on behalf of the railway company. Defendant had been to the station to see his, sister off to London en route for Australia. Apparently someone insulted his sister, and de- fendant objected. He was under the influence of drink at the time, and after the train had departed he saw Mr. W. S. Oddy, a bank clerk, and accused him of insulting the lady. Defend- ant wanted to fight, and when Mr. Oddy went down to the low level platform he followed and got in the same compartment, and in continu- ing the allegation he became very objection- able. Mr. Oddy - went into another compart- ment with another passenger, and when defend- ant followed Mr. Oddy endeavoured .to prevent him getting in by holding the handle. It was then alleged that Williams put his fist through the window. A railway detective was called and defendant wanted to fight him also. Wil- . . Hams had since written a letter to the' company apologising for his conduct, but the company were bound to take the proceedings as a warn- ing. The Chairman thought they were not dealing with the type of man who usually figured in such proceedings, and upon the de- fendant undertaking to apologise and pay the whole of the costs, the case was aismissed. HIT "WITH A BABY."—Mary Ellen Leigh, of 29, LoOkett-street, was summoned by Miry Ellen Bate, 23, Thelwall-lane, for assault.—lt was alleged that defendant hit the prosecutrix on the side of the head with her baby, after some trouble about defendant's daughter.—The as- sault was denied.—Defendant was ordered to pay 10s. RAILWAY PaoPEarv.7—Thomas Kennedy, of 85, Oldham-street, was charged with, during the past twelve months, stealing from the L. and N.-W. Railway's premises a quantity of wearing apparel.—'-A week's remand was asled for.— Prisoner pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that Kennedy had been employed as a goods porter and guard foi' eight or ten years. An application was made to the magistrates•the previous day by prisoner's wife, but it was not granted, and subsequently she gave informa- tion to the police, with the re-sult that they found defendant had been persistently pilfering from the railway company's premises. A num- ber of houses had been visited, and some articles had been Tovered.—Detective-Sergeant Red- dington said that when he arrested prisoner lie at first denied any knowledge of the thieving. but had later made an admission.—The teraand was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (in the chair), and Messrs.</p>
        <p>C. Jackson -Holines and W. T. Williams.</p>
        <p>SMASHED THE WINDOWS.—James Astley, 14, Hume-street was summoned by his son, Ernest Astley, 97, Algernon-street, for doing damage to . a</p>
        <p>nuMher-of windows to the extent a 10s.—Pris- oner, who was very deaf, pleaded guilty.—Mr. Steel appeared for complainant, and said that on Monday night defendant wanted to gain ad- mission to the house, and knowing what would</p>
        <p>.happen if he did get in, his son declined. Pris- oner then banged on the door and broke a win- dow. He went away and later returned and broke another window. Mr. Steel said he had witnesses in court who would say that prisoner threatened to smash all the windows and furni- ture in the house. So had was his conduct that it was found necessary to telephone to the police station and when constables came on the scene prisoner went away. He had a very violent temper, and his son was afraid to go to work And leave his wife at home.—Prisoner under- took not to go near his son's house, and the case was adjourned or eight weeks. LINED THE STATION.7—John Heaton, of no settled abode, made his 91st appearance before the magistrates, this time on a charge of trespass- ing in the bebking' hall at,the Bank Quay Sta- tion, and refusing to quit when requested. The prisoner was warned several times during the day to go away, but each time he returned. Ile was fined 10s. and costs or 14 days. NOT ON THE COUNCIL.—William Smith, pedlar, 3, Prince-street, was charged with being drunk and further with committing a breach 'of the peace by fighting on August 12th, 1912.—A police officer, who was on duty at Bridge Foot the pre- vious night was advising prisoner, who was drunk, to go home, when he was ,told to look after the,bridge.—The Chief Constable : Did he think you were on the Council? (Laughter.)— I don't know, sir.—Evidence was called to prove the fighting incident in 1912.—Prisoner was fined 40s. and costs or one mouth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. J. Rymer Young (in the chair), W. Roberts, W. T. Williams, A. J. Jolley and</p>
        <p>Dr. Naden.</p>
        <p>A VIOLENT PRISONER.—James McMullan, loit- erer, residing at a lodging-house at 10, Pig-hill, was charged with trespassing on the Central Station, refusing to quit, and with assaulting Constable Clarke, of the railway police?'-The prisoner was loitering about the station and was told to clear away. He refused, and on being apprehended became violent and kicked ' the constable several times on the legs, and struck him on the chest —He was committed to nprison for three months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>FRIDAY.</head>
        <p>FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor and other magistrates.</p>
        <p>ATTACHED HEE HUSBAND WITH KNIFE.—Alice Holloway, of 44), Kerfoot-street, was charged on remand with wounding her husband, Samuel Holloway, by cutting his throat.—The Chief Constable said that on Thursday, February sth, Mr. Holloway, who is an off-licence holder, was reading a newspaper at home, when his wife came in front of him' with the knife? produced. He told her to be quiet, and immediately, after-</p>
        <p>wards she drew the knife across his throat, aud two stitches had to be inserted. When the husband was called at the last hearing he tried to make out that it was an accident, but that did not coincide with the statement he made to the police.-3[r. S. Steel, defending, suggested that the case should be adjourned for sis months. In the meantime the woman should go into a home to be found in Liverpool, but if she came out before the six months had expired the case should be re-opened.—The Bench agreed, to this course.</p>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Warrington Workingmen's Associa-</head>
