      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <p>HOURS OF CHILD LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Trade Council's Federation and the Working of Young Children.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. DUKES AND ASYLUM WORKERS.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Trades Council was repre- sented at the half-yearly meeting of the Lanca- shire aml Cheshire Federation of Trades and Labour Councils, held in Manchester on Satur- day.</p>
        <p>I. W. Mellor,seeretary of the Manchester and Salford Trades Council, moved: "That this Meeting, representing 191,295 workers in Lanca- shire and Cheshire, emphatically condemns the unconstitutional and tyrannous action of the South African Government ill deporting the Labour leaders for exercising their undoubted rights as citizens. We further call upon the British Government to repudiate those proceed- ings and take such steps as will in future safe- guard the rights of all citizens under the pro- tection of the British flag. This meeting fur- ther decides, in conjunction with the Munches- ter.and Salford Trades and Labour Council, to urganise a public demonstration ill Manchester at an early date to protest against the aforesaid action of the South African Government, and that arrangements be made to invite to the 41ee,ling two of the deported Labour leaders." The resolution was adopted unanimously.</p>
        <p>POSITION OF ASYLUM WORKERS. There was a pointed discussion on the treat- :taent of employes in asylums, and it was alleged that in many institutions the higher officials had evinced antagonism to trade unionism. t,!on Whittingham and Prestwich were cited as places 11 which intimidatiott had occurred. A resolu- was passed protesting against the action of "le Visiting Committee of the County Asylum aj Whiffingham, Preston, and culling upon jets to recognise their employes' right of cora- "lllation and to protect them. ' Mr. C. Dukes '(Warrington) said that in view °f the large number of workers employed in ,aBSlatus they ought to take steps to obtain our representation on , the asylums boards,</p>
        <p>HOURS OF CHILD LABOUR. Mr. O'Cieary (Earlestown) proposed : " That inasmuch as it is cruel as well as physically harmful to children who have just left school being compelled to start work in the early hours of the morning, it is hereby resolved that this Federation demands an amendment of the Ric, t°rY Acts, so that it will be made illegal to per- mit a child under 16 years of age to work for hire earlier than 9 am., or more than 40 hours per week." The Earlestown Trades Council, he said, in Putting forward that resolution recognised that it was one of rather drastic action but some- thing would have to be done before long to re- strict the extent to which children were com- pelled to work. There was nothing that touched one more than to see little starving creatures ' going shivering down to their work at five o'clock in the morning. To-day a child was at school and got out of bed at a reasonable hour; to-morrow he must be up with the buzzer at five.</p>
        <p>lie recognised that this proposal would create some difficulty from the masters' point of view, but he would remind them that that occurred wz.hen they stopped them from working over- tune If this went befOre the Parliamentary ‘-'°rurnittee and they began to legislate about it 2,10 doubt there would be a great outcry, hut they °Mild not get anything without trying, and they Irst remember that it was for the good of their ildren that they were working. TIRED-OUT CHILDREN. said ;;Wilkinson (Earlestown), in secomling to „',.1,1eY experienced difficulty in getting boys "iitend. evening classes in continuation farel-n,"t• The reason was that the boys got home Work °— work tired' and did not want to start - al] •Tinii—for euueatio was work. Practic- this proposal was that boys should not ;'°rk before breakfast. That would enable them ",o be fit to go to classes at night and so give eta an equal chance with the boys of richer Ihirents to qualify for the best positions. Mr. J. llea-n (New Mills), while in sympathy With the resolution, said it would imu them up gaius difficultieS. He had too often seen the Pitiful Vectacle of little lads and lasses scurry- .„llg to the early mornings to work in the mill. 'tram an education point of view it Was radically Wrong. They had excellent evening classes, 'which they were paying money for, but they Were unable to get the children to attend them. But when they came to practical facts they . Must recognise that they could not have one Dart of a works starting at one time and an, •other.at another:. In .the bleaching and calico ,Printing trades it would be almost inipossiNe to work on those lines. This proposition was • _Merely sidetracking from a bigger question, and he therefore moved as an amendment "that the raecutive Committee get into active co-opera- tion with other committees and Labour organi- sations with a view to pressing forward a uni- • versal and legal eight-hour day or 48-hour week for all workers."</p>
        <p>After discussion the amendment was defeated and the resolution adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>NEW SWEET DISH.</head>
        <p>A NEW SWEET DISH.</p>
        <p>Most people are tired of the ordinary hum- drum sweets. Change of flavour is a very im- portant factor at tante, especially iii regard to tne sweet course. That which is eaten merely f's a make-weight cannot revive and strengthen body.the The best and the cheapest way of ensuring a change of flavour at the sweet course i 8 by the judicious use of Currants. This won- derful little fruit has a subtlety of flavour that enables it to be used in a countless number of ' ZilYe, always producing a different effect. This f the Cuint is well worthy of attribute of ir consideration, especially as it is so highly biltritious, being full of grape sugar, Natures °st Powerful food. The recipe for a new sweet iiiylven below. It has been prepared by a high , as"lerity on the culinary art, and will be found l'elatable as it is nutritious and digestible. COLESHILL PUDDING. ~ letu4e4l,l ‘.tirrants, 1 pint stewed apples, I pint to "ern ~ and juice of half a , sugar, f,reEnj: d eggs. Method: Put the stewed apples, Into limbs, currants, lemon rind, and juice egg, basin—sweeten to taste, add yolks of n,ad bake in a greased pie-dish until firm— ss minutes. Whip whites of eggs stiffly, tablespoonful castor sugar, pile on pml- sli.V,return to oven for a few minutes to 'Ay brown the white of egg.</p>
        <p>...</p>
        <p>Milit Wll —arY non-commissioned officers and IT 00n21, can be spared from their duty without 1 enience to the service are to be given an titirt 4ni ty of employment in the post Offices 1 . ghout the kingdom after passing an exam- • 'nation ' flip. as telegraphists. While on that dutY .v•eek. --Ir Pay will be made up to 265. for a 48-hour</p>
      </div>
