      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <p>POULTRY PEN.</p>
        <p>Hints and Interesting Facts and Chat for Amateurs. 4014•144014•1014,1014÷44444•1444,1014÷44•14</p>
        <p>The past year has marked some developments in the world of poultry. In 1913 there was a considerable .rise in the volume of eggs and poultry received from overseas, as compared with 1912, yet prices have been greater. So far as native supplies are concerned there has been a rise all round, showing that consumption is advancing more rapidly than production, whether native or foreign. What is true in Britain is equally the case in other lands. During the late autumn in many rural dis- tricts, even so remote as Wales, there was a famine in eggs. Ilalf-a-crown a dozen was fre- quently paid for " new-laids" in country mar- kets, and threepence each for some weeks was the rate in great centres of population. On the wholesale markets good fowls have this year advanced in values by 15 to 20 per cent. The Christmas trade was one of the best known. It is not a question of increased cost for food stuffs, but of consumption. One pregnant fact is that the United States have' been importing substantial quantities of eggs from Europe, mainly Russia,: and have also purchased high- class 'table poultry, which may mean much in the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>NUTRITIVE EGGS.</head>
        <p>NUTRITIVE EGGS.</p>
        <p>gs are generally considered the most nutri. tiv'eg of concentrated foods. It is of great im- )ortance that good food and pure, clean water ishall be provided for the fowls' daily susten- allCC. Unfortunately, neglect is often displayed y o ruti keepers, anjrthing being considered g enough for the fowls' diet. When eggs are properly considered as an article of diet, it will be clearly seen that tainted, impure food by l and stagnant water cannot produce a nutritious new-laid egg.</p>
        <p>Complaints are often made of the flavour of some new-laid eggs from fowls which have been well fed and cared for, and difficulty is experi- enced in ascertaining the cause, which is gener- ally due to disease of the ovary organs, often brought about by feeding with spices or too stimulating foods during the winter months, when eggs aro scarce and enforced egg-produc- tion desired. The flavour of the eggs is some- times distasteful, and unless some remedy is found they will be useless as human food. Glauber salts given daily.in. the drinking water Will generally effect a Cure, Whilst soft food, such as Spratt's poultry meal, will benefit the fowls.</p>
        <p>Plenty of green food, including clover and 'chaff, steamed, should be also given, and as much exercise as possible afforded the fowls. Maize should be given very sparingly, good, sound oats forming the principal grain. Pure water is, of course, of the greatest im- portance, for it enters largely into the composi- tion of blood, bone, and flesh, even more than other component! parts. Water, pure and un- contaminated, is the great essential to -life and growth, but its tendency to absorb noxious effluvia is the reason why a special warning should be given: Decayed vegetable matter or animal excrement soon renders it most impure, unless it can receive a filtering process in the earth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>FATTENING CHICKENS.</head>
        <p>FATTENING CHICKENS.</p>
        <p>• • • • - -- During the next ten months the trade in fattened chickens is at its height, and probably more birds will be offered for sale than during the remaining period of the year. Some of them will be excellent, and will command tip- top prices; others—l am afraid the majority— will be poor; and only classed as second-rate. There is, of course, a considerable difference in the birds themselves, some lending themselves ; readily to fattening, and possessing flesh of a good quality and flavour; while others, no mat- ter how much time and money is spent on them, will never make really good birds. ,Although something, therefore, depends upon the indivi- duality of the birds, perhaps more depends upon the food or foods supplied. Fattening birds shouldi always be given meal rather than hard tglrigaeisn' ted,beaenadusteliitsbe i)r otif %ire se rfl e silst more or r e ee r apai (IS illyy Ground oats is the most suitable meal for the purpose, as it produces flesh of first-class qt euranliiet(yi , se Sussex colour, g r oa (nu idd flavour.tittSussex e jtowiisngutsouatlhe usually fact that until thus tetni ne recently oats,g was millers a t all extensively was a tt lll6coly Ilill carriedco uaty incdh a made a speciality of ground •oats for foeeadn purposes. Owing, however; .to the establislf- ment of fattening stations all over, the country' it is now possible to procure excellent samples from almost all millers. The oats, husks and all, require to be ground exceedingly fine—almost as fine as flour. Only when a good market is being supplied does it Pay to employ ground oats, as the price is by no means low. A cheaper mixture consists of half ground oats and half barley meal; a cheaper mixture still—but one that is Much inferior—is one-third ground oats, one-third barley meal, and one-third maize meal. Maize in any form should be avoided as a food for poultry, as it has the effect of producing fat rather than flesh; fat, moreover, that is greasy and of a yellowish tinge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>EXAMINER. SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 14, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 14, 1914.</p>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>WART DISEASE OF POTATOES.</p>
        <p>Complaints are heard on both sides of the Atlantic concerning the action of the American authorities, says_" The .Times," in prohibiting the importation of potatoes from this country on account of outbreaks of wart disease. The scourge when prevalent • is serious enough to justify firm action, for it spreads easily and is exceedingly destructive. British growers con- tend. however, that the closing of the American ports is not warranted by the few isolated out- breaks in England and Scotland. If no provi- sions existed for the control and suppression of the disease there might be ground for drastic measures abroad, but the infection is under such strict supervision that total prohibition is regarded as a needless obstacle to business. The action of the American Department of Agriculture is even more severely criticised in the United States than here. According to the "Globe": (New York, there is a scarcity of pota- toes in that country, and with prices rising ' steadily a demand is made for the removal of the restrictions upon imports. It is stated that potatoes are already selling at from 85 to 90 cents, per bushel (.1:7 to .t 7 10s. a ton), and that there is every prospect of the price reaching a dollar and a half or even two dollars--..e12 10s. to .46 13s. 4d. a ton. In confirmation of a charge of inconsistency made against the Ameri- can Department our contemporary alleges the prevalence of coddling moth, " bitter rot," San Jose Scale, and other diseases in apple orchards in many of the fruit-growing States, and adds that if the British and other European author- ities were to apply the same conditions to American fruit that the United States Depart- ment applies to British .potatoes the conse- quences to California, Oregon, Virginia, West Virginia, Kansas, Illinois, Missouri, and other States would be disastrous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>CROPS FOR SEWAGE.</head>
        <p>CROPS FOR SEWAGE.</p>
        <p>The kind of crop which is best suited to sewage cultivation is a question that has been much discussed, and is an interesting one. M,lieh has been said in favour of rye grass, which was often proclaimed as about the only crop which gave a profitable return. But the conclusions come to by the committee of the British Asso- ciation, who experimented very completely in this matter in 1867-72, were fortunately much wider in their scope. They reported that "it is certain that all kinds of crops may be grown with sewage, so that the farmer may grow such as he can best sell." Cattle foods, they de- clared, must form the bulk of the produce grown, but "occasional crops of kitchen vege- tables and corn" Were useful. Vegetables and root crops for cattle are certainly among the best things to grow, and a good deal of money can be turned over by their sale. Mangolds and carrots seem to be specially favoured by sewage, as they develop very quickly, and the carrots reach, a huge size, but their quality is good. Mangolds may not midi a standard ideal to dairy farmers, but 80 to 100 tons of very respectable roots per acre can be grown.— "Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
        <p>TO ENLARGE THE ROYAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The Royal Agricultural Society have, at the suggestion of Sir AilwynHowes, appointed a committee to inquire and report as to what could be done to increase the membeiship of the society. The amount received in subscriptions from governors and members in 1914—X8,372— was the largest in the history f the society, but a total of less than 11,000 implies wide scope for well-directed efforts in swelling the lists. The object of the proposed movements is to make the society more generally effective in good work for all branches of farming. The range of its activities has been widened steadily in recent years, and it is felt that there is room for further development in this direction, but as a preliminary the more stable sources of revenue must be increased.. The income from the shows is too variabl,e to justify the Council in adding to their fixed responsibilities on the strength of success in this quarter, but if the membership were substantially increased the resulting funds could be put to good purpose in fostering pursuits that in the past have received little or no attention. • A central organisation has great power for stimulating wholesome competition and aiding developments provided it is strong financially, and the Royal Agricultural Society is going to ,make a fresh effort to procure the support that will enable it to extend its sphere of usefulness into new and important avenues.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>During the winter months horse radish, seraPed, is much appreciated when served with hot or cold beef. Few amateurs, however, grow it, although it is easily cultivated. It should not be grown in an out-of-the-way place, but in ordinary, deep, rich soil, in the open or shade. The present is a good time to plant, placing the crowns at least four or five inches deep.</p>
        <p>BROAD BEANS. , The ground for broad beans should be trenched, spreading a layer of manure between the top and bottom spits, and mix a little grit with the top soil. This work may be done at once, allowing the ground to settle a little, and after forking it over again, draw the drills and sow the seeds in February for an early crop. The drills should be two inches deep, place in the seeds, cover with a little fine cinder ash, and rake the bed even. In small gardens broad beans are sometimes sown along with early potatoes, dropping a bean between each potato set. Excellent results are often obtained from this method.</p>
        <p>SHALLOTS. When well grown there is no vegetable crop that gives so good a return as shallots. In some gardens onions do not grow satisfactorily, and shallots should be adopted as a substitute. The beet growers recommend the end of Janu- ary as a good time to plant the bulbs, but if the soil is in a fit state for planting any time dur- ing February it will be early enough in the Midlands. The ground should have been well mannred with rotted stuff. The position should be warm and sunny'. Make the soil firm and press in the bulbs into half their depth, about nine inches apart in rows one foot asunder. When the bulblets are beginning to expand, cut into the soil around the bulbs with a pointed stick, to allow of the further expansion of the offsets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>GARDEN PEAS.</head>
        <p>THE GARDEN PEAS.</p>
        <p>A farmer once declared that the cauliflower was the best flower in his garden, and he was probably right from a financial point of view. Most amateurs are convinced that the green pea is the best vegetable, in spite of cultural failures. To have a healthy row of peas, with big pods full of peas, the soil must be ,deeply dug and lib rich in manurial matter. The strong fleshy roots go deep down into the soil in search of food, and it must be evident that if there is no food there for the support of the plants the crop is unproductive. The amount of food needed to carry a crop of peas well through the season is very great. Peas are an exhausting crop, and the best policy is to use so much manure that it cannot all he used up. The necessary amount of manure should be _ob- tained and dug in as soon as possible. There is another important Point to remem- ber, that the pea delights in a fairly dry and firm soil. Though they cannot have too much water when in full growth, it ceases to thrive directly its roots 'touch stagnant water. This fact emphasises the importance of deep digging, and well breaking up the subsoils, •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>CONSCRIPTION OR NOT?</head>
        <p>CONSCRIPTION OR NOT?</p>
        <p>Captain Crosfield Debates the Question With the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Junior Liberals,</head>
        <p>Junior Liberals,</p>
        <p>MR. TOHLMIN TAKES THE OTHER SIDE.</p>
        <p>"I do not come here to advocate war, but to Prevent it• by what I consider an admirable way."</p>
        <p>Such were the words of Capt. Crosfield, who took the affirmative side in a debate on "National Service" in the Iforsemarket-street Chambers of the Junior Liberal Association on Tuesday evening. Mr. George Toulmin took the negative view, and argued that if an invading nation conquered our Navy they could starve us out and demand their own terms, and so any citizens' army would be useless. -Mr. E. Lloyd Jones presided over a large attendance. Fair arguments were put forward by both sides, and points were discussed in a very intel- ligent manner. The resolution proposed by Captain Crosfield was: "The time has now come to make it obligatory on the youth of the nation to join the Territorial Force." Mr. E. LLOYD JONES said they were met-to hear two sides of one question. Every true thinker ought to know both sides of every question,. ; otherwise it was im Possible to form a correct I opinion on the question at issue. The. question I was one which every true Englishman, whatever his politics, should clearly understand, and have a distinct and definite opinion upon. CAPT. CROSFIELD'S POINTS. To Judge Things On Their Merits." Capt. CROSFIELD said he could assure them it gave him pleasure to address the members of the Junior Liberal Association, because he took it that the Junior Liberals approached things, perhaps, with a freer outlook than the older members of the Liberal party. He believed it was one of the principles of their association to judge things on their merits, and not altogether on party lines. Feeling that, it was a pleasure to put before them what an increasing number of the people in this country agreed upon—the placing of the Territorial Force on a demo- craticand national basis, arranged so that every young man should do a certain term in the Territorial Force. Many of them no doubt reg r force, should be constantly cropping Y much that the word "force," Perhaps some of those present had followed Mr. Norman Angell, who was going up and down the country parading the absurdity of fighiing between nations. He was doing that by his brilliant writings and speeches. in which he physicalld pointed out /that it did not pay a nation to go to war, even if it won. Because of that, people were coming to think what was the good of hav- ing an army and navy. The fallacy which under- lied a great deal of Mr. Norman Angell's ideas was that nations, just like individuals, did not seek quarrels with one another simply for the sake of obtaining gain, but for the sake of beliefs and principles.</p>
        <p>ence.between the N.S.L. proposals and the Ger- man methods. But there was an alternative, fundamently different from the French, German and Russian schemes—the Swiss scheme. Every- body would have to go through the ranks, and promotion would only be by merit. The ser- vices of the men in the citizen army would only be for home defence.</p>
        <p>Not To Interfere In Strikes. r They would not be used in any way to inter- fere with the just rights of labour. How were they to get over the employers' difficulty, and make them deal fairly with their workpeople while away at their annual training. All the employers would be in the same boat, and therefore would be forced to agree with the pro- posals. All this, however, could not be done without that dreadful word " compulsion." The experience he had in South Africa showed him the need of having trained men. He contended that theirs were not illiberal, and quoted from John Stuart Mill, who said that "many thoughtful people were coming round to the Swiss system. It will be an uphill fight to get a really national defensive force, but it may t stoohisn.?,ouvntnrtyil, not only etmoderlaicalaivtelei(filteihabenudtp etdooepiali etalvtoeft they had the country to their pro- posals, the Territorial Force should receive their best support. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>" Armed Force Necessary."</head>
        <p>" Armed Force Necessary."</p>
        <p>He had prefaced his remarks by those refer- ences to Mr. Norman Angell and MS talks about force because he wanted to impress upon them that in the present state of civilisation a cer- tain amount of armed force was necessary, and it really come down to a question of degree what amount should be sufficient. Of course, some people were fond of saying if they had a wise diplomacy, a small army and navy would be sufficient. Diplomacy alone was no substi- tute for force. It was like the paper money, if you had no solid gold behind it at the bank it would be of no value. The value of it was because there was something at the back of it at the. bank.</p>
        <p>If diplomacy went on, and the armed forces, which were the solid cash, got too small, other nations would say there, was nothing behind it, and then diplomacy would fall to the ground. The specific point was how did this apply to our Territorfal Force. The Territorial Force was introduced in 1996 :by: Lord Haldane, the eminent Liberal stateknau, „What was his idea in reorganising and strengthening the then Volunteers, as they existed? What he said was that it was quite true we were an island, there- fore their first line of defence must be the navy. But the navy could not de everything.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Could Not Defend.</head>
        <p>Could Not Defend.</p>
        <p>It was a . , we have a Regular Army. Now, in .normal circumstances it wits per- big force and probably better trained than the forces on the Continent, but could they always rely upon the Regular Army being at home? Per- fectly true Trouble might arise , elsewhere, and then if they were called away, we would be left with nothing . but the Volunteers, who, from the very nature of the case, were very slightly trained. Lord Hal- dane said he wanted to alter that on a voluntary basis. He wanted a great citizen force, and he actually stated in one of his speeches at least that tiis citizen army should be 900,000 strong. Captain Crosfield imagined that Lord Hal- dane had his advisers, who told him this army ought to have six months' training. Then naturally every sane person would say they ought to have that training before war broke Out,. but he found it was going to be a difficult task to get employers of labour to fall in with the idea, so he was forced, he could not help himself, to resort to the makeshift of having the training after war broke out. Lord Haldane realised the fact that to protect these shores the navy and home army must work hand in hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>TWO Services.</head>
        <p>The TWO Services.</p>
        <p>Prince Louis of Battenhurg said he trusted Prince they did that they wood always remember that no anw and a certain number of with the other. He had told them that Lord Haldane first of all stated speech on no it Aprildispense 2l last year but he quickly gave that up and Came down to 312,000 no more than 260,000. that was °it:is:le:tic: lieaetrivszme i minimum nairnmi uynislisotirdetillghtehs:ool,ololoe sttrroa7i;ng was What had Lord Haldane The strength of the Territorial he obligations cent. of the Territorials ttrFloeroiiirllliellesye lte4govo-tedltri iyyys s‘yi,eiatasrci. to say, very far short of the 312,000 be set out to get. In spite of the great e 312.000 made to in- crease the numbers, the force had diminished. ibmutpoisiesil7ll-)eaass Islifena voluntary In addition to that; he regretted to say, that which Lord glapilediiklieolor4ini:iii.t)ell.le did not say it was any 'what Lord Haldane laid down. flsiaolmillie(eltiolilitieloi t failedir se. tl on 1913 35 ,Ovg fle :I. oy mes acoiiiiieldd ftolri :seep eiheloimw(el iiiidt. wfs3aittsis: et mio)rftaoteltiaieet tanalleynn and 100,000 °al3 trained for meh hope to tilloti?l,9glino2 151t8sok,0e0:0armym jecii.oi i Bel. eleven up against instioawtr</p>
        <p>Only War put the Ter- odli'miTolrylacinelue3, BCway lkilltevoaould take that six months' (131lietitimtooc7iaisitsic basis, "The the duty of every Tie did not say that they needed nationalise turn in that r3,if,tiootT,lot.ichiln•eieg.aaln training and put it before, not after, to go so far as six months, but only a portion of it. It would be a great thing for those who lived in the slums, for it would take them into did not have an adequate war broke out. the country for at least one period in their lives. They were up against that terrible word " corn- grew bigger and bigger. Every youth pose for one moment to get ifPiii,seei°itlijol.”thislcfoutlilletyry, the navy estimates would o this country. He believed in Germany was trained to defend his country, thought there was no differ- German idmidetliiootd sPiriolt one of the reasons why- many of them fell foul was because they</p>
