      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>MR. TOULMIN REPLIES. " Arguing Against the Territorials."</head>
        <p>MR. TOULMIN REPLIES. " Arguing Against the Territorials."</p>
        <p>Mr. TOULMIN said Mr. Crosfield had been argu- ing against the Territorial Forces, and then lie told them it was obligatory on the youth of the country to join the Territorial Force, which was not sufficient to train the youth to the use of , armaments. He would also like to know where they were going to get the money from to pay for the proposed citizen army. It would cost— so be had heard it said by National, Service League speakers-8,000,000 or X 10,000,000 per year. At present 100 millions a year were spent on armaments, and that was a tremendous amount of money to spend gn the armaments of one country, and he would very much like to know from where The National Service Leaguers proposed to get the money. Had it to come from the people who owned the territory, which was to he protected? He wondered if Mr. Crosfield suggested that the burden be placed back on the landlords of the country. He thought if that were so there would he a great 'deal less said about armaments in this country. (Hear, hear.) Armaments' Enormous Cost, Mr. Crosfield had mentioned "slums." In his (the speaker's) opinion, the slums of the country would disappear if we did not spend such enormous sums of money on armaments. They would have the money to clear the "plague spots" out. (Hear, hear.) They got over that. • difficulty quite easily. If they were really de- sirons—as.the National Service League were de- sirous—of benefiting the country from the point . of view of health and physique, they could create gYmnasiums and make it compulsory for school children to learn to swim and other , things. If they kept on spending so many mil- ' oi orimi s s per would t e 110 rieva dpnuotu ectks, , social re- , and any great measures that were put forward would be ham- pered and hindered. (Hear, bear.) It would be better if the National Service Leaguers were patriots like Mr. Will Crooks, who spelled patriotism " home." (Hear, bear.) With the talk of a German invasion he had often wondered what, would he the reason for an invasion. Norman Angell had tried to con- vince the people of the country and Europe that there was no money in warfare—that no coun- try could gain anything by fighting. If a coun- try invaded us they would have to destroy: our navy, and if they did that they could easily starve us out, and if they were out.. after money they would he able to make their own terms. If the citizens' army were brought to fruition, it would be of little use.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Conscription Not Necessary. ... •</head>
        <p>Conscription Not Necessary. ... •</p>
        <p>. - If Germany were the great military power we believed them to be, they would not be so foolish as to tryt to land men hi this country if they could starve us out. (Hear, hear.) He thought that was the reason why the present Government discountenanced the idea alto- gether. They did not think it necessary to have a conscriptionist army like that of Germany. National Service Leaguers would never tackle the Norman Angell theory at all. (Hear, hear.) Some people said the Germans were After our trade. If a German general were to land in this country, march on London and ransack the Bank of England, would it be possible for that man to enrich himself or his army? He might find a few thousands, but he would find his own banks had gone smash. (Hear, hear.) Instead of enriching himself he would have ruined him- self. A conquering nation could not enrich themselves one penny piece. It would be far better if the National Service League adapted themselves to Norman Angellism, instead of compulsory service. Warfare a Crime. They must recognise that conditions changed and all the people were beginning to realise that warfare was a crime. Why did they not take their principles in their hands and say, We will try to convince Europe that warfare is a crime and a disgrace to civilisation"? Now- adays it seemed that if we wept to teach people religion we took the sword. If we set an ex- ample to the world we should make the civilisa- [ion of the whole world better. (Hear, hear.) It was impossible for Germany or any other country to gat our trade or enrich themselves one iota. The other suggestion was that they would ask for one 4 the colonies, and that also was impossible, because they really did not be- long to us, but were independent nations in alliance with another country. (Hear, hear.) If the German flag were hoisted in London, it would not be hoisted in Montreal.</p>
        <p>Industrial Field's Battles, •To-day's fighting was not on the battlefield, but in the industrial field, and if we could not compete in the markets of the world we were no good and the possession of Dreadnoughts or • a conscript army would do no good. lie thought the National Service League would he better employed trying to convince this country and the countries of Europe that war was a crime: We were all brothers the world over, and to call one, another on the battlefield was just as much a crime as anything. War was entirely out of touch with modern conditions, and ought to be swept away. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>DISCUSSION.</head>
        <p>THE DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>An interesting discussion was by Mr. RICHARD BRIMELOW, Who thought it was as much our duty that we should go in for a citizen army as it was equally our 'duty as trade union- ists to organise and strengthen our forces. He believed that by being strong they would get 4 that peace which they were all desirous of ob- taining. (Applause.) After further discussion, in which several members took part, the principal speakers made their replies.</p>
        <p>CAPT. CROSFIELD REPLIES. • Mr. Crosfield said be profoundly disagreed with Mr. Toulmin that slums would disappear by mere building. Mr. T. Horsfall had said</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>PEOPLE'S FOOD.</head>
        <p>THE PEOPLE'S FOOD.</p>
        <p>Results of the Cheshire County Analysts' Searching Investigations.</p>
        <p>MILK BECOMING MORE PURE.</p>
        <p>How the people's food in Cheshire is watched over is shown by the quarterly report of the Public Analyst submitted to the Cheshire County Council meeting on Thursday. In this the Analyst says:--</p>
        <p>During the quarter ending December 31st, 1913, I have analysed 388 samples, consisting of 324 foods and drugs, 48 waters, 12 effluents, and four feeding-stuffs. The foods consisted of 128 milks, 53 butters, 19 whiskies, ,15 cheeses, 14 cocoas, 13 preserved creams, 11 coffees, 9 rums, five each of jam, lard and pepper, four each of cream and rice, three each of syrup, camphor- ated oil, ground ginger, Gregory powder and sugar, two each of bread, sweet wirits of nitre, gin, liquorice powder, flour, arrowroot and yeast, and one each of margarine, baking pow- der, skimmed milk, tomato sauce, ground almonds, slippery elm powder, linseed meal, mustard, powdered gentian root and compound powder of cinnamon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Milk and Cream.</head>
        <p>Milk and Cream.</p>
        <p>I Twenty-three samples, equivalent to 7.1 per I cent, of the total number examined, were re- ported against as adulterated or not up to the requisite 'standard of purity. These included ten milks, seven of which afforded evidence of the presence of added water to the extent of 3,3, 4,4, 5, 7 and 7 per cent. respectively, two were deficient in fat to the extent of 13 and 16 per cent. respectively, whilst the remaining sample was both 3 per cent, deficient in fat and contained 4 per cent, of extraneous water; three butters were proved to contain upwards of 90 pen cent, of fat foiTign to. butter-fat; four creams- were preserved .with Boron compound equivalent to 0.20, 0.23. 0.24 •and 0.25 per cent. respectively of Boric acid; two rums and one whisky showed, .respectively, 6, 17 and 4 per cent. of • excess water; one Gregory powder had been compounded with hydrated carbonate of magnesia instead Of the .oxide of magnesia; one sweet spirits of nitre proved to he five per cent. under the minimum of ethyl nitrite, its medi- cinally-active and most characteristic constitu- ent, and one sample of mustary contained a small proportion not exceeding four per cent. of wheat flour. The two latter are not serious eases and are doubtless due to inadvertence rather than fraudulent design.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Better Milk.</head>
        <p>Better Milk.</p>
        <p>There is a marked increase in the percentage of samples of .very good to superior quality, when compared with last quarter, which is quite in accordance with the generally accepted view that the best milk is secreted during the early winter months. Without exception the milks were free from preservatives and in only a few isolated cases was artificial colouring matter present. - The fat of the three adulterated samples of butter in each case contained upwards of 90 per cent. of fat foreign to butter-fat—two being of the animal-fat type and the third a mixed beef- stearin and vegetable oleo (cocoanut). The water content of the 5.1 samples varied between the limits of 7.2 and 15.6 per cent., with an average of 11.1 per cent., a figure closely agree- ing with previous determinations. Seven samples contained small amounts (not exceed- ing 0.5 per cent, in any case) of Boron preser- vative.</p>
        <p>Whisky With Water. • One whisky only contained water in excess of the statutory limit, but one of thp two adulter- ated rums was more serious, as it was at an alcoholic strength of 42 degrees 17.1). (corrected), or equal to. 17 per cent, of extraneous water. Twelve out of 15 cheeses were of Cheshire . origin, .all being of sound quality, free from excessive water, and containing an average of° 32 per cent. of true butter-fat. The cocoa samples were of good quality, hav- ing regard to the present-day commercial samples; they were more or less de-fatted pow- ders, hut in no case was there evidence of the presence of excessive or•added husk. Four samples of cream were found to contain Boron preservative in amounts varying from 0.2 to 0.23 per cent., or from 14 to 17.5 grains per pound. The coffees were shown to be free from foreign vegetable tissue, and the cereal foods, including bread, ginger, rice, pepper, flour and arrowroot, possessed normal microscopical characters and were innocent of any objectionable(mineral ad- ditions and foreign starch—the latter remark applying also to the samples of yeast.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>CONSCRIPTION OR NOTP—continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WHAT A PICTURE TELLS.</head>
        <p>WHAT A PICTURE TELLS.</p>
        <p>Paintings Which Please As Well As Convey Moral Lessons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>INSTRUCTIVE LECTURE COURSE. 11</head>
        <p>INSTRUCTIVE LECTURE COURSE. 11</p>
        <p>Very few people can see the points in pic- tures, and by missing these fail to grasp the subtle charms of such works of art. It was with the object of giving an insight into the methods of reading a picture that the first of the new course of lectures was given, under the auspices of the Ogden Trust, in the Co-opera- tive Hall, Warrington, by the Museum Com- mittee. The lecturer was Mr. B. H. Mullen, art curator of the Salford Museum, and his sub- ject was "How to enjoy a good picture." The attendance was not so large as the merit of the lecture warranted.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ridgway was the chairman, and, in in- troducing the lecturer, stated that this was the fourth year of such lectures under the auspices of the Ogden Trustees. Each year had seen fresh progress made and fresh ground covered, and he thought satisfactory results had accrued. Each season saw an increase in the number of the audience, which showed that they still re- tained the old patrons and had aroused interest in many others. He hoped the time would come when that hall would not be able to accommo- date those anxious to participate in the lectures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>PICTURES WITHIN PICTURES.</head>
        <p>PICTURES WITHIN PICTURES.</p>
        <p>The lecturer came provided with an elpborate series of lantern sliaes, illustrative of famous pictures, and the lecture thus took the form of Pictures within pictures. The chief features of each illustration Were graphically described, and many points about pictures quite new to the audience were brought forth. painting. said Mr. Mullen, must have a moral, as well as a certain amount of artistic beauty, and if it lacked these two essential qual- ities, it must be put down as a failure. The artist, when he began a picture, must start from one foundation—a harmony between form and colour. • Without these requisites the pictorial art could never be properly developed.</p>
        <p>A Picture's Moral Porce. It was well for towns to have art collections in their midst. It was a moral force which had to be reckoned with, for it inculcated a love of the beautiful and artistic in the minds of the citizens. The study of such pictures produced not only a love of the beautiful, but drew forth all that was unselfish and sincere in life. Portraits were hot like the prints taken by the camera, for the real artist not only pre- sented the actual appearance of the sitter, as did the camera, but be infused soul and charac- ter into his work. He grasped the nobler fea- tures in his patron and made them speak through the picture.</p>
        <p>The illustrations to the lecture were in them- selves works of art, and represented paintings by some of the greatest masters of the brush. The lantern was manipulated by Mr. B. Jones, who got the best effects out of the slides.</p>
      </div>
