      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Serious Complaint Made at Runcorn Rural District Council.</head>
        <p>A Serious Complaint Made at Runcorn Rural District Council.</p>
        <p>"I am inclined to think that our system of disinfecting houses is not as thorough and cora- plete As it ought to be." So,•said Mr, Wood- ward, in discussing the report of the Medical Officer of Health to the Ruitcorn Rural District 'Council at the meeting at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday. • This remark was made in an attack upon the practice of people who, upon having their homes visited with scarlet fever, kept articles away from the influence of the disinfectant. Mr. Woodward said they had a case where a person was taken to their hospital, treatetA, discharged, went home, and a shoit time latex' two further cases broke out. It seemed that there was a very serious leakage somewhere in their admin- istration. People withheld some bedding and personal clothing which had been in contact with the- sick person, although he did not sug- gest that that was the cause of further out- breaks in the case under review, To his mind, that system was not satisfactory. They would never be able to keep off scarlet fever until they thoroughly disinfected all the articles. Chil- dren played with toys and book;, and if they I came in contact with one of the sick persons it was likely that that would be a means of spread- ing the disease. . The Chairman (Mr. Linaker, sen.): Does the , Medical Officer know of the methods? Dr. Adams said his instructions to the Sani- tary Inspector was to disinfect everything in the way of clothing and bedding and everything that had been near the patients. Of course, it was quite impossible to see every small thing that had been Put away. There was a lot of dotruth in what Mr. Woodward had said, and no uht that the dis was one cause of the Spreading of, ease. ?Ir. Britch: I quite agree with what Mr. 'l34)iciewmala.(illarS :aaisd. Iledical Otte fficer left in the hands of the arid the Sanitary Inspector.</p>
        <p>cv</p>
        <p>Finished counterfeit sixpences were seized by the Sheffield police on Saturday night, when they raided a house in the centre of the city, and arrested two nien and three women on a charge of coining.</p>
