      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>IT IS REALLY NECESSARY</head>
        <p>IT IS REALLY NECESSARY</p>
        <p>TO TAKE</p>
        <p>COUG</p>
        <p>1 ,</p>
        <p>ges</p>
        <p>41 • /</p>
        <p>Warrington Commercials Prove Themselves "Jolly Travellers" at the Annual Dinner.</p>
        <p>WITTY SPEECHES A FEATURE OF THE GATHERING</p>
        <p>There is a certain geniality of manner and good-natured air about commercial travellers, that it is almost unnecessary to state that the eighth annual dinner of the Warrington and District branch of the United Kingdom Com- mercial Travellers' Association, held in the Lion Hotel on Saturday evening, was in every direc- tion a huge success. The dinner was excellent , and .well served, and the post-prandial Speeches were characterised by eloquence, wit and humour. An artistic menu card and toast list, surmounted by the monogram of the associa- tion, had been drawn up, and everything was of the best. In the speeches, probably the only "chestnut" was the joke about the bridge, but nevertheless the "spicy bits" relative to, this structure were fully appreciated. As the members arrived they were received by the Mayors of Warrington and Widnes, ,Mr. W. P. Rylands (president of the branch), and Mr. P. O'Brien (chairman of the branch). Dur- ing dinner choice musical selections were dis- dcoeunrtsepdrebsyidMede,ssarns.d J.h e wandaWs Mayors of Warrington and Widnes (Councillor supported eTdheb Presi- dent r et Peacock and Councillor -Lewis), Mr. P. O'Vrien (chairman), Mr. W. P. Ashton (vice-chairman and hon. treasurer), Mr. T. Percival (hon. secre- tary), Mr. W. L. Walker (hon. assistant secre- }t,aurylt)o,n-tar.itTh., MS.r.SHteeenl r3(,liGonr.eesnoallici iatonrd), 31111.1.r.vvJ.. Mason. Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., sent a tele- gram of apology. Entertaining items were given at intervals by several favourite artistes, Mr. H. J. Iddon being the accompanist. Miss M. E. Brundrett was encored for her finished rendition of well-chosen songs; Mr. J. B. Poole sang a number of songs in his usual cultured manner, although his gest- est success was in ,the prologue from "Ii Pag- liacci," which also gave the accompanist the opportunity of displaying his talents as an efficient pianist. Mr. Whittle scored, as usual, with his humorous items, chiefly "The Irish Walk," "The Curate," and "The Telephone," the latter provoking uproarious laughter. Mr. Rainer and Mr. Williams, from the Hippo- drome, were alsd•well received, and Mr. T. Critchley sang several soneii, for which he was applauded. - The Royal toasts were honoured, on the pro- position of the CHAIRMAN, who said the King's visits amongst the working classes proved that he was, perhaps, more interested in them than any King who had reigned over the country for ninny years. • (Applause.)</p>
        <p>- -- BRIDGE AS A "LIVENER." Mr. HENRY GREENALL proposed' the toast " The Mayers and Corporations of Warrihgton an&amp; neighbouring boroughs." He said he did not know much about the Widnes Corporation, but he understood they had the cheapest gas in the country, and the manufacturers there had good power at a cheap rate. If at any time the Mayor of Widnes felt dull in presiding over the Council, he should build a bridge, and he was afraid the somewhat dull proceedings of the Corporation would be livelier. (Laughter.) In the matter of cheap gas Warrington was in- clined to envy Widnes. So far as Warrington was concerned, no one could have anything but praise for the way in which the Corporation managed its undertakings. In the water depart- ment £9,000 had been paid out of revenue for capital expenditure, and a reserve fund of £B,OOO had been built alp, and a balance of nearly £7,000 was in hand. In the Gas Department a sum of £126,500 had been applied out of the pro- fits in aid of the rates. Although only a com- paratively new undertaking, the electricity and tramways' department ,had made a profit of £1,700, in addition to building up satisfactory reserves. He coupled with the toast the Mayors of Warrington and Widnes, and hoped they would have health and happiness in the irn- Portant and dignified functions they had been called upon to perform.: (Applause.)</p>
        <p>"THE INFERNAL BRIDGE." Councillor PEACOCK, Mayor of Warrington, in reply, said he was going to avoid mentioning the bridge if he could that night. He had had his leg pulled about the bridge more than any other subject, and he had purposely avoided that question at various functions, especially when the chairman had asked him to give par- ticulars. As the matter was a standing joke, it had Occurred to him that if they wanted to. do anything to Warrington name " bridge," and they would be able to get Warringtonians' they must mention the backs up. (Laughter.) Ire only hoped Warrington had a transporter bridge and that it would stick in the middle with some of the councillors on it. (Laughter.) The Meetings of the Warrington Council were far from dull, and the other evening they had a very long meeting about the 'infernal" bridge. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>When Will It Be Completed? Commercial travellers, in his opinion, were the greatest complainers about the bridge, because it was an obstruction with which they had to meet—for he understood a great number of them lived on the other side. They must take some of the blame off the councillors. The delay had been far too long. They said the bridge would be completed this year, but he did not believe it. (Laughter.) They also said it was going to cost £14,000 or £l6oo&gt; but if it was built under £20,000 he would be surprised, for he was of the opinion that it was going to cost even more than that. In regard to the other Corporation undertak- ings, he might say that they were going to spend about £20,000 more on the water department, and he hoped great prosperity would accompany that. (Hear, hear.) The gas department had been profitable, although they could not com- pare with Whines as regarded the price.. They had a trick in Warrington of getting a big price and reducing the rates. The electricity and tramways department were also prospering.</p>
        <p>Sunday Trams Complaint. He believed, however, that the same People who complained about the bridge had been worrying 'about the trams on Sunday mornings. , Ho was in favour of reducing Sunday labour to the lowest extent, and when they. in their wits- dons, and not otherwise, decided that they would have a half-day serviv, they had a deputation, and at the end of each tale they said " We want to go to church, and want your men to carry us there." He (the Mayor) would say, "If you want to go to heaven why don't you walk to church on Sunday mornings, and give the tram- waymen a chance?" They had reduced their labour, and they ought to do something still further. If the commercial travellers had to be carried about the same as those men wanted, the businesses of Warrington and elsewhere would undoubtedly suffer. (Hear, hear.) lie ' regretted there were not more men of the com- mercial traveller type on the Council, but no doubt the profit-making department was so serious that they could not waste the time to get through Council matters. He thought War- rington was a prospering concern. Altogether the work of the Mayor was very pleasant, and he was glad to be received that night with that spirit of loyalty commercial travellers always possessed. He hoped when his term of office was completed that he would be able to say he ' had been supported by all the citizens of the town. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>strata than other people. It was up against, business men to disclaim and disprove that— (hear, hear)—and put it forward that more de- pended on business and business Men than the country realised. (Hear; hear.) He regarded business as a high caHinEC, and the more they could dignify, purify and elevate it, so much the better for business and the country. The TJ.K.C.T.A. was doing not a little to protect not only the interests of those engaged in the busi- ness, but to exalt ideals of business, and to make men fitter for their great tasks. (Hear, hear.) His Worship then spoke in tones of admira- tion of the undertakings of the Widnes. Cor- poration, and said Whines was a young muni- cipality, but energetic and ambitious, and it was going to go strong. He only wished there was a little more coming together of the two towns. (Hear, hear, and applause.)</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S 2,000 YEARS OF TRADE. The toast, V The Town and Trade of Warring- ton," was proposed by Mr. W. P. Asirrces, who said a commercial traveller, of necessity, had to make the best of all towns, and although he might live in a certain town, he was at the least expected to be strictly neutral. The in- dustries were both extensive and numerous. The records dated from the Roman period, and Mr. Thomas May recently-discovered traces at Stock- ton Heath of glass werkS:and iron works, when investigating the Roman Settlement there. It was certainly very peculiar that after a' loP!ie of nearly 2,000 years the trades should still exist in the locality, and that.one at least should form the principal of our-industries. The smoke of about the third largest ironworks blackened our sky. Wires of all sorts, including that bar- barous make which farmers used for prevent- ing boys pulling their turnips, could N seen leaving the Own in great quantities daily. We manufactured rubber—rather a smelly process-- corrugated iron, and had one of the largest seed fertilising places in England, several tanneries (also more or less smelly), a glass works—sole relic of bygone days—a solitary cotton mill and an extensive soap works. Hard times might come and hard times might go, but with our multiplicity, of trades, we always seemed to be up to the neck in work. (Hear, hear.) That we were flourishing was proved by the fact that our manufacturers and tradesmen, even in the more humble ranks, could afford to reside away from the borough ; it might be a good thing for the town if we were less Pros- perous, then some of bur best men might stay and take a hand in its government, and if wilf.tt he read was true, there would be a slump ‘?n that growing `.f industry" talk, which ended in talk—in the Council—and led to nothing. We were well served, for the transit of goods, and he believed we had the- first railway, the first canal, and the only ship canal at can. service.</p>
        <p>The Bridge in History. We were also likely to be mentioned in history as the builders of a new and magnificent bridge; perhaps, however, the least said about that the better. It seemed to have caused quite enough heart-burnings already. If it were possible, lie would suggest that whilst we were building this new bridge we also bnild a new river. Ile saw that Alderman Tinnion the other night referred to a fishing expedition. He would have con- siderable difficulty in catching anything, except disease, in our present stream. (Laughter.) There were visions of a circus at Market Gate; Perhaps that' was hardly a trade, but lie would at least include it in the town: We were really go-ahead. We had raised a million pennies for our Infirmary, and although our rates might be on the heavy side compared with what they were years ago, there was little to grumble at, and they would bear , considerable expansion before manufacturers began to pack up their traps and look out for places where the collec- tor was less exacting. He was told that our motto was "Deus dat incrementum," the trans- lation of which, according to Canon Morley Stevenson, was "God gives increase "—of rates. (Laughter.) There was one other trade of the town to Which he might refer, and that was the 'success- ful manufacture of' commercial travellers. There were a dozen members in their associa- tion who had all worked together as clerks in the Castle Rubber Co.'s offices. Other firms, of course, had also manufactured travellers, but not quite to' that extent. He therefore claimed in all sincerity that they manufactured com- mercial travellers, who were proving themselves men of geed calibre, sound judgment, success- ful and of good report. (APplause.)</p>
        <p>TOAST OF THE ROMANS. Mr. J. I'la:rox-SmiTa replied to the toast and said that no doubt in the Roman times, when they had travellers' dinners, there was a toast of "The Town and Trade of Warrington!' (Laughter.) It was the men who were in the town who had helped to build the town and give it prominence in the markets of the world. (Hear, hear.) They were proud of their excel- lent business men. (Hear, hear.) it should not be overlooked that the business men, both in a large or small way, took an intense interest in the charitable institutions. (Hear, hear.) The support of the Infirmary had hot dimin- ished, and the enthusiasm of the business men in that direction was a Hung of which to be proud. (Hear, hear.) There were some bur- dens which might, with advantage, be spread: over the Countty, and that would give the, traveller a better chance in quoting prices. Warrington had its advantages, such as the cheapest traffic arrangements, which helped us, as a manufacturing town, to hold our own with opponents. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>A POINTED CONUNDRUM. Mr. RYLANDS proposed *the principal toast of the evening, that of the " T..T.K.C.T.A." He caused much laughter with an original conun- drum. He asked: " Why is a commercial traveller like a inotOr car?" The answer was: "Because one's own, if a good one, is a source of continual satisfaction, whilst if it is anyone else's it is an unmitigated nuisance. nuisanc Com- mercial travellers were like the machinery— the product of competition—and he, personally, detested competition. ( Lauglter.) But com- petition had good results and just as modern machinery had developed into a wonderful instrument through competition he believed the necessity of commercial travellers, dating back to the days of Tubal Cain, had brought kbout competition which must have developed just those qualities which were the best qualities for any commercial traveller 'to pos- seas. (Hear, hear.) The association had been able to accomplish much for the social well-being of its members. (Hear, bean) He deeply regqtted that Mr. Granger, of the Exe- cutive Council, was unable to be with them, (Hear, hear.) • '</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION'S OBJECT. Mr. W. G. HAYES responded. The.association, he said, was one of 14,000 commercial travellers. It was formed, in the first place, to assist the schools and benevolent institutions, and since then its scope had been increased. A Parlia- mentary agent took the Parliamentary affairs in hand. There were 70 branches of the asso- ciation in all the leading commercial centres. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>After speaking of some of the useful things the association had accomplished, he said that one of the most important branches of the association Was the Benefit Society, established in 1902, which was a useful and splendid organ- isation. (Applause) THE TRAVELLERS' SCHOOLS. . Mr. 'l'. ThatelVAL, the Fiectetary, proposed the toast "The Commercial Travellers' Schools and Benevolent Institution:"There were, he said, 300 children in the schools, whieh were sup- ported mainly by the association and by .other associations. The greatest charity came •itt in the Benevolent institution, because it helped members themselves, and also their dependents. p .plause.) . _</p>
        <p>A. J. ALLEN; in reply. said the schools were founded in 1845, with 35 scholars, and at pre-sent there were 350 scholars in the schools. The cost per child per year was £4O. During</p>
        <p>(onSinuc44n next column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>TRA_NSPORTER'S "BIG HEART."</head>
        <p>TRA_NSPORTER'S "BIG HEART."</p>
        <p>Councillor LEWIS, the Mayor of Widnes, said it was rather cruel of the Mayor to suggest that the Transporter Bridge stuck in the middle sometimes, as the bridge was going to be a very perfect machine in the, future! ' If he personified the spirit of that toast, he would say it possessed a big heart, bat 'that was only what one could expect from such a com- pany. He had formed a very high opinion of commercial travellers as a class, and he looked upon them as an important element ill a busi- ness community. There were some who snubbed commercial travellers,. and when he met such ho felt 'like kicking them. There wita a certain class of people who Skitted at business- men, and held them in+ polite contempt, as ,if they were a race of inferiors. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>. Certain "Snobbishness."</p>
        <p>There watT-a certain snobbishness abroad that placed business and business men upou a lower</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 14, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 14, 1914.</p>
        <p>BIBLE FUND RAISING.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Guardian Objects to a Concert to Get the Necessary Money.</p>
        <p>RELIGIOUS INSTRUCTOR'S £5 SALARY,</p>
        <p>The manner in which it was proposed to ralso funds for supplying Bibles to the children under the yogis of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was subjected to a little criticism at the Board's meeting at the Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, when Mr. C. E. Linakei, jun., presided. The controversy arose out of the report of the Children's Home Committee, when mention was made of a concert it was proposed to hold in the Aston-lane. Schools, at which the children were to perform. A charge of threepence was to be made, and the proceeds were to be appro- priated for the purpose of providin4 Bibles. Mr. W. L. Morris, who objected to the prin- ciple. of the recommendation, said he thought they were stooping very low to adopt such mea- sures to Make provision for Bibles. If Bibles were required the Local Government Board would allow the Board to supply them. He was strongly opposed to a charge for admission. Mr. Linaker : Does Mr. Morris think it is too much? (Laughter.) Mr. Morris: It is the principle of the thing.. Mr. Mclntyre: It is perfectly right to make a charge. (Th Mr. Morris) : Would you sing feu nothing? (Laughter.) Mr. Woodward: The Aston-lane Schools do not, pay rates, ,and by an admission you make that place liable to be rated, and I, hardly think that we are the right persons to cause an in- fringement.</p>
        <p>. TGe Chairmakn: All the schools are in the same position._)</p>
        <p>Eventually the report of the cothmittee, which also contained an intimation that the amount of over £2, collected through the children's carolling, should be kept in hand to meet future outings, was carried.</p>
        <p>A SALARY OF LS A YEAR. Mr. Jos. Spruce moved that the salary of Mr. Tjtoniiis 'Arran, religious instructor at the Workhouse, be increased from £5 to £lO per annum. He did so upon the recommendation of the Homes Committee. Their religious in. structor came into the district in 1899, and for a time visited the Workhouse, and, by the sanc- tion of the Board, gave instruction to the. in- mates. In 1905 he was given an acknowledg- ment of £.5 a year, and later he began to pay two visits a week to the house. On_ Thursdays he visited the sick in the Infirmary, and on Monday evenings conducted Bible readings for the children. That continued from .1905 until the children went to the Home at Dutton Lodge last March. When the children • were removed from the House the committee thought it would be well for him to still go on with the Bible readings, and he was asked to do so. He agreed, but he thought that considering the additional journeys lie ought to have some fur- ther recognition. If the children had remained at the House that application WoUld ,not have been made. From April, 1905, to April, 1913, ha had paid 623 visit; to the House. Mr. Spruce asked that the Board should grant the increase and ask for the sanction of the Local Govern- ment Board. Mr. W. L. Morris, in seconding, said that the work of Mr. Tarran came under his notice as chairman of the House Committee. The increase was unanimously agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>– . DROWNED IN THE MERSEY.</head>
        <p>- . DROWNED IN THE MERSEY.</p>
        <p>MYSTERY AT MARTINSCROFT.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy coroner for South- West Lancashire, conducted an inquest at the Horse and Jockey Hotel, Mailinscroft, on Mon- day, on Alfred Marsh, of the Canal Lock House, Woolston. •</p>
        <p>Rebecca Marsh, the widow, said her husband was 58 years of age, and was employed by the Manchester Ship Canal Company as lockinaster. He had been in the employ of the company for 15 years but had IA worked for some time, having been a sufferer from nervous On 'Saturday morning deceased dressed him- self, went out, and shortly after she saw him in a boat on the river, which is adjacent to the canal. Sheocalled him to come back, but he said he was going for a sail, and that he was all right. When he reached the Cheshire side of the river he fell into the water. Deceased had attempted to drown himself on the 30th September last, and after being in hospital and before the magistrates, he was discharged and given into the care of witness. The Coroner: Whose boat was io—Our oWn. I think it was an accident.</p>
        <p>Did be jump into the water?-4 could not say: Did he fall or jump out?-.--I cannot say. What was he going to the Cheshire side for? —He had been in the habit of going to a farm there.</p>
        <p>Joseph Stafford,, of ganchester-road, Wool- ston, a weirkeeper, in the employ of the Canal Company, said that he, with assistance, re- covered the body from 12 feet of water. De- ceased was fully dressed, and when they found him his hat was on. Witness knew deceased had been used to going to Mr. Warburton's farm on the Cheshire side.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : How far was the boat away from his usual landing-place?—About sixty yards lower down. It was possible that deceased was attempting to jump from the boat on to the bank and fell into the water.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>BUSINESS CARES WERE PUT ASIDE-</head>
        <p>BUSINESS CARES WERE PUT ASIDE-</p>
        <p>continued.</p>
        <p>the time the schools bad existed 3,106 scholars through, had gone rough, and out of that a uin there well? none who had not been a cr)edit to themselves and the schools. (Applause. Dur- ing the past year the Warrington ddis district branch branch had raised £3l 10s., which had been sent to the schools. Before that biranch was formed there were two firms in Warrington who had 'bed Messrs. Peter Walker and Son since subscribed and Messrs. Crosfield's since 1871— sain Mr. Peter Sloan had also subscribed for a number of years. '(Applause.) The Benevo- lent Institution granted annuities to incapaci- tated members, and the Benefit Society,. of which Mr. Hayes was secretary, was a useful institution. (Applause.) A PROGRESSIVE BRANCH. Mr. T. S. Sum proposed the toast "The War- rington and. District Branch, U.K.C.T.A." H 6 said the Warrington branch, though only seven years old, was one of the most progressive in the whole country, which, in a measure, was due to the excellent officials they had. (Ap-• Meuse.) They were the first to found an un- employment fund in the association, and other branches had copied their ideas. They had also taken up another idea—the provident fund —and of the branch it would no doubt be said: "What Warrington does to-day the other branches do to-morrow." (Hear, hear.) There were over 80 members, and it was hoped that number would be increased. He coupled with the toast the name of Mr. O'Brien. (Applause.) Mr. O'BRIEN, in reply, said the men received their benefits not as charity, but as their right. The men formed an intensely public-spirited body. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>" PRESIDENT " AND "VISITORS." Mr. TAYLOR proposed "The President," andl said his services were highly appreciated. Mr. RYLANDS said that was his fourth year in that position, and he thought they ought to have a change. ("No.")</p>
        <p>"The Visitors" was proposed by My. P. 11. BROWN, who said they were pleased to have the Mayor with them. Ire was endearing himself to all their hearts and maki4 himself popular all round. (llem., hear.) Ms year of office would go down in history. (Hear, hear.) It would be nearly impossible to have commercial travellers on the Council owing to their never being sure of their time. He coupled with the toast the name of Mr. Mason. -</p>
        <p>_ Mr. MASON said he had at first made the monogram " U.K.T.C.A." as "United Kingdom Champion Tale Concocters' Association." (Laughter.) The toast •" The Artistes" was honoured, on the proposition of Mr. W. OWEN, Mr. W. W. WHITTLE replying.</p>
      </div>
