      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Did Re Evolve Gradually or Develop With a Leap?</head>
        <p>Did Re Evolve Gradually or Develop With a Leap?</p>
        <p>A DOCTOR'S NEW THEORIES.</p>
        <p>" The antiquity of Man in the light of recent discoveries" was the subject of an interesting lecture given by Dr. W. P. Gowland to members of the Warrington' Literary and Philosophical Society in the Cairo-street Hall on Monday evening, Mr. o..Lofthouse (president) presiding over a large attendance. Commenting on the increased attendance, the CHAIRMAN said lie felt there was some hope for Warrington, after all.. (Laughter.) A few of those who were present at the last meeting would remember that he made some 'sarcastic remarks about the supposed devotion of War- rington people to science, literature, &amp;c. It was gratifying to sibte the increase that night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>HINTS FROM WRITTEN RECORDS.</head>
        <p>HINTS FROM WRITTEN RECORDS.</p>
        <p>- - At the outset Dr. GOWLAND said, it was possible to investigate the details of man's existence prior to our own generation in a variety of ways, one method consisting in studying the written records of the people, usually referred .to as histbry. The earliest historical records were those of Babylon and Egypt, dating from about 4,000 yeays before Christ, and though it might be shown that history had its birth in Europe at a date very little later than that, the earliest references to our own Continent which had so far come to hand dated from 1500 B.C. It was not, however, until 400 B.C. that history threiv any light upon the British Isles, and from that time until the Roman in- vasion, 55 8.C., the British Isles again sank into historical oblivion.</p>
        <p>The study of man was by no means confined to the comparatively recent happenings re- corded in the pages of history, but the most fascinating part of such study was that it car- ried them back into the illimitable pre-historic past. During that period the physical char- acters of man himself, the point ,at which ha emerged from the animal status to be dignified by the title of Man, and the mode' in which he lived and died, were the problems confronting them. The actual data upon which they had to base their speculations concerning pre-historic mai) fell under two headings—" the remains of man himself and of the animals who lived during the same period in the form of fossilised portions of their osseous skeletons," and "the remains of man's handiwork," which were found in fossil berls.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Trained the Horse and Dog.</head>
        <p>Trained the Horse and Dog.</p>
        <p>After dealing with the implements used by man in the Stone Ake and the Age of Metals, and in earlier or Paleolithic -period of the Stone Age, man appeared to have manufactured pot- tery and possibly domesticated the horse and dog. But his most wonderful achievements were in art. Illusfrations were given of man's handiwork with flint implements, one of the best drawings being of reindeers. The lecturer then dealt at some length with the earlier stages of man's existence, and explained the various discoveries of remains which had been made from time to time. The most recent discovery was thatof the Piltdown remains, in Su with the exceptionrisex, in 1912. The jaw, of the teeth—which, though large, were human in character—possessed a feature which was characteristic of the apes, the fore part of the jaw having a long flange fqrming a short, -bony floor to the mouth. It was upon that character E that Dr. Smith Woodward founded his claim t of a new Genus Homo which lie the iia d discoverycalled oanthropus Dawsoni.</p>
        <p>Our Monkey Stock. Dealing with wh.at lie called our monkey stock, Dr. Gowland t said that the branching off of man's ancestors along at least two distinctive lines was further borne out by. a study of the evolution of the anthropoid apes; for though none of the living anthropoids were actually our ancestors, we Were both sprung from a common stock. Man seemed to have split off from the anthropoids at a much earlier period than was once thought, and it would appear that his special peculiarities appeared in the following Order: (1) upright posture; (2) size and stature; (3) adaptation of lower limbs for Walking and teeth; (4) last of all, great mass of brain and nude skin which might be correlated.</p>
        <p>• Diversity of Man. The extraordinary diversity of man as he•was seeh at the present day was another argument in favour of the great antiquity of man. Thus we had the European, Mongolian, Red Indian, African negro, and lastly the Aboriginal Aus- tralian, who probably represented most closely the original stock from which negro and Euro- Pean arose. To produce all those varieties from one common stock required a long period of time, and we were Confirmed in that view by the fact that in Egypt, where we had actually been able to trace this process, there was. very little difference between the modern Egyptian skull and that of the pretlynastic Egyptian, who lived at least 6,000 years ago. Further, the oldest human crania found in Ame.-i,:s were of Red Indian type, and prehistoric Britons who lived about 4,000 years ago, were very like ourselves. Two years ago Sir Charles Lyell stated that in his opinion the remains of man would be found in the Cromer beds (late Pliocene age), but though actual human remains had not been found (if we excluded Professor Ragazzo.ni's discoveries) there had been discovered eoliths, probably Worked by man, not only in the Cromer beds, but also in two older strata of the Pliocene—Reg Crag and Norwich Crag. Modern 'man was thus probably evolved it the latest about the commencement of the Pliocene, that was abOut one and a half million years ago.</p>
        <p>Evolution by Leaps. The lecturer then pointed out that the opponents of that view of whom l'rofessor Boyd Dawkins, of Manchester, was a distin- guished member, rejected eoliths as evidence of man's handiwork,• and denied the existence of man before Pleistocene times. It was 'also necessary to mention that .the most advanced of the experimental ,Piologists were inclined to the view that evolaion had not occurred so gradually as Darwin supposed, but rattier by a series of leaps and bounds, and if that be true their speculation as to what occurred in bygone days might have to undergo considerable revi- sion. (Applause.) - DEPUTY MAYOR'S VIEWS. An interesting discussiou was commenced by</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>SCOTTS Emulsion</head>
        <p>SCOTTS Emulsion</p>
        <p>When buying SCOTT'S it is always necessary to see the trade mark on package.—Only in this way can inferior imitations be avoided.</p>
        <p>Councillor Dr. Jour'', who proposed a hearty vote of thanks to the lecturer. He said the lecture had been an able, interesting and scholarly one. (Hear, hear.) The first thine, to strike him was, how, from a theological point of view, it upset such idea), of time, as on derived, from Biblical history.</p>
        <p>"WEAKER SEX" INTEREST. Mr. T. RYMER YOUNG, in seconding, referred to the sprinkling of the fair sex present. In these days of frivolity it was encouraging to know that their friends, whom they had so foolishly described as the " weaker sex," could give their attention and time to such an abstruse and dry subject as had been under consideration that night. They could hardly expect ladies to find any particular pleasure in the study of ancient man, but a large section of them were much mere interested . in the modern version of the creature. (Laughter.) The lecture completely altered one's ideas of time, and made those who took such pride in their antiquity of ancestry realise that it was nothing at all. Since science had very much disproved, or compelled us to doubt, the exist- ence of Adam, or, at any, rate, question his antiquity, be thought they should go the whole hog and say they came down from a handful of respectable molecules which existed millions of years before Spyman. (Laughter and applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>OLD PREJUDICES DYING OUT.</head>
        <p>OLD PREJUDICES DYING OUT.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said that while he did not think the lapse of time measured in years was. so great as was often asserted, he joined in the view that man must have existed as man in Tertiary times. The way in which Dr. Gov,' land had been received that night proved that a lot of the old prejudices were dying out. (Hear, hear.) He thought some of the pre' historic men with the powerful jaws and small brains would have heen qualified for positions. if not as Cabinet Ministers, at least as Town Councillors. (Laughter.) The Rev. L. S. T. DARWALL asked if the age of the_Piltdown skull given by one newspaper se</p>
        <p>. . 600,000 years was correct. - • Dr. MANsoN asked at what period man left off his tail. (Laughter.) It was chiefly that' appendage which was one of the chief causes of contention between the Darwinians and anti' Darwinians. MAN'S LOST " TAIL." In reply, Br. GowiLom said he did not 101° that anything he had said that nightteriered with the general religions aspect Of thingo• Until a few years ago it was taught that mau had not existed more than a few thousand years, but one now knew, undoubtedly, that he had existed at.least a million years. The same thing probably occurred in Biblical times, the views of the people being in simi'larly incorrect focus or persiptec:aileinmAmsstsoibtieheinagteheosfetlmteatlt'ielot' years-600,000 11,t be equally ucsnikiefstiyigtli on as to the tail was a difficult one drotrotoi hlwiese down Yearsto.ne wmaayuogiritetlrie) to answer, because man in a sense had never had a tail—except in so far as all of us at one period and even now, had a tail. One of the changes from animal to. man was the loss of the tail.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>3 CHILDREN HAD SHIN TRODBLR:</head>
        <p>3 CHILDREN HAD SHIN TRODBLR:</p>
        <p>-Netherley Cottage, Chalton, Hants, Eng.-- " My little girl at the age of three months suf. fered from sores on all her joints. She was most awful sight to look at. Her limbs looked as if they would fall off. I tried Cuticura SoaP and Ointment for two weeks and she was com- pletely cured. She is now five years old and has never had a blemish since. My baby girl when she was about the same age was almost as had. I bought a tablet of Cuticura Soap and a bos of Cuticura Ointment and she was cured. "My little boy at fifteen months old wall covered from head to toes with eczema. We never had a night's rest for months. Ile was raw as a piece of'beo.and what he suffered beyond words. You could not put a pin-point anywhere about his legs and body that there was not a sore place. I was unable to put hin2 to bed until I went myself as he would scratch himself raw. So I thought I would try Cuticurs Soap and Ointment. After using them a for times be seemed better and be is now cured." (Signed) Mrs. E. Wells, August 11, 1913. Cnti- cure Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sampk of each with 32-p. book post free. Address: F. Newbery and Sons, 27. Charterhouse-square, London. or Potter Drug and Chem. Corp., Boston, U.S.A.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS WOMAN TAKES POISON.</p>
        <p>Eve Pye, married woman, of Queen-street, WJ remanded for a week at st. Helens on Mondg</p>
        <p>Oil a charge of attempted suicide.—Chief-Inspee- tor Dunn said that on Saturday night, woman took a quantity of chlorodyne ano laudanum, saying that she wanted to die. Mrs. Pye had been to the pictures with her husband on Saturday night. After they returned home a quarrel took place. The husband went to bed, but soon afterwards the wife shouted upstairs that she had taken poison. Information was given to Constable Jones, when she said that she had taken the stuff with the intention of taking her life.</p>
        <p>A boy of ten, George Challis, was at Port&amp; mouth on Saturday committed for trial on 14 Coroner's warrant charged with the man' slaughter of his seven-year-old sister by puttitig her on the fire.</p>
        <p>The body of Mr. dward Payne Press (43),'17. Bristol solicitor, who imagined that he had los'. his ability to advise his clients correctly, 30! found on the Great Western Vallway yea- Clifton, and the coroner's jury on Saturds9 returned a verdict of "Suicide during ternig'r al7 insanity."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Languor and Depression</head>
        <p>Languor and Depression</p>
        <p>Has it ever occurred to you that the feeling of languor and depression which sometimes makes you feel miserable is due to stomach and liver troubles ? It pro- bably is, for even slight indigestion, or faulty action of the liver certainly doe produce depression, fatigue, and weariness. At such times it is clear that the remedy you need is one that will restore yOUr stomach, liver, and bowels to regular healthy action. Mother Seigel's SgruP does that most effectively. For over fartl. years people all over the world have found it to be a splendid and a ready means of banishing and preventing digestive trotibls, whether arising from indigestion, a sluggish state of the liver, or inactive bowels.</p>
      </div>
