        <head>No better evidence can be had as to the merits of a medicine than the evidence of one's own neighbour.</head>
        <p>No better evidence can be had as to the merits of a medicine than the evidence of one's own neighbour.</p>
        <p>(In January 6th, 1913, Mr. J. Lakin, of 16, tiolland-street, Warrington, said: "1 work ih a hot place, and I have no, doubt that "Ming out into the cold air aftex:wards was the cause of my kidney trouble. I was attacked with acute pains in the region of the kidneys. They were dragging pains, and as my work is a strain on the back they made it difficult for me.</p>
        <p>"I felt .tired and weary in the inornirigs, and was sometimes a bit dizzy. The water Was in an unnatural condition, and I knew from this that my kidneys were affected.</p>
        <p>"I tried Doan's Backache Kidney Pills for this complaint, and they proved to be very good. After taking 'these • Pills the water - became clear again and my back free from Pain. Since then I have kept a box of tloon's Pills in the house to use occasionally it I feel any signs of the old trouble.</p>
        <p>.(Signed) "J, LAKIN."</p>
