        <p>211° Rev. H. Williamson on Sunday evening a sernion on the apathy of men to the iji-,„.!,ina of life. His remarks were based upon 1 a,-rarable of the Great Supper, where the guests rejected the gracious invitation, je.)—nanse they were busied about commercial and, udane affairs. Pin,' Parable represented in many ways the 11111-VtrY of the Saviour. Men had listened, 041,-,„gone away to the farm, their merchandise, et he home, having refused the invitation arist extended. The same conditions had Prevailed throughout all time. Luther had the- cove that that men listened and went away no trel.; and to-day the congregations gathered hear the, Gospel preached, and left to busy (nrselves with mere secular interests. complete change evidenced itself, however, When the life'was.lived-in the name and service J t esus Christ. Through Him were to be won he unsearchable riches of God. AU unpleas- "nos; tribulation and persecution' lost theii camunon meaning and were accepted in a spirit °I unbounded joy. The whole life was enriched he ; Pnul 'measure, and crammed with exhilarat- ing blessedness and joy. The new attitude was °nu of complete superiority to circuinstances, lind absolute indifference to environment. It was the type of life which bore the mark oi greatness and the stamp of soYereignty. To turn away from such a life to seculai interests was amazing. Christ came that all Might have life, and have it in abundance; and Yet the majority' rejected and trampled upon the gracious invitation.</p>
