      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WESLEY GUILD FESTIVAL.</head>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD FESTIVAL.</p>
        <p>The members of the Bewsey-roadWesleyau Guild celebrated their annual festival on Sun- (blY, when several most interesting gatherings 11-ere held. Appropriate sermons were preached niorni takes who a I; no' and evening by the Rev. Harold H. urdess 1 Manchester minister, , . . en interest in Guild work. The ~.Q afternoon was d sstt!, and Mr. J. Upson presided over this inter- M. 1.4 function, in the unavoidable sidVeeesasfbuslenocregaonf , reeitn W. Hayward. A was given by Mr. 1110 W. Pearce, while the nob:Am:work was in the hands Th ilArthur and M vv. Boor. th • '4'•• J-jj'ca !f such capable Miss Elsie Wilkinson Mrs. Heywood, ‘-leiliee•o.telirees'(4il; &lt;l4l Man cfornotmin thene d Man nMlosnlda ialyd,,,,wuh_ef; . -•,red I, " 4. , A J. Costain. M. r. A. N3Plet "37 the meN. A. who was A. :• •Y sieii iltallire to devottheeto ascer- 'Ain el. time sp chairman,ec.ialsvaid. it was and that w,, the ~ Int the Guild was doing, _ Festival. "1 object which lay behind the In -wif.l.7 Inight profitubly hold festivals connected 41'''" otl ' work. ,ne G ier branches of the church's tin uild they had nearly a d perfect organi • f one nature. The evot• ea t thei 1"al, social and intellectual sides had ._ ~ie with all sides o - sa- t,tit r turn, and it would be a calamity if the 'Clinh,.llBlasta of the members declined. The ithicelizagrleilt to look for help from the Guild, :r --"' the Guild ought to remember it was part Th lecture dealt , Poet e eeture dealt chiefly with the Isle of Man !,and writer, Thomas Edward Brown, who, "e . lecturer said, was one of the greatest 7te:nlises the little island had ever produced. , tiraw his characters true to life. ...,` (6. Divine Redeemer," sung as a duet, was haR Tiorairably rendered by the Misses V. and M. ist. nes. *miss Plinston was an efficient accom 0- 13ekett n the motion of the Rev. W. A. 1. . 11....._a Y thatnilletelde.cturer and chairman. were cor- ' 11</p>
