      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. OPEN MEETING.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. OPEN MEETING.</p>
        <p>--- Wycliffe P.S.A. on Sunday afternoon must The attendance at the open meeting hart given gratification to Councillor Millar, of flee ones, who had beei invited to address the assem- bly. The Rev, T. T. James presided. Councillor Millar, speaking on deception, said it was an awful thing to deceive, for deception I was nothing less than hypocrisy, and who could konour or respect a hypocrite. When one wrong had been done other wrongs were com- mitted to cover 'up the initial sin, for it was most difficult to explain away a wrong action. On the other band right could make its own way through life in a peaceful and happy man- ner. It was most pleasing and encouraging to realise that the people of the country were awakening to their responsibilities with regard to the drink question. This problem was once only slightly and tremblingly tackled by both doctors and ministers of the Gospel, but now the prominent medical men and ministers of all de- nominations', were on the side of sobriety, and fearlessly opposed to the evils of drink. Their outspokenness had done much to,lielp the tem- perance movements.</p>
        <p>When he was a young boy he saw quite suffi- cient of the unhappiness drink had caused in his own family, and that lesson had impressed upon him the necessity of leaving strong liquor alone. During the afternoon Miss Elsie Hewiit sweetly rendered "Entreat me not," and "When I survey the wondrous Cross," and the lesson was read by Mr. M. C. Eachern. I At the conclusion the Pastor thanked the speaker and the soloist for their services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>DROWNED IN DOLLY-TUB.</head>
        <p>DROWNED IN DOLLY-TUB.</p>
        <p>S"eild. Tra. 'I: COFFEE ESSENCE</p>
        <p>The Coffee Maid, of -Symington's, says: song of Symington's, the kettle's on the si: A little drop of Essence, makes Coffee for a king." Tilos. sYkuNcros co.. Edinburgh and Lando(</p>
        <p>The drowning in a dolly-tub of a child aged 21 years named Cissie James, daughter of an iron worker, was inquired into at Ellesmere Port on Saturday. The mother was too ill to attend the inquest, but the father explained that a dolly-tub containing water had been left in the backyara and the child had fallen into it. A key had been found in the tub, and it was surmised that she had dropped it in, and in trying to recover it had overbalanced. The mother tad supposed the child to be playing in the kitchen with another child, who was ill. A verdict of " Accidentally drowned" was returned.</p>
        <p>0</p>
        <p>,611, *Wk.'</p>
      </div>
