      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>WAS ADAM 24 FEET HIGH</head>
        <p>WAS ADAM 24 FEET HIGH</p>
        <p>Remarkable Statement at a Warring-</p>
        <p>ton Adult School Meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>DESCENT OF THE GIANTS.</head>
        <p>THE DESCENT OF THE GIANTS.</p>
        <p>Was Adam 24 feet in height? This was the interesting point brought to light at the Academy-street Men's Adult School on Sunday morning, when Mr. Drewitt gave a most attrac- tive lecturette on "Giants." He pointed out how most children were interested in the fairy tales, e.g., "Jack and the Beanstalk," "Gulliver and his travels," and although as adults we discredited the existence of any such giants, yet historically known specimens- of abnormal statue maintained our curiosity. The _Child of Hale, who measured .9ft. 2in. in height, sank into insignificance when com- pared with a French scientist's calculation of the ancients—Adam only a. few inches short of 24 feet, Abraham 20 feet, Moses 13 feet, &amp;c. The decrease continued until the Christian era, when this deterioration was almost arrested, and the normal height was approximately six feet.</p>
        <p>The skeleton measuring 22 feet discovered in Sicily in 1866, although very closely resembling human beings, has been explained away as of an extinct race of brutes. Mummified remains in Liverpool Museum show that in the time of Ramases 11. the ,etature was only slightly greater than the present. The statement of giants in Canaan was not verified. Reference was then made to the massive blocks of stone tlsed in structures of remote ages. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH WOMEN'S.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH WOMEN'S.</p>
        <p>• The school had the- pleasure of a visit fibm Mr. C. IL Muir, of Sankey Bridges, who gave a .very interesting address on "Music." The speaker quoted how Plato said music was a charm to sadness. Darwin also referred to music as the basis of all language, and the speech of God. Carlyle said poetry was musi- cal thought, and as we see deep enough we see musically. Mr. Muir pointed out very clearly the soothing, spiritual effects of music, how even opium smokers had been soothed by music, when everything else had lost its effect. One smallpox' patient Was soothed in his restlessness and struggling by singing of good music, when all else had failed. Mr. Muir's address was very much appreciated. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>BEWSEY MEN'S.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY MEN'S.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning last Mr. George Fleet, of Stockton Heath School, gave an interesting and practical paper on "Gardening." As many of the Bewsey men are devoting themselves to gardening, Mr. I'leet's address proved very use- ful. In the first place Mr. Fleet said we have to consider what is a garden, or, rather, what should it be? It is the first thought of God foi man, and from it man gets sustenance and pleasure, but not without doing his own share in the cultivation of it. The first thing to con- sider is the soil paid its nature. Is it heavy, of a clayey nature, or is it light and sandy? According to the nature of the soil, so will the treatment vary. Upon this will depend whether shrubs, fruit trees, flowers or vegetables can best be grown. The speaker pointed out the importance of sunshine, and also the need of protection from north and east winds. It was necessary to 'keep tall "growers at the back of the border, from the path, and reduce down to the smallest at the front, giving each ample room for growth and air. With perennial roots it is better to have a few of the good varieties than a host of the inferior. He insisted on growing the best as far as one's money allowed. He said care needed to be exercised in arrang- ing flowering plants, in order to prevent the clashing of colours. Dealing with vegetabres, Mr. Fleet warned his hearers not to sow r, her, the soil is wet or pasty. It is advisable not to hurry, though the sun be strong, say, in March, because late frosts are capable of doing numb damage. A hearty vote of thanks w•is given to the lecturer for his instructive address. • LONGFORD.</p>
        <p>_ On Sunday morning Mr. H. Broadhurst. one of the new members of the school, gave an inter- esting paper on Christianity of the past, the T,resent, and the future. The latter part of his address dealt with the Bible lesson for tha say, so a good opportunity was given to disco's the paper and the lesson at the same time.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH MEN'S. Councillor diaries Dukes gave a very thoughtful lecture on "Ibsen and Modern Thought." The speaker dealt with the ideals of Ibsen, and spoke of the time when he became a Realist during the years 1848-49. Reference was also made to his first drama, in which Ibsen was supposed to be a Determinist. The speaker also referred to The Doll's House" and Mir sts."</p>
        <p>The Bible ' lesson dealt a ith " Judas," and there was a good discussion on the man, his ideas, and his strong will.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SANKEY BRIDGES MEN'S.</head>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES MEN'S.</p>
        <p>Dr. J. B. Nnden gave a lecturette on " Truth " on Sunday morning, his object being to show how difficult it is to grasp the meaning of truth. Truth, the speaker said, was not a big word, but it is one which is used very loosely, and often .when the word "opinion would be more correct. To some people truth is no more than common-sense. Truth is, how- ever, beyond the senses, more than thought itself. The line between the true and the false is often very fine and difficult to recognise. As an illustration, the speaker took. the recent controversy on the food value of brown and white bread respectively. Here we had on one side an eminent, fully-qualified man, proving to his own satisfaction that white bread was superior to brown. On the other side another specialist swearing by brown. What are common folk to do when experts differ in this way? Then On the question of truth in religion. Some time ago in Warrington we saw in the public prints a claim made for a certain church that it, and it alone, was the true Church. Then comes* another similar claim from another denomination. Obviously both .of them—pos- sibly neither of them—could be absolutely true.</p>
        <p>Turning to the question of individual charac- ter, Dr. Naden pointed out that until the middle of the 19th century men were bound to make the best of themselves without much help from out- side, to work their own way in life. To-day we have reached the other extreme. We attach a great deal of importance to environment, the young were surrounded by the best of every- thing, a lot was being done for them which it might be better for them to do for themselves. The results in the earlier period were, perhaps, not ideal, but are we quite sure we are improv- ing on them? We see to-day_young people with comfortable homes, and plenty of freedom, &amp;c., but we hesitate to say that they are an improve- ment on their fathers and grandfathers, who grew up strong men without many of the pre, sent-day advantages and luxuries. In closing the speaker dealt with purely mental and intel- lectual questions, and said the right attitude to all questions was an open mind; to hold our views tenaciously with the evidence we have, and to act upon it if necessary, but to be pre- pared to change our opinion when fresh evidence turned up.</p>
        <p>The Bible lesson on "Judas" was introduced by Mr. Jos. Elwell, who said a true estimate of the man's character was a difficult one: The theory of Judas being pre-destined to betray Jesus Must be ruled out as untenable. The theory of avarice had greater claims, because of the evidence that Judas was fond of money. There might be something in the opinion that Judas expected Jesus to exhibit his Messianic' powers when the soldiers laid hands on Him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>RUNCOR4'S "LOST SOULS."</head>
        <p>RUNCOR4'S "LOST SOULS."</p>
        <p>The 'extraordinary announcement printed below has appeared, said Mr. Baker at a Liberal meeting at Runcorn on Tuesday, on a local hoarding, Owing to, a billposter mixing up the announcement of a Conservative meeting with the advertisement of a picture theatre :</p>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE MASS MEETING. Lord Hugh Cecil. Dante's Inferno.</p>
        <p>Speeches by -Sir Harmood Banner • and Mr. Jersey de Knoop. Come and See the Agonies of Lost Souls !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>WARRINGTON RUNNERS,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RUNNERS,</p>
        <p>The Local Cross Country Men Again West Lancashire Champions.</p>
        <p>THE HOLDERS' FINE WIN.</p>
        <p>At Sutton. A good attendance of spectators, many from Warrington, witnessed the Senior and junior Championships of the West Lanca- shire Association, which are also the divisional qualifying races for the Northern Champion- ships, due on Saturday' next. Owing to alter- nate night and day work, it was known that Chris. Vose was not up to his usual form; there- fore the Senior race was more oi.,en, and of the fancied candidates the most favoured was Septimus Francom, one of the United King- dom's Marathon representatives at the last Olympia, and he eventually won by about 80 yards, after wonderfully steady running, as re- flected in his times for each of the five circuits of a heavy course, in covering 91 miles, about 21 being ploughed. Although the issue was confined to Warring- ton (holders), Sutton (ex-holders) and the much improved Wirral team-, a great teams' struggle WAS anticipated, but the Warrington lads, stick- ing like leeches, soon had the opposition well scattered, ank,placing their six counters in the first eight home, they won handsomely by 42 points from Sutton, who beat Wirral by seven points for second place,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>GREAT RACE.</head>
        <p>A GREAT RACE.</p>
        <p>There was a great struggle for places behind Vose and Francom, who ran the first lap to- gether 20sec., with Holbrook about 100 yards away, leading Burgess, Bolton, Han- son and Antrobus, just in front of the pack. Half-way through the second circuit, done in llmin. 22sec., Vose knocked a tee badly, but caught‘Francom again. Holbrook, breaking a lace, stbpPed, and dropped back to 20th; this called up Turton to 15th, and Parsonage to 17th to support Bolton, who, about 150 yards behind the leaders, headed Antrobus, Barnes, Burgess, Hanson and Smart. In the third lap Voae fell a 60. yards lead, in llmin. with Bolton, now closing in, about 70 yards behind Vose, and 150 yards in front of ,Antrobus,' Hanson,. Smart, Barnes and Burgess, 2a6ncl e.sel:rancom had with Holbrook, nuw,_ g instrongly, twelfth. Fighting rough wind and rain, Prancom occupied llmin. 32sec. over the fourth lap, and Bolton, now in front of Vose, had about 100 yards leeway to ,maka. up. Antrobus, , still fourth, was bringing Hanson and Burgess Along nicely, with Smart ,and Barnes just in front of Holbrook and Welding. Francom covered the final round in llmin. 24sec., and his friends had anxious moments, as Belton gradually got within 30 yards, but fortune favoured the leader b a mile from the finish, where the little lantoeuit•national, in jumping, was let down into a ditch Oft. deep, through landing on a rotten patch, and had some difficulty in crawling out whilst the Wirral man again got away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>VOSE FINISHES THIRD.</head>
        <p>VOSE FINISHES THIRD.</p>
        <p>vose finished a leisui•ely third, and may recover his form for the "Northern," which lie woo, beating Glover, in 1911, at Haydock Park. Thirty started, 23 finishing as follows:</p>
