      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <p>Mr. ASQUITH : Yes, but I have the key! Caouus or VOICES BERINR THE DOOR: Monstrous! Unfair! Unconstitutional? Let's do something to the lock! Take away his key!</p>
        <p>By kind permission of the " Westminster Gazette."</p>
        <p>since the last quarterly meeting. The 'member- ship of the affiliated societies showed a great increase during the quarter, and arrangements were made for a vAde propaganda of organising effort throughout the iron and steel producing districts of the United Kingdom, which will include Warrington. It was decided that all political action should be conducted through the Federation Executive, and steps were agreed upon to bring the question of Parliamentary representation before the various societies prior to taking a ballot of the whole of the federated members upon the necessary amendment of the Federation rules.</p>
        <p>Gasworkers' Ballot. The result of the ballot of the members of the Gasworkers' and General Labourers' Union on the question of political action under the Trade Unions (No. 2) Act has been reported to the Chief Registrar of Friendly Societies as follows: For political action, 27,802; against political action, 4,339; majority for, 23,463. The mem- bership is 145,000, so that the percentage of members voting was only 22.1. The union is responsible for the election expenses of Mr. Will Thorne, M.P., general secretary, in South-West Ham, and it is proposed to put forward other candidates.</p>
        <p>ID 7,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>PARLIAMENTARY POINTS.</head>
        <p>PARLIAMENTARY POINTS.</p>
        <p>The Anti-Home Rule Amendment • Rejected by Majority at 78.</p>
        <p>TWO MORE BY-ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Parliament was opened on Tuesday with all the old-time ceremony. The King's Speech out- lined the lills to be dealt with, and the most ' significant passage in the Speech was that in which the King referred to Ireland and the absence of a solution of the Home Rule diffi- culty. The Speech continued: "In a matte' in which the hopes and the fears of so many of my subjects are keenly concerned, and which, unless handled now with foresight, judgment, ! and in the spirit of mutual concession, threatens grave future. difficulties, it is my most earnest wish that the good-will and co-operation of mea of all parties and creeds may heal dissension and lay the foundations of a lasting settlement." This conciliatory spirit marked also the speeches of the Prime Minister and Lord Mor- ley. Mr. Asquith refused to pronounce a "last word" on the question at present. He, scorns the notion that British statesmanship is insol- vent, and is greatly sanguine of a peaceful way , out. At an early date--after the necessary financial business of the Session—he will submit the concessions which the Government lire pre- pared to make on condition that they are accepted as the basis of a lasting settlement. A debate was started in the Rouse, of Commons , on the amendment to the Address by Mr. Long. He desired a General Election on the Home Rule question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Majority for Home Rule, 78.</head>
        <p>Majority for Home Rule, 78.</p>
        <p>- _ The debate was continued on Wednesday. The character of the speech of Sir Edward Can, who spoke first, may be gathered from the fact that, despite its harsh notes at times, it was welcomed with hope by the Chief Secretary for Ireland and by Mr. Lloyd George, who attached great importance to Sir Edward Carson's appeal to Mr. Redmond to win the co-operation of Ulster. Mr. Redmond, while expressing the view that the "suggestions" should have come from the Opposition, absolutely accepted the situation 'creoted by the Premier's speech, and made a most dramatic declaration as to his tolerant views towards his Ulster fellow-countrymen. The Rouse, late on Wednesday night, divided on the Tory motion. The figures were:— For dissolution Against</p>
        <p>Henn. Rule majority... .78</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords a similar debate was in progress. Earl Loreburn appealed for a settle. ment by consent, and suggested possible bases. Lord Lansdowne said he had always been pre- pared to consider proposals for the exclusion of Ulster, with some other safeguards, but the right course for Ministers was to announce their plan and go to the country. The Lord Chan- cellor said that self-government for Ireland was a settled principle, and the first and root matter was that the Government must have some intimation from the Unionist party of their willingness to meet them on the question of principle. The debate was adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>CABINET CHANGES.</head>
        <p>CABINET CHANGES.</p>
        <p>Several Cabinet changes were officially an- nounced. Mr. Sydney Buxton, who is to follow Lord Gladstone as Governor-General of South Africa, will be succeeded at the Board of Trade by Mr. John Burns. Mr. Herbert Samuel will go to the Local Government Board, Mr. Hob- house to the Post Offiee, and Mr. Masterman ' will enter the Cabinet as Chancellor of the Duchy, while continuing his work in connection with the Insurance Commission. • This means by-elections will take place at Poplar, where the Liberal majority is 1,829, and Bethnal Green, where Mr. Masterman will have to seek re-election. The Liberal majority at the last election was 184. THE DEPORTED LABOURISTS.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thursday the debate on the motion for an Address in reply to the Speech from the Throne was continued. Mr. J. R. MacDonald, on behalf of the Labour party, moved an amendment which asked that instructions should be given to Lord Gladstone that the Indemnity Bill now under discussion in the Parliament of the Union of South Africa should be reserved until a judicial inquiry was instituted into the circumstances of the pro- clamation of martial law within the Union, and into the scope of the Bill, particularly the pro- vision relating to the deportation of trade union leaders. Mr. L. V. Harcourt (Colonial Secretary) re- plied, and after some debate the amendment was defeated by 214 votes to 50--Government majority 164. Mr. W. Brace moved a further amendment rhich expressed regret that no mention was mde of the serious and increasing number of idents in mines and on railways, and that to action for dealing with them was promised. VlcKenna (Home Secretary) replied, and he debate was afterwards adjourned.</p>
        <p>ni; n(</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>LORDS AND HOME RULE.</head>
        <p>LORDS AND HOME RULE.</p>
        <p>The Marquis of Londonderry, Eavl Curzon, and the Marquis of Crewe were the chief speakers in the concluding stage of the debate in the House of Lords on the Opposition amend- ment to the Address. The amendment declared that it would be disastrous to proceed with the Home Rule Bill until it had been submitted to the people. Lord Curzon explained the proposal to be that a general election should be taken after the Bill had been given its final shape in Parliament, but before it had received the Royal assent. The amendment was carried by 243 votes to 55—Opposition majority, 188. The Address as amended was agreed to, and the House adjourned until Tuesday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LABOUR TOPICS.</head>
        <p>LABOUR TOPICS.</p>
        <p>A Spirit of Unrest Prevailing in Warrington Industries.</p>
        <p>THE TEACHERS' UNIQUE STRIKE.</p>
        <p>BY "A WORKER."</p>
        <p>Matters in the local labour movement have once more reached the quiet stage, and very little is stirring. I am not bad enough to pre- dict that this is but the lull before the storm, but there are indications that matters will wake up shortly. There is an under-current of unrest quietly running which, in the future, may give rise to trouble in at least one industry. How- ever, upon that point it would not be wise to make any further comment, as the matter is to some extent sub judice. Without mentioning the particular trade or saying more at present, one may be permitted to state that interesting developments may he expected in the future.</p>
        <p>The Platelayers. There is still a considerable amount of feel- ing amongst the platelayers who for some weeks ran a " stay-in " strike and then downed tools for a week prior to Christmas. As I have said before, they felt sore about the way the settle- ment was made practically over their heads by I an official from Unity house, and the soreness still exists. They have not gained a single point by the strike, and so far as I can learn are not likely to, as the company concerned look upon the matter as a complete victory for them, with no spoils to go in any shape or form to the vanquished. The wages still remain as they were, namely, from ..61 Os. 6d. to .61 2s. per week for dangerous and skilled work. An effort was promised to get the Conciliation Board to re- view the position, but these boards, like the mills of the gods, grind extremely slow. It is no secret that the platelayers are fast losing all patience over the matter. Labour Party Chairman.</p>
        <p>The re-election of Mr. Ramsay MacDonald as chairman of the Parliamentary Labour Party, not the Labour Party who recently conferred lin Glasgow, with Mr. Tom Fox, the genial Man- chester man, as chairman, which some of the daily papers have mixed up rather badly, must give satisfaction to the rank and file generally, although not absolutely to the "direct action" wing. There seems to have been no other can- didate for the position, and so Mr. MacDonald enters upon a fourth year of office. I think the honour should have been made to go round, and the claims of Mr. J. R. Clynes were worthy of consideration, but they were not brought for- ward, and so Mr. MacDonald was unanimously re-elected. With the "direct action" group growing stronger, the "chief" will have a pretty warm time of it, but MacDonald is just the man capable for the position, and possibly the secret of the absence of other candidates may be found in the fact that trouble is ominously brewing between the "direct action" group and the "Parliamentary action" side. , The Teachers' Strike.</p>
        <p>I referred last week to the Herefordshire teachers' strike as unique, and further confirma- tion of its unique character has just reached me. I have been mixed up with hundreds of strikes extending over a period of 20 years, and cannot recall a body of employers sending a resolution of sympathy to a body of strikers. This unique and remarkable incident in trade unionism has actually occurred in connection with the teachers' dispute. The Abertillery Education Committee have passed and sent to the strikers the following: " That this Educa- tion Committee places on record its sympahy with the teachers lately employed under the Herefordshire Education Authority in their struggle for a living wage." Verily, we live in strange times, but the action of Che Abertillery Education Committee is one which might be endorsed by similar authorities, for if ever workers groaned under unjust conditions they are the Herefordshire teachers.</p>
        <p>Not to Budge.</p>
        <p>.___ "We do not intend to budge" is the ultimatum of the Herefordshire Education Committee at a meeting. It is all very well for these com- mittee men to say so, but they seem to have° foro•otten the fact that they are the servants of the public, whose sympathies run strong with the teachers. Meanwhile they are ignoring the public's Wishes and seeking to play the part of ,1 dictators, and meanwhile the county is losing more in grants per week than, would pay the extra salaries to the teachers for a year. It is palpably a case of the committee cutting 01l its nose to spite its face.. One member said the teachers were already too well paid for five days per week. One head mistress, by the com- mittee's own showing, receives .225 a year, not 10s. a week, and\l suppose she, besides others, is included amongst the "too well paid." A Lancashire weaver, a typist, or even some shop girls, would not look at such a wage. The strikers' motto is "We. shall win." Every trade unionist will wish it may prove so.</p>
        <p>Iron and Steel Trades Federation. The Executive Council met at the Midland Hotel, Manchester, on Saturday. The Secre- tary reported that the correspondence with other associations not at present linked up with I the Federation showed a hopeful sign of federal lunity in the iron and steel industry. The treas- urer's report showed an income of about £4,000</p>
        <p>\,•; -.</p>
      </div>
