      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>PASSING WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD As LABOUR , CHAMPION. In another colurrin of this issue of "The Examiner" is reproduced a letter from Mr. A. H. Crosfleld, which shows the Prospective Liberal candidate for Warrington in the most unusual role of critic of the Home Office, as controlled by a Liberal Home Secretary. Those who know how strong is Mr. Crosfield's Liberalism and how deep-seated his loyalty to the party, will be able to measure the strength of his feelings and his desire for the betterment of the conditions under which the worker labours, by this thorough-going attack upon one of the heads of the Government. Mr. Gros- field is closely identified with the International Association for Labour Legislation, a body that is striving to bring about an amelioration of the conditions of the working classes. It is recog- nised that action in some of the greater trades must be on an international scale, because different conditions in different countries would be a state of things that would not appeal to manufacturers competing for the trade of the world. The Association are attacking, as a preliminary, the iron and steel trade, one of the "-continuous industries," in which there is no night and day, but in which one long day of ,24 hours is divided into shifts. In some cases these shifts are two of twelve hours each, and it is against "the scandal to all Christendom lie there Are still enagM at the blast furnaces in this industry some..tens.of thousands of men whose hours of work average 84 per week," that Mr. Crosfield and his international co'- g leagues protest. They maintain that the twelve- hour shift at the blast furnaces is brutalising to the man, destructive of home life and ruinous to the true interests of the State</p>
        <p>It happens that half the blast furnace men in England enjoy the benefits o--ef an eight-hour , shift, which divides the day into three parts,. and it is thought that as England has made such a good start and shown that the thing can be done, England ought to be urged on to lead the way in some suitable kind of universal re- form. The Home Secretary was approached and asked to take the matter up over a year ago, but those advances have met with only a "barren, obdurate, unreasoning, fossilised and petrified opposition of a stone wall," which means, we suppose, that the suggestion has been "stone walled." However, Mr. Crosfield has returned ,to the attack. Speaking for the Association, he claims that he is entitled to request Mr. McKenna's serious attention and practical assistance, and that he is also justi- tied in appealing to the public for strong back- ing for the demand made and now emphatically repeated, "in the universal* interest of indus- trial democracy."</p>
        <p>£26,000 FOR EDUCATION. ' The estimates of expenditure and receipts of the Education Committee for the year 19E4-15, which will be submitted for approval on Monday evening, show that the borough rate will be Called upon for £26,000, against £24,000 for the year 1913-14. The increases are Chiefly in the school management (elementary and higher) and•the finance departments. Salaries of elemen- tarY teachers are up £l,lOO, and of secondary and evening teachers .R3OO. The total cost of public education in the borough is £58,000, but receipts from • grants, 'fees and other sources, =canting' to over £31,000 bring the deficiency to be met by the rates down to £26,000.</p>
        <p>UNIVERSITY SCHOLARSHIPS. At the Education Committee meeting on Mon- day the scheme for establishing two university scholarships, one for a, boy hnd one for a girl, of the annual value of £7O, for three years, will be submitted for approval. The scholarships are for children whose parents or guardians, have resided in the borough for two years, and are rightly limited to those whose parents are in such circumstances "as to render if impos- sible for them to afford the expense of sending their. children to the university." Applications for Candidates permissions mustcompete must be made to the Director not later than May Ist In each year. ate. more than nineteen or less than ixteen on the first day of October following the examination, which takes place. hi July,</p>
        <p>Tim JOVIAL COMMERCIALS. No more jovial body- of men can be fOttlid than commercial travellers. On the. road they are hailed as such, but when they meet around their own festiVe boards their joviality is on a par with that of Squire Wardle, made famous in "Pickwick Papers "—they simply bubble over with exuberance The Mayor must have felt this, when he attended the Warrington travel- lers' banquet as one of the chief guests, and perhaps it was the charming influence of the surroundings Which, made him declare that he would not Jalk about the Bridge. The conntle'r- dais, however, "pulled his leg"' so effectually that he did speak about that now historic structure coming into being. The Mayor of Widnes was a guest also, and it was jocularly mentioned that Widnes could set an example to Warrington. Our Mayor wittily/retorted that Widnes could not boast, for it had it bridge troubles. At present Warrington has not got its bridge, but Widnes has, and it has occasionally the bad habit of sticking between • the two shores and leaving the occupants hang- ing between heaven and the pellucid waters of `ll.e Mersey. All this was, of course, friendly chaff, but it was a point appreciated by the Commercials. ,</p>
        <p>Whispered—" TWO LITTLE DOLLIES. ' A little mite in playing With a doll as children will, Supposed she was its Mamma. Doddy, do be 'till!" Kissing the tiny waxen face, As in pretence she'd weep, Sat rocking herself to and fro, And sang--" Dodds, go to s'eeP." "Doddy go to s'eep, dear, Doddy go to rest, Doddy is me baby, Doddy is de best. Dock-a-bye, Doddy, close ooz teeny eyes, Dtiddy is me darlin', Doddy is me p'ize:"</p>
        <p>The child in her girlhood days To a dolly still would cling, Happy and gay, all the day, If she to a doll could sing. She'd press and kiss the toy and sing— Now close your brown eyes, do! I'm so weary, by-bye, dearie, Whilst mamma sings to You." " Dolly go to rest, dear, Dolly go to sleep, Creepy little mousie, little mousie creep, Creepy, creepy, creepy, little rnousie creep; Dolly is my darling, Dolly go to sleep."</p>
        <p>The child, now a woman grown, Nurses DoDie on her knee, Dollie is a baby girl, Just as sweet as sweet can be. And the real mother kisses her, As now and then she'll peep. At dimpled cheeks and laughing eyes, Singing—" Dollie, go to sleep !"</p>
        <p>"Dolly go to sleep, dear, Dollie go to rest, Dollie is my baby, Dollie is the best, Rock-a-bye baby, close your sleepy eyes; Dollie is my darling, Dollie is my prize." R. OSSULSTON-RICHE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>TRE CONSCRIPTION DEBATE.</head>
        <p>TRE CONSCRIPTION DEBATE.</p>
        <p>The debate upon conscription which ensued he.tween Capt. Crosfield and Mr. Touhnin at the Junior Liberals' rooms, practically leaves us, in military phraseology, "as we were." Capt. Crostield made out a strong case for his side, and Mr. Toulmin was equally successful in treating the other side of the subject, and it is doubtful if anyone was converted from either camp of opinion. A rather interesting point was elicited, and it was that at one time the landowners were responsible for raising fight- ing forces, and the query which followed was: If the landowners were now compelled to do so, would we hear so much about conscription? l'he upkeep of the Army has been quietly sad- dled upon the backs of a long-suffering public by the landowners, just as has the expense of maintaining the roads, for which the squires were allowed to enclose valuable common land, formerly the property of the people. , It would be interesting to see how. the squires would take a proposal to make them pay for an increased army.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>N.U. CuP DRAW.</head>
        <p>THE N.U. CuP DRAW.</p>
        <p>- - —•-•- • • - Warringtonians, a fortnight hence, will have ! the privilege of witnessing one of the most in- teresting matches in the Irrst round of the Northern Union Cup, and a record " gate " should be the rule. In Batley, the home club have no soft mark, such as Huddersfield, the cup-We-vs, have in Swinton Park, and the Warringt.on team will have to IV "whole hog- izers if they mean to reach the next round. Ihetie is a possibility that they will, and how far they will travel in the. competition, in which last year they reached the final, remains to be ! seen. The general impressihn is that they are a stronger side this season than last, and, given a fair share of luck, may once more reach the final—and, who knows, but may repeat the triumph of Jack Fish's days and bring home the coveted trophy?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>From Everywhere.</head>
        <p>From Everywhere.</p>
        <p>Don't acquire all your polish on your shoes.</p>
        <p>Be sure you are right, but don't jump to the conclusion that everybody else is wrong.</p>
        <p>The last thing in the world a man expects to do is to die.</p>
        <p>Sarcasm. Winded fare to motor 'bus conductor: Do ye go near the Hippodrome? Conductor: Yes, sir—outside only. W.F.: You're very funny, don't you think? Do ye suppose I expect to be taken up to t'dress circle?</p>
        <p>The Strike.</p>
        <p>Employer: All the hands out, on strike? What's the grievance? Clerk: They dunno yet, sir. The gentleman from London hasn't arrived yet to tell 'em, sir. —"The Tatter."</p>
        <p>More Sarcasm. "That political rival of yours is to be con- gratulated. Fie is always in the public "Yes," replied Senator Sorghum; "so is a locomotive cinder." Following Father.</p>
        <p>"Well, my boy," said the visitor lo Bobby, "I suppose some clay you expect hi step into your father's shoes?"</p>
        <p>"0, I suppose so," said .Bobby. gloomily. " I've been wearin' out everythin' else he wears since mother has learned to cut 'ern down for me."</p>
        <p>Important 'Feature. Fond Father Well, we have a new baby at our house. Motor Enthusiast (absently): What horse- power? • A Militant Housewife.</p>
        <p>Neighbour's child, at the door: Please, ma'am,, mother wants to know if you'll be so kind as to lend her your recipe Fr makin' bombs. The last one she made only smelled bad and wouldn't bust.</p>
        <p>Tim Lawyer's Part. Litigant : Your: fee is outrageous.. Why, it's more than three-fourths of what I recovered." Lawyer: "1 furnished the skill and the legal learning for your case." Litigant: "But I furnished the ease." Lawyer: "Oh, anybody can full down 4 Coal hole."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Smith Connokseim</head>
        <p>The Smith Connokseim</p>
        <p>_ ' A Ai* Man took a moor, and, wisiling Ariake htmgelf popular, invited the farmers of the lietghbonrhood to a grand luncheon. 'The f,.-Alowas first-class, and a. very good brand of champagne was provide 4 ad lib. Two old cronies were sitting next each other, and had put away- about three bottles between them. They had never tasted such champagne before. One was overheard.saying to his friend, " Weel, Donald, I am hoping it will not be long now before he gies us sonic whuskoy. These meeneral waters become terrably monoatenas I"</p>
        <p>The Point of View. "Nice land around here," said the stranger, as his dusty gig stopped in front of toe gate; "but I reckon it is too high priced for a poor man."</p>
        <p>"Well" replied the farmer, " it is worth every ha'penny of £5O an acre. Were you thinking of buying?" • " No," replied the stranger, as he dotted Some- thing down into a book, "I'm the new county valuer."</p>
        <p>Who He Was. An old negro was up in court one morning. "Are you ,the defendant?" 'asked a man in the court mem, addressing the coloured man. " No, boss," replied the man. "I ain't done nothin' to be called ntunes like dat. "se got a lawyer. here who does de defendin'." • "Then," asked the man, "who are you?" " Why," said the negro, "Fse de gen'lemun at took de chickens."</p>
        <p>The College Graduate.</p>
        <p>"The college graduate," said a well-lcnown New Yorker, not long ago, "has many charms, but the chiefest of them is, perhaps, his cock- sureness. All, if .we were all but as cocksure as the young college graduate on commence- ment. day!</p>
        <p>"I know a manufacturer who took his son, fresh from Yale, into the mill last fall to learn the business. I met this man the other day and said</p>
        <p>"'You took George into the mill ree.ently to teach him the business. flow is it 'turning out?'</p>
        <p>"The manufacturer passed his hand over his brow and sighed. • "'Oh, splendidly,' he said. George is teach- ing me now.' "</p>
        <p>LIFE IS A WAYSIDE INN. Life is a wayside inn Whence some depart' Before the feast has been Brought on They start Upon their journey long But ill inclined The feasting, wine and song To leave behind.</p>
        <p>Some tarry till the board Be fully spread Then ere the wine is poured They go, unfed. But some the livelong day Feast, dance and sing— At eventide they pay The reckoning.</p>
