      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, FEBRUARY .14, 1014.</p>
        <p>FIRM, BUT CONSIDERATE.</p>
        <p>In a KING'S Speech that will stand out as a ' conspicuous landmark in the pages of History, the most prominent feature will be the powerful appeal made this week by HIS MAJESTY in the sacred cause of national peace. Having expressed regret that the efforts to arrive at a solution by • agreement of the problems con- nected with the Government of Ireland had not, "so far," succeeded, his nuEsry pro- ceeded:—.</p>
        <p>In a matter in which the hopes and fears of so many of my subjects are keenly concerned, and which, unless handled now aid' foresight, judgment, and in the spirit of mutual conres- sion, threatens grave future difficulties, it is my most earnest wish that the goodwill and co- operation of men of all parties and creeds may heal dissension and lay the foundations of a lasting settlement.</p>
        <p>There is some division of opinion as to how this Royal recommendation to adopt the "spirit of mutual concession" has been accepted, but all reasonable men, certainly all patriotic men, will rejoice if the fact that the absence of fire-eater speeches on both sides may be taken to mean that a falr hearing iS to be given to the question, and that prejudice and bias are not to be allowed to usurp the functions of reason and cominon-sense. We shall all have to be on our guard in such a case against the, temptation that might: make either side take an undue advantage of the other side by making advances appear to be Avealinesses. The danger that lurks in making such use of signs of conciliation may be seen in the warn- ing conveyed in the following quotation from the ' Ulster Guardiart : If there had been less talk of compromise on the Ministerial side and-in Ministerial papers, the position would have been much easier to- day. Every offer of concession after the "civil war" business started gave great encourage- ment to Ulster Unionists, and confirmed them, in the belief that the Liberal Government only needed to be threatened and bullied to be forced' to climb down. Had it been made perfectly clear by every English supporter of Home Rule' from the first day there was talk of rebellion in Ulster, that the Government would concede, nothing to threats which it refused to argument , there would have been just as much opposition to Home Rule in Ulster as there is at present,' , but there would have been infinitely less "civil war" talk and stage preparations.</p>
        <p>_ In any event, we cannot get away from essen- tials and the inevitable. Before Mr. ASQuITH entered upon his grave and powerful argument for the application of "an 'honest mind and a good will," on both sides, to the Irish problem, he set himself to sweep away the misconcep [ions which are preventing candid discussion. - He showed that in a debate six months before the General Election of December, 1910, the Issue had been raised in the House of Comrrions to exclude a Home Rule Bill from the operation of the proposed Parliament Bill. The then Home Secretary (Mr. cliunculf.l) frankly declared that if, under,the operation of such a measure, a Home Rule Bill passed through the House of Commons in three successive sessions and Over a period of two,ybars., the 11111 would embody the settled convictions and will of the maiblifY of the People of the United Kingdom. Sir EDWARD Guises, on the.same occasion, con- tended that the resolutions, on which the Parliament Act was subsequently founded, were brought in with-the ulterior object of passing the Home Rule Rill. , Arld in view, of the declaration by /%11., Cstmeinu. ,and the conteiF tion of Sir EDWARD CASON, the Ilouse of Com- mons threw out the .amendment to exclude Home Rule, and passed the resolutions leading up to the Parliament Act. Mr. ASQUITH also, to the indignation and discomfiture of the Opposition, Cited LORD LANSDOW NE's former testimony "0, before the General Election of December, 1910, the Prime Minister had made 1' it clear that, if the Parliament B.til were approved by the electors, it would be used to carry Home Rule. It was approved; it is being used for the purposes which the electors. desired. The by-elections show the country as determined to do justice to Ireland as in December, 1010. In the five most recent con- tests, while Parliament has not been sitting, there voted for Home Rule 46,800, against Home Rule 30,500, or a majority of 16,300 in favour, representing a net gain for Home Buie of 2,100 votes upon the corresponding elections in December, 1910.</p>
        <p>Mr:Asomm holds it to be of fundamental importance that any intittsures taken with a view to settling this question must be "healing." A General Election would not be healing. If the Liberals won, as they un- doubtedly would, Ulster would • still say "'NC will • not have flome. Rule." If the Unionists won, what would the majority of Ireland, baulked in their just ,demand, say? Nothing that would be " healing," we are sure. If Ulster were excluded, would either side be satisfied? Mr: ASQUITH has announced that, as soon as the necessary financial business is . transacted, the Government will produce its proposals with a view, not merely to avoid bloodshed and civil war, but to effect a final settlement of a question which it would be a scandal, to British statesmanship if we cannot settle finally and with, goodwill. In tile mean- time the best minds of those who really look at the Irish question not -from a party or sectional standpoint, but from the point of view of the permanent and vital interests Of the British Empire, will be employed in seeking a solution. In brief, the Liberal party are Willing to settle the question on equitable lines, but they are determined to maintain that equity embraces justice to the Irish nation, and that a concession to threats of riot •is not in any way covered by the teeth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>ARE WE DRIFTIIIG !GREAT DEMONSTRATIONS.</head>
        <p>ARE WE DRIFTIIIG !GREAT DEMONSTRATIONS.</p>
        <p>Unemployment and Free Meals in</p>
        <p>"Protected" America.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>HARD MINT AGAINST INTERESTS,</head>
        <p>HARD MINT AGAINST INTERESTS,</p>
        <p>By H. WHITHAM.</p>
        <p>We, in the United States, are in a very peen- liar position right now. For many years Pro- tection has been a very strong plank in the platform tif tlu3 governing party. Mr. Taft was elected President, with a platform in which. the Protection plank was but loosely nailed down. It was supposed to be a movable plank.. It was like the monkey on the stick, but no one knew how or when to pull the string. In fact no one seemed to know 3,here the numkey was, whether at the bottom or the top of the stick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>EXPECTATIONS FROM THE NEW PRESI DENT.</head>
        <p>EXPECTATIONS FROM THE NEW PRESI DENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wilson has followed Mr. Taft; the' country has been drawing a very long breath ever since the election. At last we had selected —and fortunately elected—a good Christian gentleman—one who was not only a Pedagogue, but a past master in politics, and knew how to think out a question, make up his mind, and boldly give his policy to the people. A pretty big book will ,be written, and an interesting one when the history of Protection is given to the world, since the day when Roosevelt entered the White House till the pre- sent day. Since Mr. Wilson made his triumph- ant entry into Washington he has been dragooned, cartooned and lampooned. Wall- street would have marooned him could that sacred precinct have fashioned a lasso° long enough to reach him, and strong enough to hold him.</p>
        <p>An eminent financial writer on November 3rd said: " President Wilson is gathering data pre- liminary to urging anti-Corporation laws. The Department of Justice is on the warpath against combinations. The, Department of Commerce is preparing to join the crusade. Capital, rightly or wrongly, hears it is to be subjected by the present adminihration to one unsettling onslaught after another." December 23rd thø. same writer quoted the President as saying: "I gain the impression more and more from week to week that the business men of the country are sincerely desirous of conforming with the law." _ . .</p>
        <p>This shocked the Writer and almost ruined his patriotism. Listen to him: "What extra- ordinary statements these be. They are enough to cause -nericans to hide their heads in hum- iliation. Are we living under a democracy or an autocracy? Are we governed by laws, by writtrl statements, or by the changing whims of Washington officials? Was ever Roman tyrant so clothed with autocracy? And this in the land of the free ,and the home of the brave!'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>"A SAD MESS."</head>
        <p>"A SAD MESS."</p>
        <p>Cooling down, this financier says, in parting with his readers: It is a sad mess for a nation of civilised beings to find itself in." I must make an effort here to describe the "mess" as I see it.</p>
        <p>In a speech made about the time Mr. Wilson was elected to the Presidency, he gave Wall- street to understand that should there be any attempt to unlawfully disturb the trade of the nation, or to interfere with his methods of en- forcing the laws, he would snake use of all the powers the Constitution placed in his hands to punish the guilty ones. This speech has never been forgiven. The President wishes the governing "big business" to be observed.</p>
        <p>; interests connected with trusts, railways and ' banks are determined that ir "mere Pedagogue" is unable to understand business, and must let it alone. The fight is now on. Wilson is beaten. Everything is at a standstilL In San Francisco there are thousands walking the streets. I went into the city to-day to view the situation. On a pile of building rubbish, a relic of the fires, were crowded half-a-dozen women. Several hundreds of the unemployed were listeners. I learned that other meetilgs were going on in the parks. I visited them. One was opposite our largest hotel. While standing listening to the speakers, one word-- " Protection "—made havoc with my powers of attention. Where-must I look for all the blee- sings tz,f Protectioh? There, right before me. In that Wel, which was, and ithere one would .have beers th.rough It, Yes, s, a palace. I find tepreaeataives of the world's his butii9ess, for its fame is international. But what about the anempleYed? Oize Gf tho fur the part he took in the speakers was a woman—Mrs. Parsons. Her HhusayblimaadrtYgernitttf.:eclid, icago, 26 years ago. This woman has thrown her whole soul into the movement. She is a very impassioned sPeaker. The memory of her dead husband seems to be always with her. From here to the Palace Hotel, where I found 4 another large crowd, with several speakers. Soon I heard the suggestion made: 'Raid the. hotel!" The crowd, wisely, did not take kindly to the idea.</p>
        <p>--The unemployed armY is divided between Socialists and the Industrial Workers of the World.</p>
        <p>The Socialists' desire is to avoid trouble with the authorities.</p>
        <p>The Industrial Workers of the World urge that nothing but "direct action will make the 'higher ups' take notice." A sample of its direct action was given when a sailor appeared wearing the ribbon of the cruiser California. The blue jacket was rescued by the police. All the same, an incipient riot had commenced.</p>
        <p>THE UNEMPLOYED. While jobs are being sought for the unem- ployed, and while money is being raised, the Citizens' Bureau is hard at work supplying meals. Fully 5,000 men had a substantial din- ner given to them yesterday:_</p>
        <p>The Relief committee's offer is 20 cents an hour for an eight hours' day. The direct actionists demand three dollars for a four-hour day. Whenever we, in the United States, do dny thing you may - always depend upon this: There will be a joker hidden somewhere in the move- ment. The demand made by the Industrial Workers of the World may not be so outrageous as it seems at first. 'rh6 four hours' day sup- poses two shifts. In plain English, bio men, working at shovelling and picking work, get 245. for eight hours' work. But this is the least , important part of the joke. Throughout the United States there is a large and a growing body of employers who, some years ago, banded themselves together under the distinctive title of "The Citizens' Alliance." Their object is to crush out trade unionism. They can only accomplish their object in one way, that is, by flooding the ranks of labour. Pretty well all the work being done on the Exposition is now under contract, the men being pledged not to create any labour troubles while the buildings are being erected.</p>
        <p>If the out of works accept 20 cents and work eight hours, they will receive 6s. as pay. The employers will then take advantage of the large number of men they are enabled to get at the reduced wage, and a general reduction of wages and increase of hours, would be attempted. Believe me, something would happen then, and the world would hear of it.</p>
        <p>IL WHITHAM.</p>
        <p>659, Broderick-street, San Francisco.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>Liberals' Busy Time Next Week.</head>
        <p>Liberals' Busy Time Next Week.</p>
        <p>Two great Liberal demonstrations take place in Warrington next week-end. On Friday, under the auspices of the Warrington Liberal Association, a mass meeting will be held in the Parr Hall. The chief speaker will be Mr.. Hamar Greenwood, Ml'. for Sunderland. Mr. H. Greenwood will be remembered as the speaker at the overflow meeting held in con- nection with Mr. Winston Churchill's great Lancashire tour some years ago, and he then made a favourable impression. Other speakers will be Mr. P. M. Oliver and Mr. A. H. eras&amp;ld.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>LAND REFORM DEMONSTRATION.</head>
        <p>LAND REFORM DEMONSTRATION.</p>
        <p>A monster Land Reform Demonstration will be held in the Parr Hall a week to-night, under the auspices of the Junior Liberal Association, in support of the Government's proposals. The speakers will include: Messrs. G. Hay Morgan, ALP.; Alderman P_ Wilson Rattan, M.P. for Leigh; Dr. C. W. Hayward, prospective Liberal candidate for Eddisbury; and Mr. J. B. Martindale. Mr. A. H. Crosfield will preside. Representatives will attend from the immediate districts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. T. H. LYON.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. T. H. LYON.</p>
        <p>A distinguished Army and Naval veteran passed away yesterday in the person of Mr. Thomas Henry Lyon, deputy lieutenant for the County of Chester, at his residence, Appleton Hall, Appleton. Mr. Lyon, who'.was in his 89th year, had been an invalid for some time, but the news of his death came as a shock to many who knew Him in Warrington. Educated at Eton, he joined the Royal Navy in 1839. He served during the first China War, being successively attached to H.M. Ships Im- pregnable and Blonde. At the Battle of Chin- her foo Mr. Lyon received a slight wound. In 1843 he joined the Inconstant and was made a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>laws Crimean War he fo___rtned, part of the garri The 'Chester</head>
        <p>laws Crimean War he fo___rtned, part of the garri The 'Chester</p>
        <p>His estates are entailed to his nephew, major Charles Lyon, R.A., son of Colonel Francis Lyon, who married Flora, sister of the 19th Vis- count Valentin. In 1905 Mr. and Mrs. Lyons' only child became the bride of Lord Acton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>AS A CHURCH BUILDER.</head>
        <p>AS A CHURCH BUILDER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lyon largely assisted in re-building the church at Stretton, and when -the restoration of the Warrington Parish Church took place be gave the stone that was needed. The rifle range in the old quarry at Appleton was given by him during the South African War. 'Members of the Cheshire Hunt are well acquainted with his good sportsmanship. Ele was for many years a prominent member of the' Tarporley Hunt. The Warrington Golf Club, out of respect ler the deceased, have closed the course td, 'Monday and Tuesday; The funeral will take place at 3.30 on Tiatio'. 4:1447 Ilaintoou at tstvellAnt, Church. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>DEATH OF MR.F. W. WAREIII6.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR.F. W. WAREIII6.</p>
        <p>It was with regret that the inhabitants of Grappenhall and the Warrington district gener- ally learned of the death of Mr. Frederick , William Wareing, which occurred early on Monday morning at his residence, The Poplars, Grappenhall. Mr. Wareing had .been suffering from an internal complaint for the past seven months. Ile was 49 years of age. The deceased was the works manager of Messrs. William Robertson, Limited, pol- ished shafting manufacturers, of Latchford, and also a director, and was greatbr respected by the workmen, as also by ii largo number of tradesmen in different parts of the country, whose business brought them in contact with him. Mr. Wareing was the son of Major and Mrs. Wareing, of Widnes, where he spent the early years of his life. For a few I years he held an appointment with the United Alkali Company, and was stationed at Fleetwooq.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wareing came to Warrington about 18 Years ago, upon the incorporation of Messrs. Robertson's establishment, and chiefly by his foresight and basiness acumen the firm has dereloped with rapidity. In 1892 he married Miss Jessie Pritchard, of Farnworth, who sur- vives him, as ia Iso do his two sons and two daughters'.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wareing was a man of fine physique, and he loved the open air life. lb e was an excellent golfer and had been connected with the War- rington and Lymm Golf elubs. He was a mem- ber of the Warrington Canine Society, and up to about three years ago was a member of tbe Warrington Academy.</p>
        <p>—At one time he served on the GrappenhO Parish Council and took a keen interest in paro- chial affairs. He was a Conservative in politics. but was not identified with any clubs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The interment took place on Wednesday after noon at Farnworth, near Widnes, when a fluor ber of mourners attended. A large number of floral tributes were sent, and these included the following: Mr. and Mrs. A. V. Baxter, Mr. ancl Mrs. F. A. Frost, Major Appleton and all at, Birchfield, Mrs. Tunstall and family, Dr. an° Mrs. Robinson, Mr. and Mrs. Robertson, "Toni and Effie," "Joe and Lilly," Mr. and Mrs. f: Neil and family, Mr. Fred Fogg, "Jack all°, Nina," " Charlie," "Bob and Ada," Mr. an Mrs. J. D. Pennington, Mr. and Mrs. Hodgkin" son and family, Mr. and Mrs. J. C. Parr, the Grappenhall Brass Band, Brydon and Co. (M cliester), the staff and workmen of Messrs. Wfr ham Robertson, Ltd., the directors of willlBlo • Robertson, Ltd., "Sarah," Mr. and Mrs. a g• Short, Mr. L. M. Monk. Mr. and Mrs.</p>
