      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <p>Guardians' Loan. On the recommendation of4he' Finance Cora- mittee it was decided to take up a loan for the money required for the new epileptic block at 31 per cent., repayment to be made over a period of 50 years.</p>
        <p>"Beer—for Drinking." • At the Guardians' meeting on Wednesday the Rev. A. M. Mitchell called attention to an ac- count of £2 7s. 3d. for ales and beers, and asked for what purpose the ale had been bought.—Mr. Garnett: For drinking, sir. (Laughter.)—The Clerk: It has been consumed in the Workhouse, on the recommendation of the Medical Officer.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. F. Taylor's Will. The will of the late Mr. Frederick Taylor, Manchest4r-road, for many years secretary to the Gas Company and the Gas Committee, has been preyed at a sum amounting. to X 10,368. Subject to an annuity for his niece and house- keeper, Miss Dyke, Mr. Taylor left this sum to be equally, divided among his five sons.</p>
        <p>"A Happy Thought," The Warrington Guardians On Wednesday decided, on the recommendation of the Clerk, that Mr. Partridge act as temporary master of the Workhouse until Mr. Losham, the new maater, could come. Mr. Partridge would then he in residence at the Institution and do what was required.—Mr. Mather: The idea is a happy thought.</p>
        <p>,flock Labourers' Protest. At a special meeting of the Warrington branch of the Dock Labourers' Union, the following resolution was carried unanimously: " That this meeting desires to enter its emphatic pro- test against the treatment of our brother trade unionists in South Africa, and calls on all mem- bers of this Government to institute a Govern- ment inquiry on the assembling of Parliament."</p>
        <p>St. John's Church Bazaar. As will be observed from our advertising columns, a grand bazaar in aid of St. Johp's Presbyterian Church will be held in the Cairo- street Schools, on the 28th, 27th and 28th inst. We Jinderstand the object of the effort is to pro= vide funds sufficient to purchase the freehold of the land, on which the new church in Wilders- pool-causeway stands., Members of the church' and their friends have been working indefati- gably for a very considerable time, and the result, it is confident', expected, will be a huge success.</p>
        <p>, . , Tramps on the Decrease. At the Warrington Guardians' meeting on Wednesday the Rev. A- M. Mitchell asked what was the reason of the decrease in the number of tramps during the past year at the Work- house. There ivas a deereaB° °f IC* tramps, .policy of the Guardians that and lie would like to know whether that was crimes, preferring prison to the due to the increase of work, or that it meant something not quite •so good. Was it through men deeotmonlinitinteded thing in Workhouse?--Air. Jolley. The decrease is (Laugh- pro- tbearb.&gt;,_ly Adu3efetneihNeirr; ThMitecrheemll'asyorbeatosryome• that.—lkfr. Mather: This has all been before the House Committee.</p>
        <p>Reelod)lles' Tea and Concert. •Lo. n The first annual tea and concert of the "Zion" tlie Tent Room, Primitive Methodist Schoolroom, Legh-street. About sixty persons partook of tea, which was served by the Misses day Tent in (4,380 Rechabites was held o Tues. Woodland, Jones, Abbotts, Buckley and Shaw. The Rev. A. 'J. Chessum presided at the concert. The speaker, Bro. J. Gaskell, District Deputy Ruler, of Orrell, gave an address, and the fol- lowing were the artistes: Miss N. Kershaw, Messrs. J. Tomkinson and R. Syers; Annie Edwards, Violet Smith, and David Plinston. The Band of Hope Union Choir (Mr. Spruce. conductor) rendered choruses. Miss Spruce was the pianist. The Secretary (Mr. B. T. Parkes) gave his report which said the adult member- ship was: Males 20, females 17; honorary mem- ber 1; juveniles--boys 12, girls 17; total 67. The Chief Ruler (Bro. J. H. Jones), on behalf of the Tent, gave thanks to the Chairman-and all who had assisted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>Inmates Entertained.</head>
        <p>Inmates Entertained.</p>
        <p>The inmates of the Workhouse thoroughly enjoyed the concert which had been arranged for them' on Tuesday evening. Mrs. Henry Roberts presided, and an excellent and Well varied programme, which had been arranged by Mrs. Whitby, was gone through. The minstrel troupe from the Wycliffe Congregational Church, Mesdames Hewitt,. Pennington, Holmes, Pye and Mills, gave a delightful number. The ' other artistes, who acquitted themselves admir- ably, were Mr. F. Webb, Miss H. Thomson, , Master H. Edwards, Mr. A. Webb,, Mr. R. Ash, worth, Mr. W. Clayton, Miss L. Kendrick: lifiss' Cattail, Miss Jones, Mrs. Whitby, and Mrs. Gray. The sketch, "Jackets," was given in a very able manner by the Misses Kendrick, 'Morrie, Thomson, Whitby and Hardman. Mr. , Dale proposed a, hearty vote of thanks to Mrs. Roberts, and also to ths artiste. ' The Guardians thanked Mr. Dale at the meet- ing on Wednesday.</p>
        <p>For e sixth year in succession, and, 'Warrington Photographic Sociweet:llm. the e very greatly regret to say, maybe for the last Mr. E. Seymour, of London, lectured, on Tues- day evening, to the above society on his favourite theme, "Plower Photography." Not many lecturers can make two solid hours pass without notice on the part of his hearers, but the forty members and friends who were present had that pleasant experience, for Mr. Seymour's manner and method, as well as his matter, are unque in their interest He had forty of his old slides to talk about, as well" as a hundred new studies, and it did a good deal to remove any idea that great pictures are the result of flukes to see how his celebrated "White Cur- rants" evolved into perfection through much study, much labour, and many unsuitable or futile attempt&amp; The ever-beautiful "grapes and apples" was equally interesting in its his- tory, but the humour of "She's only been with us a week !" was, perhaps, the hit of the even- ing. When one learns, too, that "White Cur- rants" has brought its lucky and talented author a long way over £lOO in hard cash, it ; proves that there is money in a successful photo- graph, as well as in a suecestful painting. The F hearty • thanks were ably • expressed by Mr. W. Daintith, and seconded by Mr. Hartley. Bank Quay Working Men's Mission.</p>
        <p>The Senior Men's Class in connection with the above thriving institution on Saturday held their annual social. There would be 90 at tea, which was thoroughly enjoyed, and certainly entitled the caterers to the praise so unani- mously given. After tea a concert was held in the Schoolroom. This proved most enjoyable and successful, applause being freelS, given to each artiste, and when all did so extremely well it would be invidious to select any particular one for praise. Mr. Williams presided, and the following ladies and gentlemen contributed : miss M. Blackburn gave "April songs" and "Mifanwy "; Miss C. Rigby, "Goodbye" and "The dear old home songs"; Mr. J. Angus, "The Skipper" and "The Fisherman"; Mr. J. Tynan, "Songs of Araby" and "Let me like a soldier fall"; Mr. G. Dale, two humorous original read- ings; Mr. F. Webb, pianoforte solo; Mr. A. Webb, violin solo; and Mr. Callam, solo on the tin whistle; Messrs. Tynan and Angus, a duet. Mr. T. Tanner, as accompanist, won the greatest praise from the artistes. The President, Captain N. Hough, in a neat speech, proposed the usual vote of thanks, which was accorded most heartily. The arrangements, which were carried out in admirable order, were in the cap- able hands of Messrs. Thomson. Ball, Whit- worth, Campbell, Webb, Croft, Cox and Long, , the latter gentleman being responsible for the , musical portion. After the concert parlour games for prizes were entered into. The wirp ners in the ladies' section were Mrs. Gray, Mrs. , Ball, Mrs. Backhouse and Mrs. Bodle; the winners in, the gentlemen's section being Messrs. Thomas, Eccleston, Tynan and Oliver.</p>
        <p>' Mr. Crosfield and Billiard League. Mr. Arthur erodeld has given a magnificent shield as a trophy for the Second Division of the Warrington and District Billiard League. lie will formally hand over his present to the League officials next Thursday night at the Liberal Club.</p>
        <p>Warrington Nursing Association. The Honoraiy Treasurer (Mr. Henry Roberts) begs gratefully to acknowledge the sum of £l6 lls. 3d. received as proceeds of a concert given in the Hippodrome on Sunday, February let, by Mr. Halliwell and the members of Cros- field's Band. The treasurer also desires pub- licly to acknowledge the kindness and courtesy of Mr. 'rurpin and the staff for their valuable assistance and interest, and to thank the pro- prietors of the Hippodrome for the free use of the building on the occasion.</p>
        <p>I Tradesmen's Whist Drive. The annual whist drive and social in connec- tion with the Warringtongrradesmen's Associa- tion took place at the Winmarleigh Cafe on Thursday, and was largely aftended. The ar- rangements were carried out by Messrs. C. E. Toft (president of the association), T. Dom- vile, B. Tyrer, J. E. White, H. Rogers and T. Whitehurst, and everything passed off most suc- cessfully. The following ladies and gentlemen kindly contributed selections during the even- ing: Madame Lily Fairhurst, Miss Brough. Messrs. Hannan, Dutton and Bate, and each met with a cordial greeting. • The whist drive prize-winners were as follow—Ladies: 1, Mrs. Owen; ° 2, Mrs. Balmford; 3, Miss Brough. Gentlemen: 1, Mr.. 3. Banks; 2, Mr. Barker; 3, Mr. Johnson.</p>
        <p>Holy Trinity Social. Success waited upon the efforts of the promo- ters of the Holy Trinity social, which took place on Thursday night in the Patten Hall and the schoolroom adjacent. An excellent programme was submitted by Messrs. J. Babbitt and J. B. Poole, and the Misses Atkinson and Rigby. The Misses Longdin and Young accompanied. Mr. S. Worthington was the M.C.• for dancing, and for the whist Messrs. J. Harding and J. Dykes. Mr. T. Broadhurst supplied the Music for the dance. 'The-winners of the whist were—Ladies: Miss L. Hayes and Mrs. J. Dykes; and for the gentlemen, Mr. Redhead and Mr. Cross, jun. The refreshinents were in charge of Miss M. Leigh. Praise is due to Mr. J. J. Hesketh and his band of workers for the admirable manner everything had been carried through, and the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, in a short speech, gave expression to these feelings.</p>
        <p>Whrrington Philomathic Society. At a meeting of the above society on Wednes- day evening, in the Old Parlour, Cairo-street Schools, Mr. Harry Sherburn opened an ani- mated and instructive discussion on Trade Unicnism." He was followed by Messrs. J. Walsh, A. H. Rathbone, R. M. Monk, A. I. New- ton, H. Dutton, T. E. Knowles and D. J. Dunn. The subject was dealt with in a wide range of aspects. These included the growth of modern unionism from its earlier forms, and its rela- tion to the 'present commercial system; the moral phase of the trade union movement; the introduction of labour-saving machinery, and its effect on employment; the organised restric- tion of output; Syndicalism and the strike as rational remedies. Political action and the limitation of dividends, as means of improving the present industrial system, were also criti- cised. Mr. J. Walsh (president) occupied the chair.</p>
        <p>Magistrates' Severe Censure. At the Warrington Juvenile Court on Wednes- day a schoolboy, named John William Keane, of 3, Silver-square, was ordered to receive six strokes with -the birch-rod for stealing a foot- ball, the property of the Boteler Grammar School. It was shown in evidence that the boy's mother was drinking on several occasions in a neighbour's house when visits were made to her house regarding the theft.—Mr. J. Upson, who presided, addressed a few striking words to the mother, telling her the magistrates thought she was far more. to blame than the boy, and if she had done her duty to him he would not have been in that position. "Unless you do your duty better there is nothing in. front of your boy but a career of crime. . • He is here entirely through lack of parental control . . . and we want you to promise us that you will take great care of him, and do your best to train him aright," remarked the Chairman.--The mother promised to do this.</p>
        <p>Commander Evans To-night. Commander Evans will be welcomed by a fine audience in Parr Hall to-night, when he will speak of his 'experiences with the Scott expedi- tion to the South Pole. There has been a big demand for tickets, but we are informed by Mr. R. H. Pearson that there are still eighty ss. dress seats available, as well as a number of nnr reserved seats'at 2s. and le. &amp;I. Some is. tickets and tickets for the platform will be availab4 at the doors to-night at 7.30. Commander Evans would riot have been giv- ing his lecture to-night had it not been for the devoted courage of two men, Chief Stoker Lash- ley and Chief Petty Officer Crean. How these gallant comrades of Commander Evans saved his life forms one of the most striking incidents that the lecture contains, and it always calls forth the hearty applause of an audience. Many brave deeds were accomplished during .the course of the expedition, as those who are pres- ent to-night will learn. Lashley and Crean re- ceived medals from his Majesty the King, and they thoroughly deserve the great praise Com- mander Evans gives them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <p>OSEAM and TAN um ELECTRIC LAMPS at reduced pricee.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street. Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>Liberals Busy.</head>
        <p>Liberals Busy.</p>
        <p>It will be satisfactory to the rank and file of the Liberal party to know that their enthusiasm is being well reflected in official circles. One of the most successful rounds of ward meetings ever held in the history of the party will close with one to be held on Monday night in the Junior Liberal Club, when the Howley Ward stalwarts will lay their plans for the coming year. To-night Mrs. Henry Roberts will entertain Whitecross Ward workers to a hotpot at the Win marleigh Cafe, after which the officers will be elected and other business transacted. .</p>
        <p>° -- entenrtl'e a lun: dclaiytt tAhetksitnewsoanrds,ecoaff:ll:o) Trgiivelbs. fork tea. The Women Liberals will complete their ward teas and socials as follows ;—Monday, Orford Ward, Brick-street School; Thursday, Fairfield and Howley Wards, Brick-street School; Mon- day, February 23, St. Austin's Ward, Thewlis- street School; Whitecross Ward, date to be fixed, Thewlis-street School. Then there will be two mass meetings—one next Friday night and the other next Saturday night, as dealt with in separate paragraphs. Mr. A. 11. Crosfield and Mrs. Crosfield will be present at all the gatherings on and after Mon- day.</p>
        <p>Further Leather ,Trade Improvement. The trade as generally experienced in this district was of strengthening character, and even in heavy leather a decided improvement is taking place. Orders from the home market are tending to greater volume, and with the spring trade well in hand, the demand will likely grow more urgent; merchants have been taking up freely such odd lots as would come in at old figures, and whilst nothing in the way of a clearance is to be finessed now, in the aggregate a considerable amount of leather has been moved during the week. Home buyers are now meeting much competition from out- side; possibly, had stocks been larger, it would not have been felt much, but with these so small it is bound soon to have a very marked effect upon supplies, and it follows, of course, upon prices. I met with a case this week where a few tons of heavy pinned shoulders had been hanging fire some time at 12d., but were picked up on sight by an American buyer at 121 d., and a further lot booked to follow at the same price. Prices are growing steadily higher and there is an insistent demand for advanoes on all stocks that are in special demand; in some instances prices are up a full penny on Decem- ber quotations, and with hides mounting at the speed they have lately one, it can only be ex• pected that all classes will be brought into line. —The Warrington correspondent of the "Leather Trade World." '</p>
        <p>PEVERIL FIRE GRATES never fail to please.— Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND. Ironmonger, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 14, 111.4.</p>
        <p>25,552 Insured Persons.</p>
        <p>The insured persons in Warrington now num- ber 25,552, giving an average of 983 for each doctor on the panel.</p>
        <p>Wood's College.</p>
        <p>We understand that during this week four students at the above College have been placed in good positions with various leading firms in town.</p>
        <p>"Futility of Political Action."</p>
        <p>"The Futility of Political Action" will be discussed by Nun Nicholas, South Wales, at the meeting of the Freedom Fellowship at Market Gate on Friday night at 8 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>Museum Committee's Lecture.</head>
        <p>Museum Committee's Lecture.</p>
        <p>"Black and White Art" will be the subject of the Museum Committee lecture in the Co- operative Hall to-night The lecturer will be Mr. E. R. Dibdin, who has a fascinating story to tell.</p>
        <p>Appointment for Deputy Mayor. Dr. Joseph, the Deputy Mayor, has been ap- pointed a member of the Executive Committee of the National Association of Insurance Corn- mittees, and he was congratulated upon his appointment at the meeting of the Warrington Insurance Committee on Wednesday.</p>
        <p>Sales by Auction. Mr. Robert Lowe, Market Gate Salerooms, rose trees, &amp;c.. Wednesday, 11 a,m. Mr. Robert Lowe, Market Gate Salerooms, furniture, Wednesday, 2.30 p.m. Mr. W. Brewer&amp; Lyme-street, china, Lc., sale commences 21st PebruarY, 7 pan.</p>
        <p>Crusade Against Conscription. A Thursday public at meeting 7tion will be held in the Co-operative Hall o n Mr. J. W. Berry, of the Australian Freedom League, will speak on the Conscription laws in Australia, andr the Rev. H. Bonnie° will also speak. The Chairman p ant.o protest against conscrip- tion will be Mr. C. Jackson Holmes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>Buried Under Limestone.</head>
        <p>Buried Under Limestone.</p>
        <p>Whilst working in Messrs. Crosfield's yard on Thursday, William Bloomfield, 2, Cherley- street, had a miraculous escape from death. He was buried under a quantity of limestone, and on removal to the Infirmary it was found there was a fracture of the femur. Last night he was progressing favourably.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>Insurance Gill Workers.</head>
        <p>Insurance Gill Workers.</p>
        <p>The question of the ;ages paid to the girls at the local Insurance Office was raisedna e rai to yog on Wednesday. Mr. Penketh at the Insurance Corn. Wednesday. The Clerk (Mr. Tilting) said the wages averaged 228. per week. They were paid piece rates at Pd. Per 100 earde.-31r. Broeklehurst : Of course they are experienced at the work.—This answer satisfied the ques- tioner.</p>
        <p>Warrington Doc-tors Receive £6,968. The 18 panel doctors in Warrington, it was reported at the Insurance Committee on Wed- nesday. have received under the Insurance Act £6,968 Is. 9d. as fees during the year, an average. of £387 2s. 3d. One doctor received £1,007 3s. Id., another 4898 199.9(1., a third £751 75.6 d. a fourth £603 4s. 2d., and another £556 19s. 3d Only one received less than £2OO. The chemists' accounts amounted to £2,108 is. 7d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>Municipal Trading.</head>
        <p>Municipal Trading.</p>
        <p>The question of Municipal Trading was dis- cussed at Thursday night's meeting of the War- rington Debating Society, when Mr. Charles McNair, of the Anti-Socialist Union of Great Britain. presented the case against such trad- ing. He stated that every. trading concern which had been taken over by municipal authorities had been a failure, and showed how private companies in other countries made huge nrofits end eswe enormous sums to the local authorities without the latter investing .a single penny in capital. Mr. C. Dukes opposed the views of Mr. McNair and said that what he had given were examples of municipal mismanage- ment. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>Young Ladles Ghe a Concert.</head>
        <p>Young Ladles Ghe a Concert.</p>
        <p>Through the energies of the members of the Young Ladies' Bible Class connected with the Working Men's Mission, a most enjoyable con- certwas wgaivseneeoa contributed b on night. The pro- grammethe Misses B. Hough, L. Jones, Hardcastle, E. Tanner, O. Dale, N. Mace, and A. Thomas, Mrs. Winstan- ley, and Messrs. Starkey, A. Dale, Arthur Webb ted and T. Tanner. "That Piece of Silk," a dia- logue presented by members of the class, was very good and did credit to the following, who took part: Misses L. Kendrick, G. Morris, H Thomson, C. Whitby, and M. Thomas. The interest in the programme was augmen by the introduction of lantern slides to accompany some of the songs. Mr. S. Rae was an efficient e ammo- lanternist, and Miss Oakley and Messrs. F. Atkinson T. Tanner were eaPabl pa</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>Royal Liver Friendly Society : warrington District.</head>
        <p>Royal Liver Friendly Society : warrington District.</p>
        <p>Kilduff, sat down to The anntuad eoeial of the.etaft, HI :- 9.4- wives and friends of the above twat'. took place „ Thareday erining et Meiont A.tkinson'e Cafe, fcto° Mr. anexdcelllrentifikrild de and fork about 50 to 60, including the. District Delegate dtilea.ondjullstisTihcwice manager (Mr. P. ?Delaney), en pro posing the toast to the society, thought the officials were more strongly united than anY i similar body. During the ntervals songs, &amp;v., were render i ed n fine style by the Misses Caldwell and Delaney, Mrs. Hey- wood, Mr. Kilduff, and Mr. P. Delaney, jun, Humorous items were given by Messrs. Kilduff and R. Delaney. Mrs. Butler was the aCCO3II- - Mr. Swarbrick and Mr. Delaney actin. as the M.C.'s. Mr, Swarbrtek, the genial secre- tary, tary, was congratulated upon the excellent provision lie had ina,de for the success of the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>Preachers for _To-morrow.</head>
        <p>Preachers for _To-morrow.</p>
        <p>- - -• Cairo-street Chapel—Morning and • eyenin Dr. Mellor. . g' Legh-street Primitive , 'Methodist—Morning Rev. A. J. Chessum; evening, Mr. G. Faulkner. Bewsey-road Wesleyan--Morning, Mr. C. T. Fielding; evening, Mr. JAL Dixon. ' Bold-street Wesleyan-310rn1ng and evening, Mr. R. M. Cowley. , St. John's Presbyterian—Morning and even- ing—Rev. W. Reid. Christadelphians, Toft's Assembly Rooms— Evening, Mr. T. Riley, Liverpool. St. Paul's Church—Mission services, &amp;.(1 , 8, 10.30, 2.15; 3.15, 4.15, 6.30, B—Rev. Rw. . :30d- Samuel, special missioner. Wycliffe Congregational—Morning, Mr. .T. W. Anderson, Lancashire College; evening, Rev. T. T. James. Latchford Baptist—Morning and evejnoinrne.gs,.sl:: 1y c S. Chedbutn. e G ol h ojronne,e-sstreetevening, B m Baptist—Morning, tis tr—. NEI.o rLnlionygd, li , Rev. W ma. pnu.e JlayCehurch—Morning and evening, Rv</p>
        <p>St. Paul's Great Mission. To-day is the eve of a great spiritual revival in the St. Paul's parish, for the Rev. Williamßracecamp, desirous of a forward movement in his district, has secured the services of the Rev. h. Wood-Samuel, who to-night will launch upon a week's crusade for the cause of righteousness and truth. The Missioner can claim a connec- tion with the parish, for some years ago he laboured at the church. Since those days he ' has been very successful, and, as a Royal chap- lain, he comes once more among the poor in the scenes of his former labours. his visit will i services in be an ad- dress be a very busy one indeed, for on Sunday alone twelve will be noAmfe Amongst tt htah4e.s e sevenw dress to men only, when his subject will be "The Ruin of a Great Man." The full list of services will be found in our advertising columns. The Vicar has made strenuous efforts to make the mission a success, and, with a large band of eager workers, every house in the parish has been visited. It is now ten years since such an effort•has been attempted in this parish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>Labour in Continuous Industries.</head>
        <p>Labour in Continuous Industries.</p>
        <p>Demand in the Universal Interest of Industrial Democracy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>Twelve Hours at Blast Furnaces a Scandal to Christendom.</head>
        <p>Twelve Hours at Blast Furnaces a Scandal to Christendom.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield, who is a very active member of the International Association for Labour Legislation, and takes the keenest interest in the association's effort to bring about a three-shift day in place of the two- shift day in such continuous industries as the iron and steel trade, has written a letter to the " Morning Post," bitterly complaining of the apparent indifference of Mr. McKenna, the Home Secretary.</p>
        <p>While complaining, Mr. Crosfield takes the opportunity of pointing out the aims and objects of the association, and the methods by which these aims might readily be accom- plished. For the benefit of our readers we quote the letter in full:—</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln a previous letter I had occasion to criticise the Home Office and the Home Secre- tary for doing their best to hinder instead of help the British Section of the International Association for Labour Legislation in the efforts which it is making to improve the standarj oi hours and conditions of work for women, and for boys in their teens. There is yet another case, one of Paramovnt and suprenn importance to every industrial nation, in Wicli I am sorry to say that soi far the /Tome Secretary has utterly failed in his duty. I refer to the question of hours of labour in continnons in- dustries. These continuous industries cover all : those varieties of manufacture where nerk is carried on night and day, whether or not such work be also continued through the week-end, and therefore without intermission month after month. Broadly speaking, it is still—the general rule throughout industrial Europe and America that where manufacture is carried on night and day two shifts of men only should be employed. The system by .whic/t. instead of that, three shifts of men, each working eight hours, are employed, is, it is true, miik:ng cen_ stant and certain progress, but it still remains .the exception and not the rule. Cur vsso- ciation aims at making it the universal rule. For technical reasons a somewhat different arrangement of daily shifts may here and titers be found preferable, but where that is the case it should not in any case increase the average hours worked per week beyond those worked by men engaged on the eight-hour shift.</p>
        <p>engaged at the blast furnaces In this industry some tens of thousands of men whose hours of work average 84 per week.</p>
        <p>BRUTALISING, DESTRUCTIVE, AND RUINOUS.</p>
        <p>The last case mentioned, that of the blast furnaces, is, taking into account both the nature of the work and the length of the hours, the worst case of all, and it is impossible to condemn too strongly a system brutalising to the men who have to endure it, destructive of family life and home life, and therefore as ruinous from the standpoint of the true inter- ests of the State as it is infamously unjust and cruel to the individual. And it is quite un- necessary. Half the men working at our blast furnaces in England now have the eight-hour shift, and I am assured there is ilot a single employer who has adopted that reform who would wish to go back to the discredited, re- ' actionary, pernicious, and altogether abomin- able twelve-hour shift. Why :should not the other half of the men engaged in the same country and in the same industry have the benefit of the same change? Why not, indeed? . There is no answer; no excuse for maintaining the old system. The evil continues partly be- cause of the timidity of employers•of labour, who are afraid of giving their competitors an advantage by making a change of which they fail to appreciate (through ignorance or thoughtlessness) the direct and indirect advan- tages; partly owing to the influence of mere use and wont and UNREASONING CUSTOM, that most persistent enemy everywhere of progress. There is not wanting in England evidence that this reform can be introduced without anything beyond temporary sacrifice to the employer. But SUPPOSING that in our country and in other countries the adoption of the eight-hour shift WOULD mean an INCREASED COST OF PRODUCTION, would that fact be the slightest justification or .:xcuse for permitting the continuance of such •tn industrial scandal and plague spot in our midst as the twelve-hour shift .for workers at the blast furnaces constitutes? There would not be vestige of excuse for it. The only pretext which employers in that industry might urge —foreign competition—vanishes when it is retognised that by means of an International Convention this abuse can be swept away, not in one country or another country alone, but in all .the leading countries competing with each other for the world's markets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>CONCENTRATION UPON THE IRON AND STEEL TRADE.</head>
        <p>CONCENTRATION UPON THE IRON AND STEEL TRADE.</p>
        <p>With that object in view a conference was held in London on June 11 and 12, 1912, attended by representatives from fourteen industrial nations. At that conference it was proposed by representatives of the British section of the association that in order to keep our reform within manageable limits, and at the same time with view to studying the convenience of our own Home Office and of corresponding Depart- ments in foreign Ministries, we should in the first instance concentrate our energies upon the iron and steel trade of Europe and the United States of America and our own Colonies, and work for the adoption of the eight-hour shift as a substitute for the twelve-hour shift for all the shift workers in that industry. A further reason for taking this course is the fact that no industry in the world presents a stronger case for far-reaching reform in reference to hours of work than does this mighty industry of iron and steel. Ilere the case for reform in favour of the shift a7orkers is absolutely overwhelming, in fact no rational being could make out any sort of a ease against it.</p>
        <p>In many eases .one sees nowadays agitatiunS tiv man whose hours of work are something in the neigkbourhood of 60 per week for a reduc- tion to etomething aearer 50: or those who are working just over 59 claim a working week of aomething a little under 50. here the demand is for a reduction from as horn's to 53; there, Instead of 53 hours, men are agitating for 43. But, taking the world's iron t.nd steel trade as a whole, one finds that, apart from some periods of rest at meal times, the enormous majority of men are working 72 hours a week, or something in that neighbourhood, while, incredible as it may seem, it is none the less the fact and a scandal to all Christendom that there are still</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>TARIFFREFORK TRADE UNIONISTS</head>
        <p>TARIFFREFORK TRADE UNIONISTS</p>
        <p>• Another Attempted Tory Make-</p>
        <p>Believe.</p>
        <p>SAME OLD DEVICE.</p>
        <p>It was recently our duty to explain that a few soi-disaut Liberals, who accompanied to Belfast a number of the most pronounced Tories that Warrington can produce, were not entitled to say what Liberals think on the Home Rule question. The scene has changed, and we have now to take in hand a scheme for making it appear that trade unions favour Tariff Reform. As Tariff Reform would annihilate trade, it would be something at which we could marvel to see the unions supporting it. But we can rest assured that working men are not willing to commit industrial suicide, and that the few who have undertaken to speak in their name have about as much right to do so as "the six" had to speak for Liberalism upon Home Rule. There are some Tory trade unionists, but when they support Tariff Reform they speak as Tories —some of them are of the street corner tub- thumping type—and they do not speak as trade unionists.</p>
        <p>Trade Unionists and Free Trade.</p>
        <p>This week we have received a batch of circu- lars and resolutions from Mr. Ashton Bagley. the secretary of the Lancashire, Cheshire and North-West Counties Division and the York- shire Districts of the Tariff Reform League. which are a direct result of the December Con- ference in Manchester, at which it was claimed that some 80 of the chief trade unions of the country had sent 300 representatives to vote against Free Trade as being diametrically .op- posed to the interests of British trade union- ists. At that conference, the "Daily News" points out, certain delegates were presented to the president as representative trade unionists, and they were given seats of prominence and honour on the platform, Since then, however, protests have been made I by various unions against the use of their name in connection with the matter. Despite this, I?tr. Ashton Bagley has sent out a circular giv- ing the names of 78 trade unions and trade pocieties whose members unanimously passed 'resolutions which, he proceeds, "should be suffi-</p>
        <p>cient to convince all. but the most prejudiced that the Parliamentary Labour party are iiot truly representing the trade union movement when they support the system of national black legging known as Free Trade." On the strength of this the Tariff Reform League offers to send their speakers to any trade union meeting to "demonstrate in speech or debate" that Free Trade and the true interests of the worker are deadly opposed. Repudiations. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The "Daily News" of Wednesday says:— The Carters Union is one of those in the list. Yesterday Mr. Hilton, the general secretary of the union, indignantly denied that his union had ever authorised anyone to pose as their representative at a Tariff Reform conference. "We have members," he said, "who may be Tariff Reformers—you cannot have 15,000 mem- bers all thinking alike—but as a union we should be dead up against Tariff Reform." The Oldham Operative Spinners and the Bol- ton Operative Spinners are also in the list, and their officials authorise similar disclaimers. The inclusion of the Bolton Operative Spinners is e peculiarly interesting, because Mr. A. H. Gill, M.P. one of its chief officials, and Mr. John Battle, the president, are among th keenest Free Traders in Bolton, whilst it is estimated that at least two-thirds of the members are also convinced Free Traders. The National Union of Journalists Published a denial at the time of the conference, and it may be mentioned that all the leaders of the miners' unions which are included are strong Free Traders.</p>
        <p>Local Attempts.</p>
        <p>Several attempts , have been made locally to make it appear that Trade Unionists favour Tariff Reform, but as the champions of this kind are exceedingly few and show no signs of in- crease, those attempts have never succeeded.</p>
        <p>£5O for the Guardians. The Warrington Guardians have got an arnymous gift of a cheque for £5O. This was sent through Mr. T. Knowles, 6, Egypt-street, who wrote : "Dear Sir,—A client of mine, who wishes to remain anonymous, has instructed me to offer the Guardians the sum of £5O as a gift to be applied to the funds of the Union. I may say in recognition of the fact that my' client had a relative who for a considerable time was a charge on your Union."—Mr. McKinley: Is she or he going to keep it up, or is it only this one donation? We should say we are very glad to have it. (Rear, hear.)—The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: It is a matter which ought to be made well known.—Mr. McKinley: It is anonymous, and that is the worst of it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>"PETRIFIED OPPOSITION."</head>
        <p>"PETRIFIED OPPOSITION."</p>
        <p>The International Association has already in two cases of the greatest importance achieved striking success on these lines, and it is now working on precisely the same lines for this reform of world-wide importance—the introduc- tiort of the eight-hour shift for every shift worker in every iron and steel works of each of those countries which, industrially speaking, take a lead in this great department of human activity.</p>
        <p>Thei+ is no reason to doubt the response that would be given if England gave a lead in this matter. It is for England to do it, because it is England, and England alone, which has already proved on a large scale the manifold advantages —commercial and industrial, moral and social— which are the certain result of this wise and just reform. Therefore members of the British Section of the International Association ap- proached the Home Secretary more than a year ago and asked him to take the matter up. I am sorry to say, however, that although we have communicated time and again with the Home Secretary on this all-important question, we have so far met with, at the Home Office, only the barren, obdurate, unreasoning, fossilised, and petrified opposition of a stone wall. No valid excuse for, or intelligible explanation of, I this persistent inaction has been giver/ us, nor has any single objection to the course we recom- mend been pointed out. The exigencies of\ official life no doubt have to be borne in mind. Cabinet Ministers have many demands upon their time, and it may be, and I would fain hope it is, the fact, that Mr. McKenna has not yet grasped, either from the national or the international point of view, the full gjgnifieance, and importance of this reform. But now the time has come when we bold that we are en- titled to request his serious attention and prac- tical assistance in the prosecution of our work, I and we feel also justified In appealing to public opinion in the confident hope that we shall receive a strong and wholehearted backing for the demand we have made, and which we now emphatically repeat in the universal interest of industrial democracy.—Yours. A. H. CROSFIELD. February 10th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>HOME RULE FOR IRELAND</head>
        <p>HOME RULE FOR IRELAND</p>
        <p>Mr. England's Muddled Conception of Liberal and Free Trade Principles.</p>
        <p>WHAT ALTERNATIVE TO THE BILL?</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner." Sir,—A curious habit characterises almost everyone who secedes from any political party. The seceder tries to persuade himself and others that it is the party which has changed, not him- self. How often have those who ha7e left the Liberal party said, "It is not I who have left the- party, it is the party that has left me; it is the Liberals who have abandoned their prin- 3iples !" Mr. Ebenezer England has followed this general habit. He tells us "that the Liberal Gov- umment has scattered to the winds some of the great Liberal principles." In his attempts to justify this statement he is singularly unfor. tunate. He first names an instance which has no connection with any Liberal principle, but is solely a question of the logical symmetry of the Imperial Parliament; and second, he in- stances a section of the Home Rule Bill, which is strictly in harmony with Free Trade prin- ciples.</p>
        <p>Liberal Principles Intact. The point first used in his attempt to prove a desertion of Liberal principles is stated thus:— "Forty-two Irish members under the Bill are to sit in Westminster and legislate on English, Scotch and Welsh Bills, and yet at the same time the House of Commons will hare no power to legislate on Irish affairs." By Irish affairs he means Irish local and not Imperial interests, and he characterises this as a _"ridiculous privilege." Let us get down to facts, for no Liberal principle is involved. Scotch, Eng- lish and Irish legislators rightly complain that they cannot at present get opportunity properly to discuss their local affairs, because of the con- gestion at Westminster. Every one of these, I mean those who want progress, will be greatly relieved to get Irish local affairs out of the way, so that more time can be given to English and Scotch interestg. Instead, therefore, of it being a grievance, it will be a great blessing to have this extra time. The probability is that in the future schemes for local and subordinate Par- liaments will be evolved for the other sections of the kingdom, but until the sections are ripe for the extension and show it as Ireland has done, it is well to deal with Ireland, which is ripe, and whose request has been too long delayed. Further, as long as Westminster Parliament is the Imperial Parliament, it is essential that Ireland should be represented. • The point Mr. England tries to make against the consistency of the Government in connec- tion ivith the principle of Free Trade is stated is follows:—" The Free Trade Government under this Bill allows the Irish Parliament to ' legislate and fix duties on manufactured goods imported into Ireland."</p>
        <p>To the ordinary reader manufactured goods would include those entering Ireland duty free. There is no such power given in the Bill. All it does is to give power to the Irish Parliament to vary the custom duties on those articles already taxed for revenue purposes on entrance at the ports, such as tobacco, spirits, &amp;c. If this is done a corresponding variation has to be made in the Excise duties on these goods manu- factured in Ireland, so that no preference is given to the Irish manufacturer. If, in the de- velopment of the country, more funds 're re- quired, or less are needed, the Irish Parliament ought to have the power to vary taxation, but not to give to her manufacturers any preferen- tial treatment; this the Bill makes clear.</p>
        <p>There is no power under this Bill to 'evy duties for Protection, and hence no deviation from Free Trade principles. Mr. England is either not so ingenuous es be appears, or he has a very muddled conception of what Free Trade principles are. I think ihe latter is his trouble. A Small Change Controversy. But is this controversy not getting down to very small change? The deputation came back with a report with the flaring flourish that to force this measure into law was a crime. Now its members appear to be seeking for arguments in the quiet waters of custom duties on whisky and " baccy."</p>
        <p>Mr. England says, " The people in the North, despite the greater difficulties which have been experienced than by the men in the South, have left them far behind in most of the arts and manufactures of civilisation." Will Mr. Eng- land kindly give us proof in respect to the greater. difficulties. It seems to me he is either reading history upside down, or is himself standing on his head.</p>
        <p>In regard to the question of majority rule, I quite agree "reason" must prevail. But on what ground has Mr. England the right tw claim far the Vlet-er minority, even when backet? by the Warrington deputetion, including him- self, a mo-nopely of reason? When botli parties show that they have reason on theishaide and cannet agree, upon what principle other thttn that el numbers are you to determine which view shall prevail? Mr. England's anahogy about the Irish majority and its application to the majority who cried out for Barrabas and sent Christ to the Cross is 'not particularly courteous to myself as a "Home Ruler." Ide not mind this, but I should like Mr. England to tell us clearly how it applies. The most significant feature in Mr. England' letter is the entire omission of any response tok my appeal, specially made to the Liberal mem- bers of the deputation, to state what is their alternative to the present Home Rule Bill. This concentration on N.E. Ulster is liable to throw the larger Irish question entirely out of focus. I would repeat my appeal, this time specially to Mr. England. What is his alterna- tive? Misleading talk about customs, futile attempts to prove that a Free Trade Govern- ment is Protectionist, a successful effort to show that by relieving Parliament of Irish local affairs you benefit the other sections, but at the same time produce conditions not strictly logical —all this is paltry. We admit the ques- tion is difficult—very difficult—but if the Ulster party, backed up by the Warrington Liberal seceders, managed to defeat the Bill, the Irish - question would still hold the field and demand .a settlement. What does Mr. England propose? CHARLEg E. PARKER. February 9, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>IRISH DIFFICULTY.</head>
        <p>THE IRISH DIFFICULTY.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of 'The Examiner." Sir,—lt is a very lame cause that requires to be bolstered up by appeals to the penal laws and to write of Protestants and Romanists as top and bottom dogs; to take out of their setting cir.! cumstances of between one and two hundred years ago, and hold them up to infamy, to justify- present-day events. What has Mr. C. E. Parker to say to the last 114 years of British rule in Ireland? The nine- teenth century opened with the abolition of the Parliament of the Protestant Pale, to be fol- lowed by the passing of Catholic emancipation, and the numerous other ameliorating measures that have been continued to the present time. These measures have made a new Ireland, and many Irishmen are thankful for the change, but there seems a remnant whom nothing will satisfy. The greatest thing the Ireland of to- day needs is peace and freedom from intimida- tion, for \where uncertainty exists capital will not go. The South and West of Ireland need manufacturers and capital, and neither of these will go without security. The capitalist is on the look-out for cheap labour, but he will not go where he is likely to be intimidated or his works destroyed. You will not bring peace to Ireland if, after you abolished a Parlia- ment over one hundred years ago because it was Protestant, you now create one that will be pre- eminently Romanist. The argument of Mr. C. E. Parker would be quite puerile in an ordinary person, but, in his case, I should say it is criminal thus to mislead a party, of which lie is the local head. / PHIL NIIOD.</p>
      </div>
