      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>GUARDIANS' WARNING.</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS' WARNING.</p>
        <p>Hint to People Who Take Care of Children from Other Folk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>PROSECUTIONS THREATENED.</head>
        <p>PROSECUTIONS THREATENED.</p>
        <p>Persons having the custody of a child other than their own under the age of seven years, must, by order of the Children's Act, give notice to the Guardians at once, or they will be prosecuted. We were requested at the Guar- dians' meeting on Wednesday to make this fact public. The Guardians have done what they could to acquaint the public with the Act, but still there are some who plead ignorance of the taw, which is no excuse. One person has been prosecuted, and another case came before the meeting on Wednesday, and the Clerk asked for instructions. Mr. Garnett: I think we decided that we should prosecute in the next case that came before us. Why should we break away from that decision? The Clerk: If Mr. Garnett would only re- member what the Guardians do he would not make that statement. I brought a -CaSe Wore you and we prosecuted. (Hear, hear.) Mr. McKinley moved that the fact be adver- tised by circular. There had been a prosecu- tion, but he did not think they should repeat it in that case. The Chairman (Mr. E. L. Heat' : he people don't know. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: Mr. Girn3tt means we should follow up our decisi.m. The Clerk: If you want to prosente you can, instruct me.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>ALWAYS HAVE THESE CASES.</head>
        <p>ALWAYS HAVE THESE CASES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lee: You will always have these cases.' Even respectable and educated people offend. Mr. Garnett: The more respectable and edu- cated the more they ought to know. Prosecu- tion is the best way to advertise. It was mentioned that people could get to know by reading the newspapers. Mrs. Simpson: Some women are too busy to read the papers, and some husbands do not communicate anything to their wives. The Clerk: We have a serious duty to pert form in carrying out the Act. You must not let them ride slipshod over the Act. The hoardings have been placarded. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>• The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: Have they the same difficulty in other Unions? The Clerk; I am only the Clerk to the War- rington Union.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>"A KIND OF BISHOP."</head>
        <p>"A KIND OF BISHOP."</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: You are a kind of bishop, you know. (Laughter.) &gt;</p>
        <p>Mr: McKinley: A diocesan' bishop.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mather: I suggest that you get a notice with the heading: "Grand Football Match !" and then follow on with the particulars. People would read it then.</p>
        <p>Mr. Furness: The Board have spent a consid- erable amount of money on advertising the Act.- If you are going to differentiate between one case and another you are going to create a condition of things not to be desired by a Board of this standing. Mr. Brooks: Could we not have a handbill to be given by the Registrar to people who register births? The Clerk: We capnot call on the Registrars. Mr. McKinley: There will be no leniency in the next case.</p>
        <p>Mr. Furness: The Warrington magistrates are very merciful.</p>
        <p>It was decided not to prosecute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>New Workhouse Master.</head>
        <p>New Workhouse Master.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Loxham, Master and Matron at the Barton Workhouse, Patricroft, were ap- pointed to similar positions in the Warrington Institution on Wednesday. Mr. Loxham has had 13 years' experience of Poor Law work, starting at the Fulwood Workhouse, Preston, as imbecile attendant and then as storekeeper. He went to Toxteth Park, Liverpool, as store- keeper, and to Derby as assistant master. He then went to Haslingden as master and from there to Patricroft. Mrs. Loxham was ap- pointed superintendent laundress at Dewsbury and as assistant matron at Preston. She was the matron at Haslingden and Barton.</p>
        <p>LINIIINi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>.:,.01</head>
        <p>.:,.01</p>
        <p>STeeKTON IFIEPITH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>TOWNSHIP TITTLE-TATTLE.</head>
        <p>TOWNSHIP TITTLE-TATTLE.</p>
        <p>'The-firenien are smiling. • The cause is due to the fact That the Warrington Brigade were called To a "fire" which was but a Reflection seen in a shop window. It seems that it is not only the "Heathen" fire-fighters who have been - Deceived by reflections instead of fire. There is one licensed house in the parish For every 500 odd persons in the Heath. If all went at once—what a cram. Motor cyclists tore through the parish During Saturday afternoon; the cause was A race of some 75 miles—a mere spin. One lady cyclist, done up in mere man's Togs, very nearly gave the donee "Heathens" a fit. She was one of the many competitors. Licensed victuallers must now keep books. This is the edict of the licensing justices. Some of the thirsty ones fear that the amount They consume will be recorded in the books. One, however, thinks the idea is good, and His reason for so 'thinking is that a book Is not quite so public as the old slate. Primroses have been found blooming in A "Heathen's" garden, and the larks have Commenced to sing—Spring is truly coming. The Women Liberals are very active members - And do things in thorough good style. This was evident at their party this week. The nipper had been sent to the chemist To get ts-opennyworth of .some chemical. En route he forgot the name of the stuff, so He quietly solved the problem by asking The chemist "to smell the bottle an' give Me tuppence worth of what was in it." It is not on record whether he got it.</p>
        <p>A Sewerage Scheme.—At the meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday it was stated that the Walton Inferior sewage scheme was advancing and that progress was being made with the work.</p>
        <p>Assessable Value.—The Cheshire County Council propose to increase the assessable ,value of Stockton Heath by £582. The colseisabl value of land is £l2O, and that of buildings, Sze., £17,086. The increase proposed in Walton In- ferior is £966, and £1,69 i Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>No Address—At the Stockton Heath Petty Sessions an Tuesday, Christian Bower, 430, inutstord-road, was ordered to pay the sum- mons fee for not having his address on a cart.— Defendant said he thought the name was suffi- cient, as he did not use the cart much.</p>
        <p>Main Road Question.—lt was reported at the Cheshire County Council meeting on Thursday that the Bridgewater Trustees had offered a piece of land adjoining the main road, Walton, for £34, to be either used for widening the road or for a stone depot. The matter was left to a sub-committee for consideration.</p>
        <p>Young Worshippers' League.—A praiseworthy effort is being made by the Stockton Heath Wesleyans to get the young people interested in the work of the church, and for this purpose a Young Worshippers' League has been formed. Already 50 members have been secured, and Mr. G. A. Whitfield, secretary, is hopeful of greatly augmenting that number.</p>
        <p>Liberal Auditor.—At a meeting of the Women's Liberal Association, held in the Provi- dence School, Chapel-lane, on Thursday night— Mrs. Henry Roberts presiding—it was unani- mously agreed that Mr. Brayshaw be appointed auditor to the association. It was reported that the association was increasing in its member- ship and was forging ahead.</p>
        <p>• Tail, Light Tale.—Francis Cecil Brown, taxi driver, 7, Hart's-Place, Bridge-street, Warring- ton, was summoned for not having a red light at the rear of a taxicab on January 28, and also with not having had a light at the rear of his taxicab on February sth.—Mr. E. A. Connor said that in the first instance the rough state of the road had caused the light to go out, and in, the second case the defendant did not know hut what his light was in. The defendant had not to see to the lamps, as that was the duty of the lampmen before the drivers came out.— Defendant was ordered to pay the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>LICENSING SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>LICENSING SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Magistrates Give a Hint to the Licence-</p>
        <p>holders.</p>
        <p>At the Stockton Heath Licensing Sessions on Tuesday, Supt. Owen presented his annual re- port, in which he said that in the district there were 20 licensed victuallers and three beer- sellers. During the year no licensee had been protteeded against. Twenty-nine persons, all males, had been proceeded against for drunken- ness, and there were 28 convicted, as compared with 11 persons convicted the previous year. Thirteen were residents and 16 were strangers. The Chairman said they had been requested by the Secretary of State to point out the lack of business method on the part of licence- holders, and they took that opportunity of drawing the attention of licensees to the neces- sity of keeping proper hooks, and such a neces- sity would be regarded as an essential item in the proper management of licensed premises. It was announced that the adjourned annual licensing meeting would be held in the Guild Room on March 10th at 11 a.m.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>HOVELS AS HOUSES.</head>
        <p>HOVELS AS HOUSES.</p>
        <p>A , Woman Liberal •Tells of What She Discovered iii the Coontry. • -</p>
        <p>AN INSPIRING LIBERAL CATHERINE.. A thrilling story of the conditions under which the land workers have to live and about the hovels in which they have to reside was narrated at the meeting following the annual tea of the Stockton Heath Women's Liberal Association, held on Thursday evening in the Providence School, Chapel-lane, by Miss Shaw, of London. Mr. P. M. Oliver also attended and spoke on the new Home Rule situation. Mrs. Henry Roberts, the president, was in the chair, and was supported by Mr. P. M. Oliver and Miss Shaw, London. In initiating the con- cert Mrs. Roberts expressed delight at seeing such a large gathering. It was really very re- freshing and did all good. (Hear, hear.) It was the third visit Miss Shaw had paid to Stockton Heath, and Mr. Oliver they were pleased to have often amongst them. (Ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>MR. OLIVER'S SPEECH:</head>
        <p>MR. OLIVER'S SPEECH:</p>
        <p>Mr. OLIVER, who was most cordially received, paid a flattering tribute to the work the Liberal women were doing in Stockton Heath. Some women said they would not work for Liberal- ism until they got the vote. They were not like that in Stockton Heath. They should stay in the Liberal Associations and help to attain the other great reforms coming. (Applause.) In I the hungry "forties" and the " sixties" it was the voteless men who stood respectively by Cob- den to secure the repeal of the Corn Laws, and John Bright to prevent war with America, which the vote-holding class were anxious for— a war that would have been a crime upon humanity. Thus the women should adopt that example and remain with their Liberal Associa- tions. and assist to carry other great reforms. (Applause.) You, as a Women's Liberal Association, said Mr. Oliver, are, perhaps, not so interested in the question of Ireland as the Conservative women. There is no danger of your homes being overcrowded with Ulster widows and babies— (laughter)—nevertheless Ireland is the outstand- ing feature in the political arena, and we ought to give a thought to what has been going on in the House of Commons within the past few days.</p>
        <p>Suggested Co-operation. In the King's Speech there was an anxious appeal to everybody concerned to co-operate to settle this tzisll question. Whether we shall get that co-operation from the Conservative party, I do not know. We have tried our best in the past, and we have asked them to make sugges- tions, and no suggestion came from them. In- stead they have continued to talk about civil war. Every speech since has bristled with bayonets and the ,roar of cannonalias rumbled throngh their pre-orations. There are some who say, "You ought to ex- clude Ulster from the Bill." That seems a very easy way to settle the question, but the exclu- sion of Ulster would not settle anything. If you put away all the froth and bubbles we hear in Conservative speeches, and get down to the surface, what do you find?—the divided opinion and bitterness between one Ulsterman and his neighbour. In the whole of Ulster you will find 46 out of every 100 persons are in favour of Home Rule, and if you take the four homo- geneous counties you will find 30 out of the 100 are in favour of Home Rule, and between these two classes there is the greatest bitternesS. If you excluded Ulster that bitterness would re- main.</p>
        <p>Remedy for Bitterness. We believe the only remedy for that bitter- ness is to give Ulster Home Rule, and we be- lieve that for the simple reason that when you have religious bitterness of that character you can only get rid of it by turtling men's minds away from the question of HOME, Rule or anti- Home Rule, and giving them something else, healthier and saner, to think about. We want, to give Ulster a healthy national feeling, and we believe that is the only way this religious bigotry and bitterness can be killed. (Applause.) It had been stated that the 1909 Budget had proved a failure, and the Conservatives said that after that the land reform would be a failure. Was it a failure, when last year 25 millions were raised in inew taxatiop, chiefly from thti luxuries of the rich? In every .2 the income tax gave 6s. loa., estate duty ss. 5d., spirits, liquor and tobacco ss. 5d., stamp dues is. lid., motor spirit and licences 11d., and land duty 4d.—these taxes were levied on the luxuries of the wealthy. Mr. Lloyd George used these taxes for old-age pensions, insurance, Labour Exchanges, national development, agriculture, main roads Cheshire in two years had got .i. 626,000 toivards the upkeep of the main roads, and thus saved the rates—and money for the navy. Did that show the 1909 Budget to be a failure? (" No, and applause.)</p>
        <p>HOVELS FOR THE WORKERS. A Woman Liberal's Discoveries. ... .</p>
        <p>_ . Miss SHAW, in a talk from the woman's view- point of politics, gave interesting facts of her work among women. .</p>
        <p>_ You should see some of the hovels I found in ,the rural districts, she said, houses unfit for people to live in. I know people working six whole days a week for 125., and these have come to appreciate the old-age pensions, as they are saved paying for the upkeep of their aged par- ents out of their small wages. They have come to bless the Act. Many people on th'e land are living under ter- rible conditions. I have 'found hundreds living in the worst of hovels. In one house in a certain county I mind the inmate, an aged lady, sit- ting before the fire with an umbrella over her to keep off the rain coming through the roof. (Voice: "Shame.") I asked her why she did not see the landlord about the state of the roof. She replied that he had come and looked at the roof and said : " Surely, Mrs. —, you don't object to a little fresh air and ventilation." (" Shame.") Later the landlord said he would make the roof rainproof, but she would have to pay is. a week More in rent for the repairs to the hovel. (".Shatrie.") She was already paying 3s. 9d. a week, and would have to pay 4s. f)d., or be turned out, and if she was turned out, she could not get another house. - - Should Save on 121.</p>
        <p>--••--- I have heard Tories say that farm servants</p>
        <p>, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 14, 1914.</p>
        <p>earning 12s. per week should be able to live on that and save money., (Laughter.) There are 30,000 adults earning less than 16s. a week, and 60 per cent. less than 18s. a week, yet it costs £1 per head per week to'keeP people in the work- houses. If so, how can a family live on 165.? They do not live, they merely exist. (Applause.) We have got to clear the hovels away and to give the people on the land a living wage and good houses, and that is what the Liberals mean to do. (Loud applause.) There was an excellent 'musical Programme provided, and the efforts of the artistes were thoroughly appreciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
