      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>RIXTON.</head>
        <p>RIXTON.</p>
        <p>Social and Dance.—A successful concert, whist drive and dance was held in the Rixton Mission I Room on Saturday, in aid of the Tempest Hero 'Society of Oddfeltows. An.,eotcellent prOgramme was submitted by Messrs? Sutton, G. Higham, Kenyon, Hindley and Robinson, and Mrs. G. Higham, Miss Jackson and Mr. H. Atkinson acting as accompanists, whilst for the dancing Miss Atherton was responsible for the music. The winners of the whist were :—Ladies: Mr. W. James (playing is a " lady ") and Miss L Gibbons; gentlemen—Messrs. W. Rate and E Codlin. Mr. J. R. Evans was M.C. for the dancing, and Mr. R. Pye for the whist. Praise is due to Mr. and Mrs. G. Higham for the admir- able arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>DARESBURY.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY.</p>
        <p>"The Bells."—On Sunday next Daresbui7 Church will once more resound with pealing bells, the work of hanging the new bells now being completed. At the morning service the Rev. J. Binney, Canon of Chester, will dedicate the new bells. In the afternoon the ringers will ring until evensong 4,000 changes. The Vicar, in announcing the occasion, hoped as many as possible would put in an appearance on this historic occasion, and that the parish- ioners will respond in goodly numbers to the message of the bells.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>PEN SETH.</head>
        <p>PEN SETH.</p>
        <p>Britain in Africa.—An interesting, illustrated lecture on "Britain's burden in the Dark Con- tinent" was delivered at the Friends School on Monday evening by Mr. J. Burtt, of Mat- lock. The Headmaster, Mr. W. E. Brown, pre- sided. The lecturer dealt with the queer cii&amp;, toms, mostly religidus, of the natives of Africa, and he impressed upon the audience the need of Britain doing more to bring enlightenment amongst the black denizens of the African Continent. The lantern, which showed re- markably fine views, was admirably operated by Messrs. G. J. Baker and T. H. Brunyee, assistant masters at the school. To these and the lecturer, Miss Ethel Eckersley proposed a vote of thanks. This was seconded by Mr. R. Walton and carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>OR FORD.</head>
        <p>OR FORD.</p>
        <p>Old Resident's Death.—Mrs. C. Povey, wife of ' Mr. Charles Povey, Long-row, has died there 'at the age of 75 years. She ranked as the second oldest surviving native of Orford, and was born a short distance away from the house in which she died. All her life had been spent in Orford, and she very rarely took a holiday away from the district. rive years ago she celebrated her golden wedding and is survived by her husband, a hale gentleman of 80 years. Mr. Povey only retired from work siic years ago, after having been connected with the tannery of Messrs. W. Mortimer and Co., Ltd., for just over 50 years. The funeral took place on Wednesday, the Vicar (the Rev. G. Vale- Owen) officiating. :Wreaths were sent by the following: Messrs. Charles and James Povey, Mr. and Mrs. Swindells, Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Povey and family, Mr. and Mrs. Walter Povey and family. Mr. and Mrs. William Povev, Mr. and Mrs. John Povey, Mr. and Mrs. Charles Povey, jun.. "The Boys from Birtles-road," Mr. and Mrs. Houghton and family, Mr. and Mrs. Marsh, "The nephews and neices," Mrs. Collier and family, "The Neighbours," and a lovely bunch of white tulips was sent as a kind remem- brance from Mr. and Mrs. H. P. Densham. The funeral arrangements.. were parried out by Mr. S. Houghton, Latchford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>HIGH LEGH.</head>
        <p>HIGH LEGH.</p>
        <p>. Student's Success.—At a. gathering of the Incorporated Society of Musicians in Manches- ter last Saturday, Mr. Herbert Atkinson, of High Legh, was awarded a prize for having taken the highest position in Grade 4 of the examinations in organ ,playing at the Manches- ter Centre during 1913. The prize was in tho form of a book, "Master Musicians," by J. -Cuthbert Hadden.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>GRAP PENH ALL.</head>
        <p>GRAP PENH ALL.</p>
        <p>Street-making.—At the meeting of the Run- corn :Rural District Council at Dutton Work- house on Tuesday it was mentioned that the work of street-making in Grappenhall was pro- gressing satisfactorily.</p>
        <p>A Bird Fancier's Success.—Mr. Evans, of Vic- toria-road, secured a sound success at the Crystal Palace Bird Show. Out of an entry of five birds he secured one first, one second and two third prizes, while the other bird gained a commended card. In the class for dark mules he won a gold medal, for light mules a silver medal, and in the other classes for dark mules bronze medals, all against strong competition.</p>
        <p>Footballers Concert.=—A concert, promoted by the committee of the Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's A.F.C., to augment their funds, was held in the Large Poem on Thursday. In spite of the treacherous weather which prevailed, there was iin excellent attendance to witness the follow- ing programme :-----Quartets -by mixed voices, songs by Miss Cross, Mrs. Wicks, and Messrs. W. E. Garnett and J. Leigh. Messrs. J. Gibbs J and . Peters were responsible for a mandoline a solo and mandoline and flute duet. The humorous element was entrusted to Mr. F. . J. Emson and Mr. A. Hale. The respective items were well received by the audience, all the ar- tistes being local talent. Miss G. R: Hale !Act e d as the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
