      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>LAY IVI M</head>
        <p>LAY IVI M</p>
        <p>thought it was the natural fruitage of the work of Liberal legislation which had taken place since the fifties of last century. Their legisla- tion had been giving power to the working manl who had been steadily gaining influence. In the future the people would be more profoundly interested in political and Social questions. He hoped that interest on the part of the working- class would continue until some of the great measures promised were not only thoroughly discussed, but , placed on the Statute Book. (Loud applause)</p>
        <p>THANKS TO THE LADIES. Mr. Whitworth, in, proposing a vote of thanks to the4ladieS, congratulated the4ymm upon being able to bring together such a large gathering. Home Rule and Welsh Disestablish- ment, he thought, was well on the way to a final settlement. (Applause.) The King, in his Speech from the Throne, called Upon all classes to help in a friendly settlement of the Irish question, and it was to be hoped such would prove the case. Mr. Smith seconded, and spoke highly of the work accomplished on behalf of Liberalism by the local secretary, Mr. Barker. The resolution was carried, and Mr. Welford and Mr. Barker responded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>LIBERALS VERY ACTIVE.</head>
        <p>LIBERALS VERY ACTIVE.</p>
        <p>Inspiring Attendance at Annual Gath- ering in the Drill Hall.</p>
        <p>MR. WELFORD'S STRIKING SPEECII.</p>
        <p>There was a distinct air of festivity permeat- ing the atmosphere of the Lymni Drill Hall on Wednesday evening, when Liberals, young and old, assembled for the annual party, concert and dance. The Urge hap, whißli found itself, in somewhat of a dilemma in accomniodating the gathering, had been tastefully decorated, and this, combined with the divers coloured dresses of the ladies, made a pretty picture. The scene during the tea, with its 500 guests, was an animated one. A' concert followed, and be- tween the items were sandwiched a few speeches.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. L. Welford, the President of the local association, was in the chair, and he alas sup- ported by Mr. J. D. B. Smith, chairman of the association; Mr. R. Barker, the indefatigable honorary secretary; and Mr. J. H. Whitworth, chairman of the Altrincham Division Liberal Association. Amongst other well-known Lib- erals present were : Messrs. C. J. Barnes, J. Brearley, P. Clare, G. W. Cross, D. Daniels, A. Davies, F. Guest, W. Mercer, F. A. Morris, C. Moss, F. Moss, W. Moss, J. Moston, W. Moston, S. Moston, J. Oakes, Radcliffe, W. Seymour, J. Seymour, J. S. Clarlge, (hon. treas- urer), and G. Warburton.</p>
        <p>POLITICAL AND SOCIAL. ' Mr. Welford, who was accorded a hearty greeting, said that was a political as well as a social occasion. He was pleased to see so many present. At similar festive celebrations in the past he had had occasion to congratulate them upon the large numbers which had been present and he was pleased to be able to congratulate them once more upon the very large attendance that night. It augured well for the future of ,the Liberal party that they were willing to come in such large numbers, and he was• pleased that so many of the young men and women of the district were present. It was al very well to have a strong party of people of his age, or a little older, but the hope of the party was in its young people, and he was delighted to sec, that so many young people in Lymm held alle- giance to their party and vrert, prepared to work font. A Liberal M.P.'s Return.</p>
        <p>The Liberals in the past had hitcl their suc- cesses, and they hoped to repeat these sue- ceases. They hoped at no distant date to celebrate again the return of a Liberal Member of Parliament for the Altrincham Division. (Hear, hear, and applause.) It was well that they should keep their powder dry. The next General Election could not be far off. He sup- posed it must take place some time wit year, according to . the new order of things. He understood that they should have a fight, which, meant they would have a Liberal candidate, and it was to be hoped that wnen the fight was ended the result would be different from the last occasion. (Applause.) He was not him- self prepared to accept the defeat at the last by-election as a prophecy of what would ha;)- pen at the General Election.</p>
        <p>Much-needed Measure. • If the Plural Voters' Bill should become law and be operative at the election, that in itself must make a very considerable difference in favour of the Liberal party in that 'division, as in other divisions throughout the country, but he was hopeful, apart from the advantages that would accrue to. their party from such a mea- sure, that the result would, be different next time.</p>
        <p>Other great questions were coming up—rques- tions'which would stir the nation very deeply. It might be that they would stir the nation so deeply that there would be a great wave of enthusiasm over the measures, such a wave as might very well secure for the Liberal party thraughout the kingdom a greater victory than at either election of 1910, and which might secure a victory in that division. (Hear, hear)</p>
        <p>The Great Land Question. They knew the, measures which were indicated in the recent speeches of Mr. Lloyd George— (applause)—on the land question might very well bring about such a state of things for the election as would give them the victory in their division. The political world was full of stir and activity, and the social world was full of unrest. Everywhere throughout the whole civi- lised world 'there was that state of unrest; men and women everywhere were seeking after other and better things, reaching out for a higher and better life. The Liberal party was the one to guidelhose great movements, and the one which naturally became the great advocate of such questions as those. They should be'active'in the support of, and should do the very best work they could for the promotion and success of those great move- ments, in the years which were immediately in front of them. The great questions which had engaged the country for the last few years would soon be settled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>Home Rule This Year.</head>
        <p>Home Rule This Year.</p>
        <p>- --- - This year would see the Home Rule question settled—(applause)—and also the Welsh Dis- establishment question, the two great measures which the Conservative party very prudently and wisely, from their point of view, had ob- structed in every possible way, and would con- tinue to obstruct, because they knew that so I long as they could keep those questions before the country and Parliament itself, no other great questions could be properly pushed for- ward .or engage the serious attention of the country at large. The Conservatives had other interests at stake in trying to prevent the settlement of those two questions, but by the wonderful skill in guiding and controlling the Parliamentary forces shown by Mr. Asquith, the great Liberal I'remier—(applause)—and through the aid of the Parliament Act, they had been able to curb the power of the House of Lords. That House was no longer able to permanently obstruct and frustrate the passing of Liberal measures, and they Would be eye- witnesses of one of the great Parliamentary facts in the wonderful lxisfary of their Parlia- ment, when they saw Home Rule and Welsh Disestablishment passed into law, probably without the consent of the House of Lords.</p>
        <p>A Wonderful Step.</p>
        <p>It marked a wonderful step in the develop- ment of the political and Parliamentary history and methods. Therefore the privilege was theirs of watching with their own eyes one of the great changes in English political life. (Ap- plause.) As Liberals they need not fear the unrest that was everywhere so common. As Liberals they might welcome the unrest He</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>CAPABLE BAND OF WORKERS.</head>
        <p>A CAPABLE BAND OF WORKERS.</p>
        <p>The lads entered into the work with enthu- siasm, aud they well deserved the thanks be- stowed upon them. Amongst those who took an active part were: Mesdames C. J. Barnes, J. Bell, J. Bell, J. Brearley, L. Broadey, F. Brown, H.-Champ, J. ClAre, G. W. Cross, D. Daniels, Davies, Guest, Kirk, T. Knowles, Leigh, Lord, W. Mercer, W. Mort, W. Mess, W. Moston, J. Radcliffe, W. Seymour, Smith, T. Thernicroft, and G. L. Welford; the Misses D. Barker, E. Barker, Brazendale, F. Cowsill, Jones, Knowles, Mclntyre, C. Mercer-, L. Seymour, E. H. Stott, C. Thernieroft, Wiftstanley,mind E. Wright. 0 The task of decorating the tea tables was most tastefully accomplished by Mrs. Wyatt, and the refreshment room was admirably managed by Mrs. D. Daniels and Miss Shore. The artistes who contributed an excellent programme were: Mrs. Sanders, solos; Mr. Sanders, humorous sketches; Heatley Troop B.P. Scouts, handbell ringers, conducted by Mr. T. Nash, and Mr. F. Champ, accompanist. The M.C.'s for dancing were Messrs. W. H. Moston and A. Clare, the music being supplied by Tonk's band.</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>LINES FROM LYMM.</head>
        <p>LINES FROM LYMM.</p>
        <p>Some 500 sat down to tea. This was ale Liberal Association 'record. Thereat the other side have been A " leetle" bit surprised, weren't they? Young Liberals are becoming numerous. This is a happy augury for the future. Mr. Welford made a brilliant speech. His points were most effective. The Liberal ladies.did well in„the , Way they catered for the Liberal fivediundredA They well deserved the special vote of thanks. The tables were charmingly decorated. For this the decorators deserve all praise. We had to go to Lymm to discover new Humorous talent existing in Warrington. The Liberal agent delighted with his mono- logues.</p>
        <p>.•_mollg the applications for A Road Scheme to the Cheshire bridge s- A road improvements oilinuedfdade rionma otfhea Lnyew t m teroilitntCyouCiolcuinlefiolr t-ehe b Bridgewater Canal, and at Ditchfield overt es atthede eaapeptroiaach£7,s7too, tohfe wmhiaeihn trheaed.widrehneinegstiotaf £125 is for land and £4,445 for structural works. An Attractive Concert.—The concert of the season in Lymm takes place on Tuesday in the Drill Hall. It will be given by the Lymm Choral Society, and a most attractive pro- gramme has been arranged. The principal artistes will be Miss Doris Buckley, soprano, who earned fame by winning the first prize at the Blackpool Musical Festival; Mr. Hamilton Harris, bass, of the Queen's Hall and Albert Hall concerts, kondon ; and Mr. Albert Andrew, a talented solo violinist. The choir, who have been admirably trained, are to render part- songs, and these should prove a strong feature in the programme. The conductor is Mr. John H. Brazendale, who has assiduously devoted himself to the preparation of the choir. The conductor will be Mr. Thomas M. Eastham.</p>
        <p>Resident's Tragic Death.—Mr. Cecil parley, well-known in business circles in Manchester, Liverpool and Warrington, died ,with tragic suddenness at his residence at Lymm on Mon- day evening. He was to have spent the even- ing, With the High Sheriff of Cheshire, arid it Was while prepariig for the visit that he col- lapsed and died in his bath. At the inquest Dr. Burrows stated that death resulted from syncope. Mr. Darley came of an old Yorkshire family. • For many years he carried on business as a wino merchant in Manchester, and eventu- ally became one of the senior partners in the firm of G. and J. Greenall, Ltd., distillers, War- rington. He was a member of the Union Club, Manchester, and in politics was a Conserva- tive. lie was a bachelor, and was in his 73rd year.</p>
        <p>Fancy Dress Dance.—ln connection with the Heatley Tennis Club a fancy dress dance and a whist drive was held in the Conservative Club on Wednesday, and was attended by about 50 members and friends. Fancy dress is an inno- vation, so far as the Lymm district is concerned. There was a-good variety of characters, many , of them were pretty, others quaint and amus- ing. The event was well organised and reflected every credit upon the enterprise and efforts of the committee and the secretary, Miss Warbur- ton. The prize-winners at whist were—Ladies: I Miss G. Wright; Mrs. Westbrook, Mrs. Yarwood and Miss Parry. Gentlemen: Messrs. F. E. Evans, J. Travis, E. Lecomber and Miss Shor- rocks. The M.C. for whist was Mr. G. H. Parker, and for the dancing Mr. S. Latham offi- ciated. The following is a list of the characters assumed :—Miss Warburton, Butterflies; Miss Rowlinson, Carmen; Mr. Stones, Gambling Pierrot; Mr. V. L. Warburton, English Boy; Miss L. Warburton, • Folly; Mr. L L. Allen, Gretchen; Mr. F. Rowlinson, Cavalier; Mrs. Rowlinson, Dewdrops; Mr. Cecil Lovett, Louis XIV.; Miss G. Brown, Quaker Girl; Miss For- rest, Mary Queen of Scots; Mr. Latham, Master of Ceremonies; Mrs. Latham, Lymm Lady; Mr. Parker, English Nobleman; Mrs. Parker, Her Ladyship; Mrs. Walker; Moorish Maid; Miss Howarth, Forget-me-Not; Mrs. Dunmuir, Erin; Mrs. Hinde, Evening; Mrs. Westbrook, Blue Bird; Mr. Westbrook, Chef; Mrs. Ingham. Moonlight; Mr. Ingham, English gentleman;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>A New Schooknaster.—To succeed Mr. Jon who recently retired from the position assistant schoolmaster at the Padgate Hon to become a relieving officer under the Warrii ton Guardians, Mr. John Molynewc. of I training ship Indefatigable, has been appoini as teacher at the Homes. Mr. Molyneux v trained at St. Mark's College, London.</p>
