      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 14, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 14, 1914.</p>
        <p>THE ELECTION OF 1868.</p>
        <p>Some Gleanings and Memories of a Famous Pre-Ballot</p>
        <p>Contest.</p>
        <p>ELECTION POETRY OF EARLIER DAYS.</p>
        <p>A few readers have .bcen good enough to send us stories and memories of the famous election of 1868, which contain some interesting echoes of a stirring period. We shall be glad to receive further contributions on the same subject. Each section is contributed by a different reader.</p>
        <p>THE FIGHT AT MOTTRAM'S YARD. Bottled Voters and a Threatened Riot. Dear Sir,—Your account of the election of 1868 brought back many memories to me. I was then a youth not long past my schooldays, yet shared the excitement as though I,had been a full-grown man, full of political knowledge and enthusiasm. An election then was differ- ent in almost every detail. There were no whizzing motor ears to swirl men to the poll, the slow-moving four-wheeler being the quickest thing on the streets. The cabs were all hired beforehand by the two parties and were pasted over with party placards. It was a time of general licence, and to break the cab windows of the opposite party was one of the delights of the boys of my age. There must have been some big bills to be footed by the candidates !or broken glass in vehicles. On the day of election both sides issued from hour to hour printed sheets, which professed to show the state of the poll. Each side had a poll clerk, who gave the figures, more or less accurately, and as neither side wished to give the impression that it was losing, both issued figures that gave good prospects of victory. The scrimmage at Mottram's Yard was an ugly affair, and might have been much uglier. Indeed, it might easily have developed into riot and loss of life. There is no doubt whatever that the Tories carried out a big scheme of bottling." For the advantage of youngeir I had better explain what " bottling" was, it was a device for keeping people away from the polls by keeping them drunk through the elec- tion day. Men who were known to intend vot- ing Liberal were, in this case, made drunk in the public-houses the night before, and success- fully drugged. They were then taken off in cabs to Mottram's Yard, and housed on straw. If they woke up they were plied with liquor and sent quickly off to sleep again. The building in which they were placed is that now used by Mr. Cotton as his workshop—so at least I have always urrderstood. A strong guard of Tories kept watch over them, and also guarded the en- trance to the yard. Mottram's Yard was a pretty big area, and</p>
        <p>Our turn came early the next morning. The Mayor, Mr. Nield, gave Mr. Itylands as the victor. I do not know at what hour the an- nouncement was made, but it reached us in dramatic fashion before daylight next morning. I lived in Church-street, and we were wakened by the clanging of Rylands' bell. It was not an ordinary ringing. It had a wild sort of note that said " Victory." I and my brother dressed and went out into the street. Other figures ap- peared, and one of Itylands' workmen ran down the street to say that " Peter" was " in." I don't suppose that many towns have experi- enced two such days and nights as Warrington had in 1868. First, the excited joy of the Tories, who nearly made riots in their delight, and then the sudden reverse and the breaking out of the joy of the Liberals. Of couTrse, it lies to be re- membered that the contest had been a long one, and that the town had nearly gone made with election fever before the day of the poll.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>BY ARTHUR MOUNFIELD.</head>
        <p>BY ARTHUR MOUNFIELD.</p>
        <p>was approached from Scotland-road, as at pres- ent, and also from Newton-street (now Crown- street). There was a timber yard at the New- ton-street end. About three o'clock on the elec- tion day the alarm was given that many Liberals could not be found. It was also whis- pered that they were imprisoned at Mottram's 1 Yard. A small regiment of Liberals marched Upon the citadel, but they were not strong I enough for the defenders. Armed with blud- geons and sheltered by the gates, these made a fine defence and held the fort until four o'clock struck and the poll was closed. An uncle of mine had his head broken in the affray, and he was not the only Liberal who went to bed in bandages as a result of the battle of Mottram's Yard. But when the good news came the next morning that Peter Rylands had won, neither wounds nor bandages kept them from the streets.</p>
        <p>Mr. Itylands claimed that 200 of his sup- porters had been forcibly kept from voting. This is a large estimate, but operations at Mottram's Yard were carried out in wholesale way, and the figure may not, after all, be an exaggeration.</p>
        <p>HOW RYLANDS' BELL SAID VICTORY. I went through all the excitement of the 1868 election and will tell you of the two scenes that are most vivid in my memory. I remember very well the nomination day. In those days the candidates were nominated openly on what was called the hustings. A large, open-air platform was erected, and the rival candidates with their supporters appeared there and were proposed as suitable candidates. The crowd, of course, was a mixed one, and each speaker was duly howled down by those of the opposite party. On this occasion the Liberals appear to have placed themselves on the same side as their candidate, and the Tories did the same. Some- body began a campaign of pushing. The Liberal section tried to push their opponents off the ground, and the challenge was promptly taken up. The fortunes of the parties varied, and the crowd swayed from side to side. Those in the centre of that dense mass must have had a very bad time. Many people tried to make speeches, but it was like addressing a storm at sea.</p>
        <p>My second recollection is of the morning after the poll. My father was a good Liberal, and, like many other Liberal households, we went to bed very early and hoped the Tories would let us sleep. The night was a merry one for Mr. Greenall's supporters, who paraded the streets in a delirium of happiness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>AN ELECTION PLACARD OF 1868.</head>
        <p>AN ELECTION PLACARD OF 1868.</p>
        <p>About 25 years ago I uncovered a placard of 1868. It had been posted on the wall of the old Wesleyan Sunday School in Buttermarket- street, near to the Naylor-street entrance. A sign-board of the school had been placed over it, and by keeping off the weather had preserved it for twenty years or more. I have always been sorry I did not COPY it. It accused Peter Itylands of being a Fenian, an accomplice in Irish plots to undermine the Empire, of Anarchy and Plotting and I hardly know what else. It was evidently a "Scare" placard issued by the Tories.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>“oUR OLD FRIEND PETER.”</head>
        <p>“oUR OLD FRIEND PETER.”</p>
        <p>Dear A.M.,—Your account Of —Peter Rylands has brought back a scene that must linger in other minds beside my own. I remember listen- ing to Mr. Gladstone when he spoke from a railway carriage window whilst on his way to Midlothian. After a reference to the Liberals who had deserted him, he added, "And our old friend Peter has gone to the bad." It was a pathetic word, and I can still feel the thrill that it gave when it fell from the lips of the brave old statesman, who was sacrificing comfort and posi- tion for the sake of Ireland. I have often heard it said that the words cut Peter Rylands worse than anything in his career. Of course, Mr. Gladstone had alienated Peter by not Put- ting him in office. There were many who thought he deserved a place in the Government, and he himself was confident of it, but Mr. Gladstone did not share that view, and Peter lost patience and broke away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>nreo,9os,o.2oosecotosw.wo.2.ow.Lyn CHILDREN'S CORNER. 1 Conducted by UNCLE JACK. 0h24490NP-XY-0</head>
        <p>nreo,9os,o.2oosecotosw.wo.2.ow.Lyn CHILDREN'S CORNER. 1 Conducted by UNCLE JACK. 0h24490NP-XY-0</p>
        <p>OUR PRIZE-WINNERS.</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,—</p>
        <p>Below I have pleasure to give the names of our prize-winners. The number of com- petitors is not so large as I have seen, but the letters are most interesting, and I am sorry I cannot give everyone of you a prize. The prizes, which will consist of books, are awarded to:—</p>
        <p>NELLIE MAsoN, 174, Longford-street; JOHN STEPHENS, 13, Hoyle-street.</p>
        <p>I have received the following nice letter from Niece Hilda Palmer, for which I have to thank her very much : "Dear Uncle Jack,—l am glad to say that some of my friends have joined the club, and I hope, as you say, to see their names in "The Examiner" often, showing they have joined the competitions. I have read the book which you sent me. It was very nice indeed and very interesting.—Your affectionate niece, HILDA • PALMER."</p>
        <p>I am always delighted to receive letters from my nephews and nieces, for I like to keep in touch with all of you. I suppose now the long nights are coming you will not have much time to write—it will be all play, won't it?—but any time you care to write I shall be pleased to hear from you.</p>
        <p>Your loving</p>
        <p>- - - UNCLE JACK. PS.—How many new members are you each going to get during the next month? U.J.</p>
        <p>WANTS TO BE A TEACHER.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Jack,—You ask me what I should like to be when lam grown up, and why. Well, I should like to be a school mistress when. I grow up. Some of my reasons why are: (1) I am very fond of school life and I am also very fond of children; (2) I think it must be glorioui- to leltrn all about grammar, history, geography and arithmetic, and to be able to pass all my exathinations; (3) if I should be successful in my work at school and reach the position, I should try not to be snappy, but to be nice and try to make the children happy, and try to make the children think that school was a great pleasure, so that when they grow up and leave school they will say: "I wish I was going to school again; it was one of the greatest pleasures of my life."</p>
        <p>NELLIE MASON,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>174, Longford-street, Warrington.</head>
        <p>174, Longford-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>ENGINEER OR JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>_ Dear Uncle Jack,—Many hours have I spent in thinking what I would like to be. To enter, and be successful, in your competition I must think harder and select a trade or profession which I would like to earn my living at. As soon as "The Examiner" came to our house I looked for the competition which you men- tioned in previous weeks, namely. "What you would like to be when a man, and why?" I would like to become an engineer; first, because I have a liking for mechanical things, and, secondly', because my uncle is an engineer, and I would" like to follow his profession. If I should fail to become such, I would like to become a "journalist" like you, because, be- sides having a liking for mechanical things, I have also a liking for writing. MAl:Talcs McKinrr. 208, Orford-lane, Warrfngton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>AMBITIOUS TO GET. ON..</head>
        <p>AMBITIOUS TO GET. ON..</p>
        <p>-...-... - . - Dear Uncle Jack,—My greatest ambition in life is to become a confidential clerk, and I would learn three or four languages, so that whenever any translation of letter il was to be done I would be of great service to a firm. It would mean that there would be a lot of work to do, but I would just delight in it, because I like a situation where work is always to be carried on. The reason I should like, to be a confidential clerk is that I wish to earn enough money to keep my father and mother when they are getting old, because they have worked hard to give me a good education, and therefore I wish to pay back in kindness the good services rendered to me. I should like to become a confidential clerk and then perhaps I could work my way up to be a prominent and ener- getic director of some large firm. And now my letter for this encouraging competition closes. JOHN gTEPHENS. . 13, Hoyle-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>AS A DRESSMAKER.</head>
        <p>AS A DRESSMAKER.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Jack,—As I am nearly thirteen years of age I think it is about time I decided what I should like to be when I leave school. The occupation I should like to follow is that of a dressmaker. I should first of all have to be an apprentice for two years. Although it seems a long time to work without wages, still I should hope to reap the benefit in the future. It is a very useful trade, but still it is tedious. The reason why I should like to be a dress- maker is because I have alWays liked sewing, and therefore -could take great, interest in my work. It will be very useful to me, as I shall always be able to make ray own and other people's clothes, and earn my living 'at home. GLADYS NOLAN, • 93, West-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>DRESSMAKING USEFUL. Dear Uncle Jack,-1 am only twelve years of age, but two more years will soon pass, and then I shall have to seek work. Ido not think there will be much hesitation about deciding what I should like to be, because I have cast my mind upon dressmaking. I have chosen dressmaking so that I shall be able to make dresses And other things for .my- self, and for other people, too. llovr proud I shall be when I have a dress on that I have made myself. Ido think that dressmaking is a very useful occupation to learn. It would save lots of money. if everybody could make ' .their own clothes, so then large bills would not have to be paid for the making of dresses. , HILDA PALMER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>AS AN OFFICE CLERK.</head>
        <p>AS AN OFFICE CLERK.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Jack,—When I grow up I would like to be an office clerk, because it is very light work and it is very good pay. I can write shorthand and I can operate a typewriter, so I think I will be qualified for the work. If I should get in an office I hope I will please my employer in all I do.</p>
        <p>FRED SHELLEY. • , 85, Winwick-road, Warrington.</p>
        <p>A BIBLE WOMAN. Dear Uncle Jack,—When I grow up I should like to be a Bible woman. A Bible woman has the opportunity of going into the world preach- ing and doing good to the poor people. I also thank you very much for making me one of your nieces in the "Children's Corner." JESSIE ir. MUIR. 125, Wellfield-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>TEACHING JUST` SUITS HIM. Dear Uncle Jack,—When I am older I should like to be a teacher very much. The reason why I should like to be a teacher is that it is a nice occupation and will just suit me. Hoping my wish will be fulfilled. Toms TABERNACLE. 63, Marsh House-lane, Warrington.</p>
        <p>FOND OF NEEDLEWORK. • Dear Uncle Jack,—l should like to he a dress- maker when I grow up, as I am very fond of needlework, and why I should like to be 01S</p>
        <p>(Continued in next column.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>WORLD OF LETTERS.</head>
        <p>THE WORLD OF LETTERS.</p>
        <p>New Books.</p>
        <p>Hypnotism: Its History, Practice and Theory. By J. Milne Bramwell, M. 8., C.M. London: William Rider and Son, Ltd. 128. Gd. net. .</p>
        <p>Hypnotism is not a subject of which much is known by the general public, and, it is associ- ated in the. popular mind with so much legend- ary matter that any account of it which aims at being in any degree scientific must be largely taken up with describing what hypnotism is not. Thus, it is popularly supposed that a per- son can be hypnotised against his will. This is quite erroneous. Again it is thought that hypnotismjends to weaken the will, and that susceptibility to hypnosis itself evinces a lack of volitional power. The very opposite is the fact. It is found by careful observation that it is just those who are capable of the strongest self-control who are most readily amenable to hypnotic suggestion. Such a book as Dr. Bram- well's treatise is, therefore, exactly what is needed, for it gives just the information neces- sary in order to arrive at a sound judgment on this very important matter. And we could hardly have a safer guide to the intricacies of the subject, for Dr-Bramwell has been engaged in hypnotic practice for the past twenty years, and is thoroughly conversant—as the list' of references covering 24 pages in this book will show—with the enormous number of books, pamphlets and articles which have been written on the subject of hypnotism, especially on the Continent. Dr. BTamwell writes a very full and interest- ing account of the mesmerists, and of the rise of modern hypnotism, of which Braid was the founder, and he shows that the latter's views have been misunderstood, and much of his work under-rated, through igruirance of his later writings on the subject. Although Dr. Bram- well has proved in his own practice that hypnotism is of service in surgical work, he is of opinion that its usefulness in this branch of medical practice is extremely restricted, because "generally hypnosis never becomes deep enough for operative purposes." It is in the treatment of disease that the chief value of. hypnotism lies. A great many ailments which are of nervous origin may be cured, or at' least greatly relieved by suggestion. Dr. Bramwell gives a large number of illustrative cases from his own prac- tice, and that of others. He describes also how some of his patients, who had been deeply hypnotised, were instructed to reintroduce the condition at will and to originate suggestions regarding their health, comfort er work.</p>
        <p>' The popular prejudice against hypnotism, that it tends to weaken the subject's will-power, has already been referred to. Dr. Bramwell de- votes a considerable portion of his book to prov- ing from among his own patients that not only has hypnotism not this tendency, but that patients have full power to refuse any sugges- tion made to them, which is not in accord with their moral sense. An individual cannot be hypnotised against his will, and when his con- sent has been obtained, and he is hypnotised, he is not an automaton in the hands of the hypnotiser. Very often in hypnosis, as Dr. Bramwell shows, the subject reveals an intelli- gence very much superior to that of his normal waking life. And in many experiments which have been made for the express purpose of test- ing the will-power of the subject, it has been found to be fully equal to that of the ordinary state, and sometimes the moral sense has ap- peared to be more acute. Dr. Bramwell says "I have frequently noticed increased refine- ment in hypnosis; subjects have refused sug- gestions which they would have accepted in the normal condition." •</p>
        <p>Extremely interesting and valuable is the chapter—which takes up nearly one-third of the book—dealing with the various theories that have been advanced from time to time to account for the. phenomena of hypnotism. Dr. Bramwell, we think, is scrupulously fair in his summaries of these theories, giving them in great detail ana with very important critical notes. The value of the work is enhanced by the admirable bibliography and the indices and appendix. Dr. Bramwell's book is a remarkable one, and should be of great service to the medical pro- fession. It is an exhaustive study in a depart- ment of psychology only in recent years recog- nised by scientific men at all, but it may prove in the future:to be of the utmost import- ance, especially in view of its immense thera- peutic possibilities. No extravagant claims are put forward, but the' subject is considered in a strictly scientific spirit and with admirable im- partiality. We cordially recommend Dr. Bramwell's book to all interested in psychological questions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>NIGHT THIEF AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>NIGHT THIEF AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Joseph Day, labourer, of 5, Cliff-street, Wid- nes, was charged at St. Helens on Tuesday, with stealing a mackintosh and a quantity of groceries, value 10s., from the house of John Marsh, commercial traveller, of 434, Leech-lane,l Clock Face. Mrs. Marsh said that at eleven o'clock on Wednesday evening, January 28th, she locked up the house and went to bed. Dur- ing the night she heard a noise downstairs, but thought it was caused by the cat. At five o'clock in the morning she found that the backi window had been broken and the place ran- sacked—Constable Gates, of Widnes, said that on Saturday morning he searched prisoner's house and found the mackintosh hanging in the bedroom. He showed it to prisoner, who said he had stolen it from a house just outside St. Helens.—Chief Inspector Dunn said that -Day was a native of Widnes, and on the previous day was fined 40s. and costs for stealing lead.—Tha Bench sent Day to gaol for 28 days for breaking into the house and stealing.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. W. Clifford has resigned the position of prospective Unionist candidate for the Cleveland Division of Yorkshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER.</p>
        <p>Literary Gleanings.</p>
        <p>HOW THE WORLD SLEEPS. Most people sleep on their sides, with the knees drawn up. Elephants always and horses commonly sleep standing up. Birds, with the exception of owls and the hanging parrots of .India, sleep with their heads turned tailward over the back and the beak thrust among the feathers between the wing and body.</p>
        <p>Storks, gulls, and other long-legged birds, sleep standing on one leg. _</p>
        <p>Ducks sleep on open water. To avoid drifting shoreward, they keep paddling with one foot, thus making them move in a circle. _ _</p>
        <p>Sloths sleep hanging by their four feet, the head tucked in between their fore legs. Foxes awl wolves sleep curled up, their noses and the soles of their feet close together and blanketed by their bushy tails. Hares, snakes and fish sleep with their eyes wide open.</p>
        <p>Owls, in addition to their eyelids, have a screen that they draw sideways across their eyes to shut out the light, for they sleep in the daytime.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>AVIATION OF TO-MORROW.</head>
        <p>AVIATION OF TO-MORROW.</p>
        <p>As to the machines of five years hence,, they will be still more solidly and elaborately con- structed, more elastic also, and better hung for landing, like luxuriously appointed automo- biles. Some will have dimensions far greater than those of the machines we see nowadays; they will be, if not liners, at any rate aerial yachts, capable of carrying in a comfortable and luxurious cabin passengers who will be simply travellers, and also cargo. With their powerful motor they will possess not only a very great speed, but also sails and tail of reduced dimensions. These aerial vehicles will offer, then, the maximum of comfort, security, and speed. But as, in spite of improvements in the motors, breakdowns will still have to be antici- pated, a remedy for these will have had to be provided in some way or other. So I clearly foresee, given the lighter weight of the motors at that time, the employment of an auxiliary motor intended either to bring the machine to earth as gently as possible, or to operate a horizontal helix which will lessen, the fall by reducing the speed to that of a normal landing; perhaps, too, there will be a combination of the two systems. Having acquired an important degree of security, aeroplanes will have become practical. But do not mistake me. Ido not claim that in five years ordinary aerial trans- port will be as general as the same services on the earth, at any rate, with the use of aero- planes. lanes For these latter the struggle will be too difficult to enter on and sustain against powerful and rapid steam or electric machines. But it is certain by that time that aviation will have been made use of for the ordinary postal service. Attempts are made to annihilate dis- tance by the telegraph and telephone. With the aeroplane these same distances will . have become almost non-existent for the couriers.— Louis l'aulhan, in the "Globe."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>AUSTRALIAN DESERT.</head>
        <p>THE AUSTRALIAN DESERT.</p>
        <p>There are parts of Australia which the white man traverses at his peril—the dry land where the banes of those who perish from the intoler- able thirst are found stark naked, their hands bloody with digging for water, their eyes wide open and white, their tongues swollen. No rivers water this desolate tract. There are no oases at which the traveller who has wandered from the way may drink and live. Convicts who escaped in olden days, prospectors of later times—all have left their trail in bleached bones in this arid wilderness as a warning fo those who would venture far into its deadly embrace. It is in the goldfields country of Western Aus- tralia that the first glimpse of the waterless tracts of sand and spinnifex is obtained from a rocky elevation at the edge of the habitable land. Here is seen the very sorriest habitable Australian country. Beyond, a flaming wilder- ness—a red prospect, splashed with the green of hardy scrub; its remoter parts, where 'a sullen wind lazily stirs, lie in a haze Of heat and crim- son dust. The lean trees—the quick and the dead—run diminishing to the north lie a deathless army, and there vanish, discouraged by the awful prospect beyond, where .the dry lands, glowing red-hot, form an effectual barrier to the march Of vegetation. An expedition of proportions, outfitted with experienced precaution—a seasoned leader with his camels and bushmen and black fellows— could not advance through the centre from Kalgoorlie and come safely to the nearest settle- ments of Sydney side except by grace of those fortuitous chances whith men in the extremity of distress call the goodness of Providence. Such is Australia's No Man's Land, on which the Asiatic has cast covetous eyes. A dislodged pebble, a broken twig, or the stub of a burnt match gives the experienced bushman a ready clue to the trail in these regions, and his sharp, Sure sight in picking up the track is astonish- ing. In a baffled attempt to reach the centre of the continent, one of the first explorers, being forced long ago to summer in this selfsame lati- tude—ruuch as an Arctic explorer winters on his ground—found far to the east a shade tem- perature of 132 degrees, which rose in the sun to 157 degrees. The Mean tempertiture for January, in that situation and exceptional season, was 104 degrees in the shade. "Time ground was thoroughly heated to a depth of three or four feet," he records; " and the tre- mendous heat had parched all vegetation. Under its effects every screw in our boxes had been drawn. Horn handles and combs were split into fine laminfr. The lead dropped out of our pencils. Our hair, as well as the sheep's wool, ceased to grow, and our nails became brittle as glass. The flour lost more than eight per cent. of its original weight. We were obliged to bury our wax candles. We found it difficult to write or draw, so rapidly did the fluid dry in our pens and brushes."—Norman Duncan in " llarper's Magazine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>BLUE GAS AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>BLUE GAS AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Blue gas invades Widnes almostidaily and the cause was discussed at the Town Council on Tuesday. The Mayor (Councillor D. Lewis) said the output of noxious gases had been observed as far afield as Farnworth Station. For it to be distributed over so wide an area was most un- usual, it was a great nuisance, and person- ally he would gladly hail the day when there would be considerable improvement. Councillor Bullough contended that the in- spections to detect these escapes from. the vari- ous works were not as efficiently conducted as could be desired. People were being driven out of the town by the output .of sulphuretted hydrogen. Councillor Garghan (Labour) remarked that the throwing off of chlorine by the chemical works had recently been worse than for a num- ber of years. In the early morning the atmos- phere was so bad that it affected the eyes and face, and gas covered the streets as thickly as - paint.</p>
        <p>Councillor Millar (chairmar of the Parks Committee) said that the valuable properties which his committee controlled were being damaged by these gases. Strangers were aston- ished at the variegated appearance of the streets. The chemical coating remained every- where until a beneficent sun came out and ab- sorbed some, leaving the remainder to be inhaled 1)3- the inhabitants. The matter was referred to the Health Com- mittee for them to take any action they deemed' advisable.</p>
      </div>
