      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>HARD-WORKED ACTOR.</head>
        <p>THE HARD-WORKED ACTOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Martin Harvey has some confessions to make about the theatrical profession. "The people who throng towards the stage only see its attractive side," he says. "They should understand the care, the misery, the privation which ever dogs the footsteps of the capable player—troubles intensified for the younger man. People say actors are improvident. Why, they are the most frugal and careful people I have ever met. They want so little out of life, beyond employment in the theatre. • When times are good and engagements are long ones; as a class they save regularly, and save a bigger proportion of their incomes than most people. The poor actor has two problems before him, in his economics. He cannot lay money away for</p>
        <p>his old age unless he is lucky. Long before he has the right to think of providing for. his old age, he must be concerned about making provi- __ .</p>
        <p>si-on. for the accidents of his profession. He is always saving when employed, and yet he rarely possesses funds for his old age. A long rest will dissipate the scanty savings of years, and when he goes hack to work the business of frugality begins again, and he has to make pro- vision for the next and ever-threatening rainy day. No one is happier, than the actor with regular work. lie saves for the rainy day, and also for the worse spectre looming behind—the period of old age, when his talents are enfeebled and his charm begins to fade. The average actor has but few extravagances, lie is con- tent to do his work and husband his resources, and, after the work is done, a simple meal and a chat with other friends who do the same work are nearly all the pleasures he expects from his leisure."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>-WOMEN'S QUESTION&amp;</head>
        <p>-WOMEN'S QUESTION&amp;</p>
        <p>Pioneers in Advertising Schemes of Startling Kinds.</p>
        <p>THE QUEEN AT, WINDSOR.</p>
        <p>• Queen Mary, who is the most active of Royal ladies, has been busily employed during her sojourn at Windsor in classifying and arrang- ing the superb collections of china that are, perhaps, the finest in the world. Her Majesty has also driven frequently in the Park, accom- panied by Princess Mary, and the two have on several occasions joined the guns at luncheon time. The pre-Easter season is to be quite gay this year. The King and Queen pay, in com- mon with King Edward, little heed to the super- stitions connected with Friday, for several COiirts will be held on that day. The Princess Royal and her daughter, Princess Maud, visited their Majesties at Windsor. The health of the former has lately greatly improved, conse- quently her Royal Highness is seen much more than formerly at various social functions.</p>
        <p>The Revival of the Plain Tea. Unmistakable signs, although at present not very pronounced, go to show that society has become surfeited with amusements and spec- tacular displays, in conjunction with its meals, and that the plain dinner and tea will have a vogue in the coming season. Dinner Music, however, will probably still find favour. Danc- ing, reciting and potted plays at meal times have been overdone, hence the rwction in favour of the meal as it was taken &amp;fore the craze for these innovations set in.</p>
        <p>!! Women and the Advertising Business. The Americans long ago discovered the im- portance of employing women " pushers" in their business, especially in the "publicity de- partment." Nor is this surprising, seeing that the great bulk of the shopping of the town is done by women, and the business of attracting these customers is better understood by women. They have introduced many novel ideas in ad- vertising, including the spoken advertisement. In these days, if you bear two women talking in resonant voices in a tram or omnibus, and possibly praising a certain theatrical perform- ance, or shop, it is more likely than not to be " advertising business." This is being done in many towns in this country, and is a develop- ment of the "professional patient" employed by American doctors. This lady is one who spends her time in the waiting rooms of rising phyeicians, and describes in glowing terms the great benefit she has derived from the treatment of the particular specialist in the next room, and how wonderfully clever he must be. In towns all over America there is just now in full swing a great poster campaign against alcohol, and it was a woman who first started the busi- ness of painting these posters on the hides of cows for all to see as they graze in the fields.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>Belts and Girdles.</head>
        <p>Belts and Girdles.</p>
        <p>The high waist is to disappear, or so it is said, but it never goes quite out of fashion, for its neatness and trimness are much in its favour. Moreover it is exceedingly becoming to all but the stout figure. However, if unfashionable, it may be easily disguised with the aid of a deep belt, or by one of the new ribbon girdles. The latter are made of wide ribbon, and hang in long ends at one side, or an end at each side, accord- ing to taste. The deep belt May be of satin, brocade, velvet, or of ribbon, and beautiful pieces of the latter may still be picked up at the sales, at ridiculously low prices. White blouses are worn with coloured skirts of every description, although many women prefer to have a special blouse for each suit. Never was there such diversity in these useful garments, that, instead of losing favour, become every day more indispensable to women of all classes.</p>
        <p>Fascinating Neckwear. . The woman with light fingers :and a dainty touch may safety invest • in remnants of lawn, hemstitched or otherwise, lace; muslin,: or,net— anything, in fact, that may be utilised for pretty neckwear. The success of a toilette, whether for morning, afternoon, or evening wear, depends nowadays on the treatment of the neck. Medici collars, made of richly-em- broidered tulle, or of metallic lace, are used on evening gowns, and make a most picturesque</p>
      </div>
