      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>MIIIIIIIk-</head>
        <p>MIIIIIIIk-</p>
        <p>TEE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, FEBRUARY 14, 1914.</p>
        <p>frame for the face and shoulders. They are seen also on dinner, afternoon and semi- decolette gowns. In pleated net or chiffon— stiffened, of course, with a fine wire—medici collars are much worn by Parisiennes, who, it must be admitted, carry them with a special grace, all their own. A black velvet ribbon is invariably worn with this, and also a Marie Antoinette collar, •clasped with paste, or other Ornament. Similar bands of velvet are some- times worn with the neckband. and on the arms just above the wrist. These collars are not diffi- cult to make, and the saving in the cost is very considerable. Pretty frills for day and evening dresses may be had for the proverbial song, if advantage is taken of the opportunities the sales afford for securing suitable materials at low prices, and these frills will be very much in evidence for some considerable time to some.</p>
        <p>The Vogue for Jet. The continued favour shown to the all-black toilette and to magpie effects doubtless accounts for the popularity of jet, which is just now a successful rival to the pearl. It is a very long time since jet has been so universally used as it is at present. All kinds of ornaments for the !lair are made of it, the most popular, perhaps, being the plain band. The jet dog collar, with diamond fastening, has taken the place of the pearl necklace, and is particularly suited to the middle-aged woman. Jet bracelets throw up a youthful, white arm to perfection, and many novel designs are shown for these ornaments. Bags, umbrella handles, buttons, and many other articles for use and adornment are fash- ioned of this beautiful mineral. In millinery it is the latest novelty, both for morning and afternoon wear, in combination with black and white tulle, covered with masses of cut beads.</p>
        <p>The New Coiffure. Another revival that will be seen in the near future is the .old-fashioned, high, tortoiseshell comb, for the coming coiffure lends itself to the wearing of these often beautiful ornaments. There is no doubt that in a short time everyone will wear the hair dressed on the top of the head, and rolled back from the forehead, which will be shown. The new hats which are still very' small, arc remarkable for the height of their crowns, and this style demands the high coiffure. There are, at present, no signs of the return of the picture hat, with its wealth of plumes. In fact, ostrich feathers are for the noment out. of favour. The new hats require so very little trimming, and that consists often of a very high ribbon mount, or a jaunty plume rises from folds of velvet or moire. A notice- able feature of the new hats is the lack of brim, and to be at all becoming they must be put on, or set, at the right angle, or they have a top- heavy and by no means pleasing or smart ap- pearance.</p>
        <p>REGINALD GOODWIN, FOR SILKS OF MERIT. 40in. Cymberline 2s. 614. 4020. Roman Satin, 3s. 6d. 40in. Enjoleuse, 4s. 11d. 40in. Printed and Plain Ninon', is. hid. t„. 6s. 11d. 40in. Soft Broches, 2s. Did. to 10a. 6d. Post Free. 'Phone 5883 City. Saturday-8, 1 o'clock. 9, RIDGEFIELD, KING-STREET MANCHESTER</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>RUNCORN WOMEN OF PEACE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN WOMEN OF PEACE.</p>
        <p>A meeting in connection with the Runcorn Women's Liberal Association was held in ! Greenway-road Schools on Tuesday. Mrs. harry ! Baker presided over a good attendance, and was ! supported by Mrs. Bradlaugh Bonner and ! others. Mrs. Baker said she believed in peace at any price. It was far better to train sons to love their country rather than to train them to die for it. Mrs. Bradlaugh Bonner said that the econo- mic security of woman was imperilled by war, and it was their duty to make themselves ac- quainted with All its harmful essentials. The secrecy of the Foreign Office should be done away with, and an accurate statement of the relations with foreign Powers should he given to the public. The public of tlOs country were not a box of marionettes who had to dance war or peace at the wishes of those .behind the scenes. The suggestion that mothers should rear sons to make material for war was an aspect of the matter which outraged their tenderest feelings, and they indignantly resented it. . It was one of the meanest aspects of the whole question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>COMPLETE EXAMINER STORY.</head>
        <p>COMPLETE EXAMINER STORY.</p>
        <p>the air was hull 01 iiie with singing rams ana rustling leaves, blosvining flowers and radi- ant sunshine in the azure depths of the sky. Dick began to whistle out of sheer light heartedness, then stopped with a shocked feeling, lest Miss Horn should think he was triumphing over her disappointment. Being a well-mannered youth he considered it neces- sary to be demure in the presence of a girl , who had been left without a shilling, and hait been insulted into the bargain.</p>
        <p>Miss Horn was seated Under the gigantic tree with the tea-caddy on .the lap of her cheap black gown. Although she had been weeping copiously, and her hazel eyes were infinitely sad, she looked remarkably pretty with her curling brown hair and lair com- plexion and general patrician air. Sire had slim, well-shaped feet also and hands to match, and Dick halted before She be- came aware of his presence, in order to mentally ouserve that she really was quite lovely. He felt a thrill somewhere about the region of the heart, but stntly declined to acknowledge that it was-love. It was sorrow, pity, regret—anything but• a desire to take Patty in his arms and kiss away her falling tears.</p>
        <p>" Mr. Miller !" cried the girl, rising so suddenly that the tea caddy fell 011 the grass. "I--I—l didn't expect to see you here." Dick handed back the caddy with a grave bow, and noticed that she spoke in respect- ful tones. "And you didn't want to see me, I suppose," he observed. No, Mr. Miller," she answered quietly. "I certainly did not." "I can't help Aunt Lavinia giving me the fortune," Hick began apologetically. iViss Horn cut him short "I don't mind your having it. Indeed I am gad it is you, since your cousins were always down here paying court to Lady Durvernay. But—" she stopped and flushed and bit her lip. "She left me this thing," she ended holding up the tea caddy, and striving to speak calmly. " Why ?" asked Dick bluntly, "'unless you are an enthusiatic tea-drinker." Patty laughed softly. "It wouldn't be much good to me if I were," she said, the laugh becoming a sigh, " a letter came with the caddy, written by Lady Durvernay, say- ing that I was not to open it until my wed- ding-day." The young man stared. "In heaven's name why_?',_</p>
        <p>"- I don't know, Mr. Curwin is to give me the key after lam married. Stupid old thing,' she added, looking revengefully at the caddy. "I've a good mind to break it open now."</p>
        <p>"Let me help you," said Dick, obligingly producing his pocket-knife. Patty warded him off hurriedly. " No! no! no! I shall obey Lady Durvernay's instruc- tions, for she must have had some reason for giving them." " She never had any reas,n save her con- stant desire to play the bully," cried Dick, his face becoming hot and angry, "and when I think how she treated—" " Never mind how she treated me," snapped the girl colouring. " But, Patty, you—" " Miss Horn to you, Mr. Miller," was the haughty reply. " It was Dick and Patty when you nursed me," he answered, reproachfully. " I darcsay; but times have changed." ". They have," he said meaningly. "You have the tca-:addy and I have—nothing." Patty opened her bright eyes to their widest extent. " You have nothing!" she re- peated slowly, " how can that be, when the will—"</p>
        <p>" Oh, hang the will. pardon." " I don't mind: say it again," and her eyes shone. " Hang the will," said Dick fervently. The girl sighed in quite a happy manner. " That does me good! It's a horrid will in- deed. But I daresay you d )n't think so." •• Yes, I do. Upon my soul I do. I would rather you had the money." Oh, nonsense," said Patty raising her " you require it badly.'' "I require you badly." " How dare you speak to me in that way," Miss Horn stamped her pretty foot and clenched ,her pretty hands. ' Your attrit in- sulted me, and now y.iu do the same. Go and live at. the Hal on your five thousand a yea' and leave me to my tea-caddy." " I'm not goinc, to live at the Hall—by my- self," vociferated ° Dick, as she began to cry. " Don't, dear—don't." The</p>
        <p>I--er--I beg your</p>
        <p>" Yes, I shall,— you horrid thing. will—"</p>
        <p>" Hang the will and da—" " Don't swear," Patty, • fogetting Lady Duivernary's interference which had raised an unnecessary barrier between them, placed her .hand OWT his mouth.</p>
        <p>Dick caught it and kissed it." Oh, Patty, The girl tore herself away. " You coward: you coward," she sobbed, and dropped down oa the grass to. weep floods of angry tears The young man, much distressed, knelt beside her. Upon my word I didn't Mean to hurt your feelings. Can't you understand. I—l—l love you."</p>
        <p>Patty sat up with a gasp. " You don't: you can't: you shouldn't. It's quite impos- sible for you to love me in five minutes.</p>
        <p>" I've known you for five ;-e.a-rs—;;;;;iore," insisted Dick, speaking quite honecitly. I liked you no end when you nursed me, and now that Aunt Lavinia says I'm not to man, you, I jolly well will."</p>
        <p>" Yoti haven't got my consent yet." " I3ut you'll give it, dear: I know YOU will. I'm sure it's love I feel: all at once it came over me that I'd loved you all hatloilge. Only Aunt Lavinia's meddling brought feeling- uppermost—through a thi crust Of friendship, ended Dick in quite a poetic way or a British subaltern. " How absurd you are. I'm not worth five thousand a year.' " And the house and the estate. Yes 7.,011 are: you are worth the world to me, darling, Bother the money and become my wife.'" " No! no! no!" cried Patty springing to her feet, and hugging the tea-caddy to her breast. " I can't let you marry me. It's your chivalry.". " It's my selfishness," Dick assured her. I don't think it is selfish to give up a fortune—for—for—" " Love," suggested Dick, supplying the mis,ing word. ' Say love." No, indeed, I shan't. You can't possibly love me," her face grew rosy as she spoke, her eyes shone with a starry lustre and het voice trembled. " Oh, I know quite well why you Want to marry me," she spoke very fast and evaded his arms. " You Always hated be- ing told to do anything, especially by Lady Durvernay, so wheu she says that you are not to marry me you—" No, really, no," exclaimed Dick earn- estly. "I know there L 3 a good deal of ob- stinacy in my composition, but I'm not such a mean hound as to marry you in order to spite my aunt. I really came down to see if you would accept part of the money to make up for that infernal tea caddy. But when I saw you "—his eyes grew sunny—" I really realised—quite suddenly too—that you .tvete the one wife in the world for me." Do You really mean that, Dick ?" Patty</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>LALL EIGHTS RESERVED..?</head>
