        <p>FERGUS HUME fAuthor of "The Mystery of a Hansom Cab," " The Nameless City," etc.).</p>
        <p>"It's an utterly absurd will!" declared ;;;ke young man, fuming. " Certainly an imperative ,one," admitted the solicitor blandly. " Still your late aunt always liked to have her own way." " And everyone else's way in addition, Mr. Curwin." " Natural enough from a, woman' a point of view. Still live thousand a year with a fine old family mansion in Sussex and a large es- tate--" " All at the price of my not doing what .1 _never_ wanted to do."</p>
        <p>"That should</p>
        <p>The lawyer coughed drily. please you, Mr. Ittiller.' "Then it dpesn't," retorted Mr. Miller, ° truculently, " the moment I'm told not to do a thing, I immediately want to do it." "Lady Durvernay seems to have guessed as much, and se— , he waved his hand to- wards the will and coughed again, whereat Mr. Miller swore softly. He was a very nice looking young man of twenty-five with a tanned skin, a small . moustache and closely clipped fair hair. His appearance was that of a soldier, as indeed he was, and the perfection of his dress showed that he owed more money to his tailor than his position as a -subaltern in the Gunners warranted. Yet hard-up as Dick Miller had always been, he had never calculated that his aunt, whom he always disobeyed, would leave him her money. Out of perversity, as it seemed, she had done so, but with a res- triction which put him into a fine rage. He had never obeyed her before, but he woUld have to do so now, or forfeit more than he wished to lose. But the odd thing was that he had never given a thought towards doing what Lady Durvernay objected to in her will. Still the mere fact that she had behaved posthumously in what he regarded as a dicta- torial way, made him burn -to decline her or- der. There was a fair amount of human na- ture in Dick.. "Just read the beastly will again, Mr. Cur- win," he observed when he had cooled down and could resume hia seat without exploding. The lawyer did so. It was a short and some- what snappy will, which left Richard Tar- rant Algernon Miller an estate, a mansion, ' s and five thousand a year on condition that he did not marry Mis Patty Horn, the com- panion oi the testatrix. If he did the proper- ty real and personal was to . revert to an Or- phan Asylum. Lady Durvernay had left vari- ous legacies to her servants in the shape of money, and one to Miss Horn in the form of an antique tortoise-shell tea-caddy of the Queen Anne period. 'And it isn't worth a fivers" burst Out Dick at this stage of the second read- ing. "Why the poor girl waited on Aunt La- vinia for years, and put up with her tan- trums like an angel. I ' never would; not me ; the old cat." " Well, Mr. Miller, the old cat, as you dis- respectfully call her, has left you a veri handsome fortune, and I congratulate you. " Don't be in a hurry," Dick advised him coolly. "I mayn't take it. "Oh!" Mr. Curwiv's face expressed un- bounded astonishment. " Then—then—oh, pardon me, Mr. Miller, but are you—to put it, plainly—it love with—" ' With Miss Horn ? No I'm not, and I never intended to be, until I heard you read that rotten will." " But I don't see—" "Of course you don't," interrupted the client testily, "no one expected that you would. After all Patty's a deuced pretty girl, and the danghter of a General, who died without leaving her a cent. Aunt La- vinia took her as a companion, and led her the life of . a third-rate dog. Patty nursed me when I broke My aria out hunting, too," end- ed Mr. Miller gratefully. " and kept Aunt Lavinia away when she bothered me, which she was always doing. And if I choose to marry the girl," declared Dick furiously, " that's my affair and not—" "But you don't want to marry her. said as much." " And I say it again. I don't: at least I didn't, until I heard the will. Now I'm be- ginning to think she's the prettiest girl I ever saw, and—" " Take care, Mr. Miller, she will cbst you—" " She may be worth it for all you know," Jaid Dick, who seemed determined never to allow Mr. Curwin to finish his sentences. He stood up very straight, adjusted his eye-glass and pulled down his frock-ceat simmering all the time like a kettle on the boil. " It s all bosh you know, Mr. Curwin. "I don't want to lose the fortune, and yet what right has Aunt Lavinia to tell Inc that I'm not to marry the girl. Oh, indeed: that's a good one. I wish I could drop across Aunt La- vinia's gliest. give her or It a piece of my mind.. I daresay she's in 'the room here burbling over the full." Mr. Curwin was a dry-as-dust lawyer, but when his wrathful client looked into viewless air in search of Lady Durvernay's spectre, he shivered. The old lady had been so objec- tionable during her long life that she would certainly prove to.be a most unpleasant ap- parition. And in a Chancery Lane office too. The idea was uncomfortable, and Mr. Curwin Mr. Miller was grew cross. " Well, what is your decision ?" he demanded, tartly. " I shall tell you," said Dick, marching towards the door, " when I've seen Miss Patty Born," and he betook himself to the West End, leaving the solicitor much per- perplexed himself and could riot enoY, for that reassn, the ver tempt- ing dinner he ordered at his club. What. he iy, had stated to Curwin was true enough: he liked !MK-tilled, clever, patient nurse, besides con- Miss Horn, and thought her a kind- sidering her an extremely pretty girl. But he had never entertained the idea of falling, in love with her, until the, will ordered him not to. Inen think how charming she was, and Whan to at a ffi Popular ocer's„svife she would be. Then he hims told out of sheer perversity, he begelf that he knew his own mind, and was not going to have dead and gone elderly ladies dictate to him. If Aunt Lavinia thought that he was going to obey her whims he would jolly well show her that he would not, if only t) prove that he was a man and not a slave. And after Coming to this conclu- sma, Dick, with his young head in the air, went to seek distraction for his already dis- tracted mind at a music-hall, whistling Rule Britannia. The night's rest—wb a there was of it— brought counsel, and Dick told. himself that t he was a fool to let his desire to manage his own affairs interfere with' the possession of a large property, which he badly, wanted. In this frame of mind he sat•down and wrote to Curwin sayin- g that he would accept the money on the conditiona named in the will. Then. his obstinate vela earpo uppermost and tore the letter into pieecs with further whistlings of Rule Britannia. Finally, he changed into a tweed suit, and took a taxi to Victoria Station. In something over the hour he arrived at Lewes, and chartered a flv, which carried him five Miles into the country and deposited him at the door of the lordly mansion he would inherit if he chose to do violence to his feelings and obey a dead ty- rant. " Is Miss Horn indoors ?" Dick asked the old bptler,. who had known him since he was a dour lcaby in short clothes. " Sittin' under the Druid's Oak, Master Richard, the day be•ing fine." •• I'll look her up," said Dick promptly. " Is she—er—quite well ?'' " As well as can be exnected. Master Rich- ard, seem' my lady left her nothin but an old tea-caddy, and she not havin' a penny. But I'm 17,1;d sir, as you're comm n' to be— why, he's gone !" and The butler rubbed the ancient eyes to make certain that Miller had vanished into thin air, which he had. " A very prompt young gentleman is Master Richard ! ' was the butler's comment. Meanwhile the subaltern was marching un- der the glorious foliage of the park trees to- wards a central dell, where grew the Druid's , Oak iv attered seclusiaa. 4s it wits still snriug</p>
