        <p>Looked at him searchingly. " Can't you see !" lie advanced with mas- terful arms, but, she slipped behind the trunk of the tree, half amused . and half annoyed. Like children they ran round and round. Patty crying out breathlessly, " lon Gun t know your own mind." " I know my own wife and you are she." " You 41ways were silly." "Always, save on this occasion. Ah! I've caught you at last. Dearest Patty, do—do-- say—'' ' I shall say nothing—for a week any- how." " And then, darling ?" Patty ,laughed emodonally and pushed him away. It's ridiculous. I have my pride as well as you have, and I shan't De ordered to marry you. • You were ordered not to," Dick remind- ed her with a shrug and a hug. " I mean you are ordering me, and it's —it's silly. Go away and it you are in the same mind in seven days—" " Well ? Well ? Well ?" Miss Horn suddenly twisted herself out of his embrace and fled. " I shall consider what Lo say," came floating back, as she sped up to the house. Of course Dick followed to extort a more intelligible reply, and of course Patty acing ail fleet-footed as Atalanta, managed to gain shelter first, and lock herself in her bedroom. Dick returned to Town, burning with afrec. tion; and wrote to Curwin saying that he in. ecnded to surrender the property to the Or- phan Asylum and marry Miss Horn—in one month. As a matter of fact he did not marry her for. three, and during that time by letters and telegrams and I.)y word of mouth, he so thoroughly convinced her that he could not exist without her, that Patty, who had real,y been in love with him for years, yielded to his proposals. The wedding was very quiet as befitted a pair that were starting married life on three hundred a year, but several of Dick's brother officers came with presents. Mr. and Mrs. Richard Miller started for a honeymoon at Salcombe in Devonshire, and carried with them the famous tea-caddy. Also they had the key of the same which was solemnly presented to the bride by Mr. Curwin when the ceremony was at an end. "And he told me that he admired you ex- ceedingly for preferring love to nioney," ex- plained alrs. Miller, ' although he didn't think you were wise." I'm the ,wisest man in the world and the happiest husband," said Dick kissing her again and again. "It was wonderful how quickly I discovered how ardently I loved you. "Oh, Dick!" she clung to him in the first- class compaihment strewn with rice, which bore them to Salcombe, " you have acted so nobly. I really am not worth five thousand a year and a large estate." "Yes you are: yes you are." " You'll never repent " Never, you foolish darling," he kissed her three times and the placed on her lap the tea-caddy. " Open tluis, dear. Perhaps it's filled with jewels." The bride laughed and shook her pretty head. And when the caddy was opened all it contained was a doubled up blue '— directed in Lady Durvernay s scratchi writing to " Mrs. Richard Miller looked annoyed. " Hang it,"- he murmured, staring, " she expected that I would marry you after all." Patty laughed again and tore open the en- ;elope. "My dear, she knew how obstinate you were. But what can she—oh, Dick, it's a will." It certainly was—a later will than that which had lost him the money and gained him a wife. But it left the Hall in Sussex together with the estate and the five thous- and a year to Richard Tarrant Algernon Miller, exactly as in the former document but without any restriction. The second tes- tament had been drawn up by another lawyer so, as Dick grimly observed, Mr.- Curwin had undoubtedly been genuine in his condolences. And with the will there was a letter, which the two read together. Dick's prophetic soul told him of the contents beforehand. Aunt Lavinia is going to crow over me," he said, hastily. did, and nevertewlvolla and WTO but I know -acAidunttriLamavpinhia ,ce, for you only read this obey me, my dear Patty, ow that he ,edding-day, and when you Yourrtainl yh wife of that mulish nleopelexedwiof mine, lheatsterdoneen syoaanio.w,74, I knew that d yeo: wheiniml ages are the tdheear, ago, and if I was arive, it was only because I wishe4 to assure myself that you would make Dick a good wife. Not that Dick deserves such an one, as dof five fia thousandui quite sur eathlastar he is so obstinate. He always declined to obey tnhoe• even ysibmlee'lons kept. him, from what he thinks.' is his own way. Well, in spite of being dead, I have forced him to obey me, and to marry the girl whom .I trained 80 carefully to be his wife. My knowledge of human nature in general and of Dick's nature ' particular, has en- abled me to carry out this little your husband how .he feels now—" The reader broke off to look at Dick. M husband," she d aske solemnly, "how do you feel now ?" " I feel," said the happy bridegroom with deep emotion, " that Aunt Lavinia, has scored a bull's-eve, and that I am properly sold." " Are you sorry Dick took her on his knee to assure her with many kisses that he was in a state of blissful joy, which rendered earth heaven. Piwo thousand a year," said Dick, " a nice country estate and the prettiest and loveliest and jolliest and altogether most scrumptious wife in the world. And—" " What are you listening to, Dick ?" asked Patty looking at his uplifted finger with surprise. " Hush !" said the bridegrooM mysterious- ly, " can't you hear Aunt Lavinia's ghost Chuckling</p>
