        <head>Fat People Should Eat No Late Meal.</head>
        <p>Fat People Should Eat No Late Meal.</p>
        <p>Dr. Galisch reduces the weight successfo corpulent patients by a simple measure, "Science Siftings." lie modifies the am° food taken after mid-day. By this metbot patient has less opportunity to build night time. The appetite receives satisfs' at breakfast find noonday dinner. For 811,1 one small sandwich and a cup of tea or ce', allowed. Work or exercise is prescribe'. the afternoons. Of the patients treated 19 manner, all lost one or two pounds a.: There was no weakening.</p>
        <p>A Man Eats Fifty-four .Tons. An English scientist with a passion for ties has been computing the amount of foc the average man eats in a lifetime. He that in the process of attaining his Orel</p>
        <p>years and ten he eats about fifty-four to° solid food and fifty-three tons of liquid. will have eaten 12,000 eggs, 400 pounds of about four tons of fish, and, could it have all baked at once, a loaf of bread equal øj to an ordinary family totel. But think (I vegetables devoured ! Our scientist says three miles long would be required to 1-0" life's supply to the average man.</p>
        <p>Pulled Out His Own Teeth. An inmate of the lunatic asylum at Chi Prance, had the obsession that his I/ smelled so bad that no girl would accept t' a husband. Brooding over this totally 0, ary defect, he decided that his teeth must l cause of it. lie abstracted a forceps frol surgeon's ease, and, when he found hi alone, carefully pulled out all his teeth. Wounds healed rapidly, and the poor 1:13 confessed that the operation had been painful. Antiquity of the Hammer.</p>
        <p>Hammers have been used from the el times of pre-historic existence, for the of the stone age, the moment they use block of stone to chip into shape another had at once elaborated -the earliest he Then, with a hole drilled roughly throng] which was fitted a bit of branch as a hami have practically the same tool as is used in thousands of ways. With the inere civilisation, trifling improvements were but to all intents and purposes, the smith'f mer of to-day is the same article as means of which our pre-historic foref fashioned their rough article's of furnituil the chase: 18,680,776 . Horse-power.</p>
