      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>ST. HELENS' DRINK FACILITIES.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS' DRINK FACILITIES.</p>
        <p>Alderman J. Forster presided at the Brewster Sessions for the St. Helens Petty Sessional Division on Tuesday. Superintendent WLean, of l'rescot, reported that there were 24 'licensed victuallers, one beerseller, and two other licensees in the dis- trict—two fewer than last year. There were no registered clubs. The population was 8,854, and there was a licence to every 327 persons... Forty-one persons were proceeded against for drunkenness, an increase of seven. On August 12th last the licensee of the Derby Arms, Rain- ford, was proceeded against for permitting drunkenness, but the eases were dismissed on payment of costs. The licensee of the Griffin Inn, Bold, had also been prosecuted, but the cases dismissed. The houses generally had been well conducted. The Gerard Arms, Moss Bank, and the Old House at Home beerhoitse, Ecelestom had been closed on compensation. Alterations had been carried out to the Star Painford. • The Ultairnian said that the Bench proposed to renew all the licences.</p>
        <p>While being shaved at Ashford, Kent. on Sat- llrdaY, Mr. W. Jarvis, captain of the local fire brigade, heard 'the call to a fire, and imme- diately left the shop with half his face covered with lather to attend to his duty.</p>
        <p>Fires last year cost London ,L 339,080 less than the annual cost of the fire brigade.</p>
