      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>GALLANT YOUTHS' " STIFF TASK.</head>
        <p>THE GALLANT YOUTHS' " STIFF TASK.</p>
        <p>By</p>
        <p>Northern Inent Union circles were agog with excite- "es the draw for the first round of the This was madam Tuesday, and Warring- ton have been allotted a' match with Batley at Wilderspool. laT:viee Warrington have defeated the " Gal- norkf' Yeatho " this season, once at Mount Pleas- ..;," and later at Wilderspool, and I see no reason tATY they should not be, able to add a third vie- rY to their total. teem. all round Warrington is the stronger h.oThe forwaxds are much superior to those eY in both the tight and the loose. In as and Young we have a couple of half- -0,7-is quite able to hold their own with the poo—i'eY Pair. The threequarters of the Wilders- be'" ekle Are equal to, and, personally, I think, then the Mount Pleasant quartet, and .te stronger at back. eirt4th these advantage's and the additional one WaZing at home I cannot see any reason why rohn—egton should not walk into the second hot -lc: °f the competition. Over-confidence must e allowed to develop, for the "Youths" ha great Cul) tie fighters, and if this does not PPen victory seems assured.</p>
        <p>firLANDS' STIFF TASK. 4Ylands' have done particularly well in t"•arlf reaching the first round of the competi- -I°4 Proper, end if they manage to pull through (heir match to-day with Wigan tfighfield, they ni figure ag-ainst St. Helens at St. Helens. The critical point in their Cup tie career centres first r all On to-day's match. Whoever wins can ardlY be expected to defeat the Saints upon their /I Own A d p ' itchstill I should like to see s' have the opportunity of making an 14teDiPt in that 'direction. Of the other ties, lluddersfield has one of the softest things on to defeat Swinton Park, wher- sver the match is played, although the draw ilas given the Park choice of ground. Runcorn has been drawn against Bradford, 'lt home, and with the Yorkshire club on the down grade, it is possible to expect the "young 'llen" of Runcorn to secure a draw if not a nn. Widnes have Dewsbury to meet on the 4stoe pitch, and despite the probable presence °,.t Todd and Renwick, the last late of Warring- "ll. the " Chemicals ' should pull through.</p>
        <p>PROBABLE VICTORS. beThe teams set in black type should prove win- till's lb thp matches which will be played ea th: 28th February. Matches to be played on " ground of the first-named. Tarrington v. Batley. t• Helens v. Wigan Highfield or Rylands'. Ocorn V. Bradford. wid latttnes v. Dewsbury. v. Halifax. tli„j• Salford. teed " v. Featherstone Rangers. s lf•ii Xeighley. - 04 cast, v. Hull Kingston Rovers. %h—ae'ord v. Wigan. 8 -ale Hornets v. TTunslet. 131*„:.ton Park V. Huddersfield. 'ow v. Broughton Rangers. YeLgil v. Oldham. V. Deresham or Classon. wakefield Trinity v. Swinton.</p>
        <p>STUBBORN GAME AT LEEDS.</p>
        <p>/ainteth Gives A Capital Display as a</p>
        <p>the picture, and he was within an ace of scoring when Will Davies intervened and the Welsh- man's weight was too much for the little three- quarter. The Wildetspool forwards next took their share in the game, and some of their rushes were extremely dangerous, only they were not followed up until the scoring point. There was not the finish about the forwards that we have seen in other 'matches. Had there been, Lewis would not have been able to keep ,his goal-line intact. Within the last quarter of an hour the War- rington men played desperately hard to pull down the score. This they failed to do, and the final came with Leeds victors by two tries (six points) to nil.</p>
        <p>CENTRE THREEQUARTERS WEAK. Taken as a whole, Warrington played a better game than was anticipated from their weakened position. Jolley's absence was most marked. Dainteth played a good game in his new position as a full-back, and some of his tackling work was brilliant, only he was not up to the Leeds tactics as Jolley would have been. This does not cast any reflection upon Dainteth, for he played perhaps better in the back posi- tion than any other man in the team. The weak spot in the Warrington side was at centre threequarter. Tranter and Dickenson had not the speed to keep up with Ruddick and Waywell, and, consequently, when the wing men made runs they were not sufficiently backed up: 'As tacklers, however, Dickenson and Tranter did good work. Young and Nicholas were quite as good as the two Leeds half-backs, only Nicholas was slower than usual in getting the ball away. The forwards all did well and wheeled the scrums adroitly. Clare, Thomas and Chester were perhaps more in evidence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>Full-back.</head>
        <p>Full-back.</p>
        <p>F°4 WARDS KEEP DOWN THE SCORE. A 5 / ventured to predict in last issue, only a majority of points won the match at .eaa • leY Leeds were fortunate to gain the SiZ Were points to nil in the first half, and t eNen Morp lucky in preventing Warring- scoring in the second portion, when vvilderspool men were undoubtedly playing knob A.. ". the better game. Taking the play all ;,"r• Leeds may well deem themselves fortu- "ate in winning. It Was a clean and open game, marked with a onsiderable array of incidents, and was just ,„1:1,_ the type which appealed to the spectators, "40 numbered some 9,000.</p>
        <p>ALTERNATE ATTACKS. `'d3oth sides put in spells of useful attacking. were seen to greater advantage in the first ;wenty minutes, but after that Warrington got `llto their proper stride, and then matters be- LI"Ile still more balanced, and had Warrington ..:*ll given a tithe, 'of the -Leediauck a draw ?kid Probably have ensued. home threequarters were particularly in the earlier part, but they were up ranist a stubborn proposition in Waywell, v4di,ok and Dainteth, all three of whom s, tliZe,a a sterling defensive game. Soon after a, `ali the home threequarters"got away with n0..-“lliant . got • on and was speeding for the line, when bit of passing. Will Davies n dashed after the Welshman.. w" a rare trial...of speed. Waywell, how- prom Y pt] overhauled his man and •I&gt;avf,"Y ended the run* by bringing, down sdiel goon after Campbell got on the run. tile on , was his trail and the "little un Nri very cleverly.</p>
        <p>• Warr. INE HANDLING GAME. gare.,, thgton with this began to wk the t;" "etter display of the handlingaep section u "o d tf Ia tjt4le than they have done for some etrimiore.. 4. bacil.:`ork Leeds, however, were the sup.iiter.:l '"Dteet, Placed pass by Nicholas was 1 dashed away for the we "3' Campbell, and he dash c, __,_ 4is earlifiton line. Dainteth could not aees out. ~eer and Campbell planted a try half, way Tho "ailley failed with the kick. , with AT airington forwards next came along _ knd Mille fiLewis footwork, only they found 1, l , this e threequarters at the top of form, and, lleualYl° of i)lay was not so successful as o 1.11 [sides, 4 Is. More b fine passing took place on ti (Itref,,,a•ild in this work Warrington were 50 so Le -' and effective ecis s ' t thelar Deetators on more than one occasion gave (.1 a cheer. who was playing a clever game for Rave,'l", Made an opening for, Campbell, who I.4tt ultimate possession to Sanders, and the ella4aliailding to Ganley in the nick of time, lliee , the last-named half-back to notch a :thus ‘ry. W. Davies failed with the kick and tillse elided the last of the Leeds scoring. Half- "ma with the home men leading by two , Csi Points) to nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON FAIL TO SCORE. 'he second period was undoubtedly in favour thitWarrington, and they managed to do every- "eePt score. Ruddick often came into</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>SWINTON AT WILDERSPOOL. Will the." Lions" be Defeated?</head>
        <p>SWINTON AT WILDERSPOOL. Will the." Lions" be Defeated?</p>
        <p>Warrington team have a their first. visit to Wilderspool toanndnaasy to wipe out, they ought to • give us nasty defeat to-clay,Bwin displays. one of their be f!t At Swinton in September the Wilderspool the W ith a surprise defeat of nine points men met w , . Since then Swinton, like WarringtoA, tim7(l)iad their up,s. and downs. Their position in the League table s below that of Warring- nutbt he latter have played two ... more Tim teams are fairly well matched, games. but I am inclined to think that another victory will have to be recorded for the home side. The corresponding match last year resulted in a 13_12 points, victory for Warrington, ,but this time they ought to win more handsomely. - Warrington will once more turn up a full side. Jolley, who, as "The . Examiner " exclusively announced last week, was injured, has recovered sufficiently to return to hie position at full-back. Dainteth will accordingly go on to the inside right threequarter as partner to Ruddick. Skel- home, who was injured some weeks, ago, hilis• now _recovered, and will turn out with the for- wards Ace more. The sidi3s will be:- -</p>
        <p>Warrington. Jolley. Waywell, Dickenson, Dainteth, Ruddick. Nicholas, Young. Cox, Thomas, Fearnley, Cheaters, Skelhorne, Clare.</p>
        <p>Smith.</p>
        <p>Bailey, Cullis, Dale, Priee, Sharpies, Pollitt, Heaton, Once. Anderson, Ryder, Leah, Winstanley. Frodsham. Swinton.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A's " NARROW ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>NVigan IligWield Create a Surprise.</p>
        <p>By "lAKEIANDER." The visit of tothWe iflacdetrstpohaotl on s this smart organisation from the Colliery townt Saturday asWigiannter ilesitgihnfig elbdy are due at Gorsey-lane this afternoon to ry conclusions with Rylands' Recreation, in the fifth qualifying round of the N.U. Cup. Judg- ing them on the display given against Warring- ton "A," they should have no difficulty in putting "paid" to the account of the Gorsey- lane men, as they proved themselves to be a really nippy lot, and, in my opinion, were unfortunate ,in returning home with only a division of the Points—the result of a hard game being two tries (six points) each. A draw of two tries each was not in keeping with the opportunities presented to both sides to augment the score, but no doubt the strong wind which was blowing mitigated against accurate passing. The Wilderspool lads had the advantage of this wind in the firSt half, but they failed to make use of it. Hi hfield were often dangerous, and both fore and aft were much smarter than the home lot. The only score in the first half fell to Wigan Higfifie ld, after a bad mistake on the part of Page and Brookes, of which one of the visiting forwards was not slow in taking advantage of.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>HIGHFIELD'S FINE PASSING.</head>
        <p>HIGHFIELD'S FINE PASSING.</p>
        <p>In the second half Critchley was not long before he equalised. matters with a smart try, but he failed to add the extra points. Baker was the next to get over, after receiving a smart pass from Arnold, but Critchley found the wind too troublesome to steer the hall .between the uprights, although he made a fine attempt. Highfield tried hard to break down the defence, and some of their passing was really clever, and once they ought to have scored from a penaltY Hart in front of goal. Towards the end, how- ever, their efforts were successful, for they grounded, the ball at the corner, but they, like Warrington, could not get the ball over the bar. Warrington's chances of being Combination champions are now very remote indeed. They certainly are to be sympathised with in not being able to play their regular team.. Undoubt- edly J. Brown is sadly missed in the full-back position. A trial was given in this position to 3o Page, who was unearthed during the strike, but as a full-back I don't think he is a success. He can kick a good length, but lacks judgment and dallies too long. Alec Brown, too, is another player Warrington "A" can ill-afford to have out of harness. He and Douglas understand each other's play so well, and Douglas seems "all at sea" without him Not that he and Brookes do not play well to- gether, for on Saturday they were quite equal to the nippy Highfield pair. Douglas, however, stuck to the ball too long at times. and ought to have plied his " threes" more. Brookes again showed what a tower of strength he is, and more</p>
        <p>than one of the Highfield lads can bear testi- mony to this. :Both. in ,attack and.defence he was ever to the fore, and was easily the most useful man on his side.</p>
        <p>STEEL GIVES PROMISE. Steel was again tried on the wing and wa* anything but a passenger, and at times put iX some useful runs. .Baker once, more made good use of his speed and played a sound game ou the other wing, and this time his efforts were rewarded with a try. Shaw was a good centre, but Brown was again rather weak. The forwards could not compare with the Highfield sextet, who were masters in the tight as well as in-the loose, though Arnold and Critchley were continually in the picture. Pouncey, the new man, also did well in the loose and was often seen' heading a forward rush.</p>
        <p>As I said before, the Highfield possess a nippy lot of lads, and, taken on the whole, their backs were far smarter than the Wilderspool men, and their passing at times was really brilliant. If they keep last Saturday's form up this after- noon, I think they will be altogether too smart for the Gorsey-lane organisation.</p>
        <p>• WARRINGTON "A" GO TO SWINTON. Bramhall Re-appears at Full-back. , The "A" men have rather a stiff game on hand at Swinton, where they meet 'the Junior "Lions," but there is a sporting chance that they may bring home a victory. A. Bramhall re-appears at full-back, and Tranter goes into the threequarter line. J. Brown has recovered from injury, and re-appears at half-back. The Warrington side will be :--- A. Bramhall; Baker, Tranter, Shaw, Steele; J. Brown, Brookes; Naylor, Harmer, Critchley, Green, Bevan, Bradshaw.</p>
        <p>MU. CUP FOR SCHOOLBOYS. One Warrington Team in Competition. The South-West Lancashire Cap, for which the senior N.U. clubs of Widnes, Wigan, War- rington, St. Helens, Runcorn and Leigh have for many years annually competed, has been handed over for competition amongst the schoolboy teams of the same six towns. The new competition starts during the course of the present month, and at a committee meet- ing the draw was made as follows : ‘First round :—Wigan v. Widnes, at Wigan, February 28th or March 4th. Warrington, St. 'Helens, arid Runcorn byes. • Second round :—St. Helens v. Runcorn, at St. Helens, Wigtin or Widnes v. Warrington, on thg ground of the firstnamed. The dates in the second round are to be mutually agreed upon by the contestants. Mr. Charles, Widnes, . has been appointed secretary.</p>
        <p>WORKSHOP COMPETITION. The Warrington Club's Arrangements. _</p>
        <p>The Warrington Club again intend to run a Work4' Competition on similar lines to previous years. Entries close Tuesday, March 17th, and the draw will be made on the following day. Forms of application may be obtained from the Honorary Secretary, Mr. T. P. Bradshaw. 3, Alexandra-street, or at the ground on Wed- nesday evenings and Saturday afternoons.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' CUP-TIE TO-DAY. _ _ A Fine Game May Ensue._</p>
        <p>Rylands' have a stiff game on hand in the sth qualifying round of the N.U. Cup, at Gorsey- lane, when they meet Wigan Ilighfield. They are turning out the strongest side, and there is a sporting chance of Rylands' winning. The team will be:—</p>
        <p>F. Horrocks ; T. Laws, S,Tarter, A. Horrocks. and P. Morris; G. Warren and J. Gallop; A Stretford (captain), J. Whittaker, F. Mason,• J. Brandwood, H. Morris and D. Slater.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>eRD TIND DELAGE eTIRS.</head>
        <p>eRD TIND DELAGE eTIRS.</p>
        <p>Douglas, Budge, 8.5. A,, Sunbeam, and Bradbury Motor Cycles. SOl-e _. District Agent:---Vi. KNIGHT 7 Stockton Heath Garage, TQlerohone 63. lO9, LONDON ROAD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>PICKS OUT OF THE SCRUM.</head>
        <p>PICKS OUT OF THE SCRUM.</p>
        <p>Pithy Pars About N.U. Games.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>eeeeee</head>
        <p>eeeeee</p>
        <p>It was not so bad at Leeds. Still Warrington had hard luck. In the second half they had By far the best of the play, And could do everything but score. Ruddick nuide a gallant attempt To cross the Leeds goal-line, but Will Davies's weight proved far Too heavy for the little threequarter. It was only quid pro quo, for Ruddick neatly grassed the Leeds Plater earlier,in the game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>,eC e e C 415</head>
        <p>,eC e e C 415</p>
        <p>Dainteth was a dainty back. He was, however, not quite so Jolly as Tolley usually proves. Still he did good work there. Nicholas did not pass out The ball with his customary precision. One—a cumbersome pass—resulted In Leeds getting away and scoring. Young was spasnaodic in his game. At times he was brilliant, and On other occasions he was weak. • Young is not quite up to form.</p>
        <p>cc 0 0 Waywell was in lively form and He often got away well. but Backing was not given him as . It might have been by his partner. Ruddick had the same trouble on The right wing, although there were Some good bouts of passing indulged in. The forwards wheeled the scrums nicely. Unfortunately the advantage was often Lost through the smartness of the Leeds halves. Clare was once more in evidence, and So were Thomas and Chesters• in the front.</p>
        <p>C e e 0-0 e The "A" men got a surprise. Wigan Bighfield played a rattling game. Rylands' will have to go all the way To beat them in the N.I.T. Cup to-day; Douglas held on to the ball too long. He evidently misses his old partner—Brown. Brookes showed himself a hard worker, But he does not balance with Douglas So well as does A. Brown, the other half. Shaw is now playing a good game. , C. Brown is not up to past form. Baker is one of the best of the "A" side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>NATIVES OF CHESHIRE IN LONDON.</head>
        <p>NATIVES OF CHESHIRE IN LONDON.</p>
        <p>Proposed Society. Major Louis A. Newton, writing to "The Times" from 25, College-hill, Cannon-street. E.C., calls attention to the fact that there are to-day 30 English county associations in London, but the name of the county of Cheshire is not to be found amongst them. He points out the benefits derived from the existence of such societies, and adds that the English County Societies' Conference (to which 20 of the county societies are affiliated) hag promised him every assistance in the formation of a Cheshire Asso- ciation for London. He finds from the census returns for 1911 that there are 3,581 males and 4,274 females natives of Cheshire, who are resi- dent in the administrative county of London, an excellent nucleus, he observes, for mem- befship of a county society. He would be glad to receive the names of any natives of the county who, favour the inauguration of such a move- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>BUTCHERS AT PLAY.</head>
        <p>BUTCHERS AT PLAY.</p>
        <p>Warrington Win a Stiff Game by Two</p>
        <p>Clear Points.</p>
        <p>Much interest was taken in the charity foot- ball match on Thursday afternoon, when the Warrington and St. -Helens Butchers were in opposition on the Wilderspool Ground. The Mayor and Mayoress were present. Teams : Warrington Mynekylne; Peet, Grounds, Littlewood and Cross; Emmett and Baty; Holland, Taylor, She pherd, Harrison, Mathei and Duckworth,</p>
        <p>_ St. Helens—Hatton; R. Fitzpatrick, Jones, Houlton and Dobbs; Peet and Ford; A. Gilvaray (captain), Pemberton, F. ~Gilvaray, Burns, Turner and W. Fitzpatrick. Referee, Mr. Ennion, Wigan. The Mayor kicked off for St. Helens before about 1,000 sPectators. Early on the War- 'rington lads became aggressive, and, twice in as many minutes the visiting full-back was obliged to touch down. Warrington were awarded a free-kick, but Baty shot wide. Grounds was remarkably fleet of foot, and after running a quarter of the length of the field and passing several opponents, kicked when hard pressed, but an opponent tipped-the ball dead. Rain began to fall freely and the lot of the amateurs was not an enviable one. Some ex- cellent passing was given by the Warrington threequarters, but Grounds passed forward. Hatton was next prominent in eluding three of his opponents, and .getting the ball away. The visitors were offside, ind from the. resulting penalty kick Raffty obtained a really excellent goal. Interval: Warrington, I goal (2 points); St. Helens, nil.</p>
        <p>THE SECOND HALF. When near the line Littlewood knocked for- ward and spoiled a good opening. The ball was immediately transferred by a long kick to the Warrington end, where Mynekyme was tackled in possession. Owing to the greasy nature of the ground it was difficult for the players tG keep their feet. Grounds made a neat kick, which found touch in the visitors' quarters. Peet made an excellent attempt, but his efforts were futile, through lack of support. Myne- kyme was to the fore in following up his kick and regaining the ball, after bouncing off one of the, visitors, but he was overwhelmed through numbers. There was a melee on thbeSt. Helens line, when the home team were near scoring, but they fumbled. Raffty made another shot at goal, which' only just missed. The visitors were awarded a penalty kick, but nothing re- sulted. ' Mynekyme mulled, but the ball rolled into touch. From the serum R. Fitzpatrick scored an unimproved try amid a bunth of players. From a melee Shepherd put War- rington in front by a try. Result: Winrrington, goal 1 try (5 points); St. Helens, 1 try (3 points). AN ENJOYABLE SMOKER.</p>
        <p>_ After the match the players, officials and friends were entertained at the Norton Arms. Justice was done to the splendid repast pro- vided, which was followed by a smoking con- cert. Councillor Archer apologised on behalf of the Mayor, whose official duties prevented him being present. Mr. Blashill and Mr. Brown, both of St. Helens, spoke on behalf of the visitors; and thanked the butchers of War- rington very heartily for the splendid reception they had accorded them, and expressed the hope that the rivalry of that day would long continue to be of the same friendly and sportsmanlike spirit. Mr. Bowcock suitably responded and also moved ,vote of thanks to the Warrington Club far their kinaness placing the ground, jerseys; &amp;c., at the disposal of the two teams. As representing the Football .Club, Mr. T. p. Bradshaw assured them that. the pleasure was mutual, and stated that-if at. any future time the butchers organised another match, having for its object the benefit of the Infirmary 01 other suitable institution, his committee Would always be prepared to do all in their power to make the affair a success. Humorous and musical items were contri- buted by friends from St. Helens and Warring- ton.est t aThenks lerf on e p o setvearnydH Hostess t, were and after givent the b r Mr. Lodge had replied and assured them that the pleasure was his, the party dispersed, amid mutual expressions of goodwill and the hopes of another meeting in the future. In every respect the gathering was a success and every- thing passed off in a delightful manner.</p>
        <p>iuld</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S "WHITE HOPES."</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S "WHITE HOPES."</p>
        <p>FATE OF THREE LOCAL BOXERS.</p>
        <p>The search for a "white hope" to endeavour to win back the boxing championships from One. W. Monks,negro pugilists led to a contest being held in If a nscehnets ttehrr eoen" Monw h itdeaht, when Warring- tonny 0 p nie hopes. . ,o knocked out his man, and the second, Tom Thomas, was so badly punished that he had to give up at the seventh round, while W. Tabener also met a similar fate. The majority of the contestants fought with rare pluck, but only two or ' three gave any promise. W. Monks, of Warrington, displayed the greatest skill. He uses his 1 :t very cleverly and has a good defence. He I ocked out his opponent, J. Butterworth (Pen leton), with a fine left hook. Monks, who is 20 Years of age, sft. 11iin. and list. 101 b., has a good chance of reaching the final bout.- , ,</p>
        <p>so severely pun- ished Tom Thomas, that the referee stopped TOM THOMAS BADLY BEATEN i. the town n dW , fight n the seventh round of a contest which was to go ten rounds. Wilde, a thin, but wiry a world'silde, the c h cleveram pi of !fl y weight oflors- , little chap, only 7st. in weight, Was conceding between 181 b. and 201 b. to Thomas, a neatly built boy. The Warrington youth fought very gamely, but he was no match for the champion, who hits remarkably hard for one so small. Quick footed and very cunning, he is a marvellous little boxer. In the first round he knocked Thomas to the ground with a right swinger on the jaw, and twice in the second round the War- rington youth went down to be counted to nine. Wilde's fist came in contact with Thomas's face with such force that the bigger boy was knocked against the ropes and he rebounded face down- wards—rather a -nasty fall. But he got up pluckily. Thomas reached Wilde's jaw with a clever left in the third round. but the fight was going all one way, and again Thomas was floored, to get up at seven. Thomas was bleeding from the mouth in the fourth round, and in the fifth Referee Gamble found it necessary to adminis- ter a gentle rebuke to both. When Thomas knocked Wilde on to the ropes hi the sixth round the crowd cheered the Lan- cashire boy, but with his face covered with blood Thomas looked dazed early in the seventh round, and his seconds wisely stepped in at the instigation of the referee, when he was floored with another severe blow in the face.</p>
      </div>
