        <p>5X3'704C111L.X.4 CCP WI 11 3/E M4l" V M "ir //M IEO lEtnr 3FUE JM Mt Mt.</p>
        <p>MATCHES OFF FROM STRANGE CAUSES</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>Did the Weaver Refinery Get Lost? What Happened to the Emmanuel Old Boys?</head>
        <p>Did the Weaver Refinery Get Lost? What Happened to the Emmanuel Old Boys?</p>
        <p>UNSATISFACTORY VICTORIA PARK ARAANGEMENTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>By 4 4 Linesman."</head>
        <p>By 4 4 Linesman."</p>
        <p>On Orford St. John's ground at Long-lane the semi-final fie in the Widnes Minor Medal Com- petition between Bank Quay Recs. and St. Bar- nabas' ended in a drawn game, each side scor- ing once. The display of both, teams was below the average League match form.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District League there were only two fixtures, the most interesting being fixed for Weston. The Weaver secretary wrote to the Weston secretary notifying him that their team were going to travel, and at he appointed time for starting the home 'players and referee were ready to-go on the field, but the Weaver Refiners failed to put in an appearance, and this caused a great disappoint- ment. The Emmanuel Old Boys and Fairfield should have • met in. the Victoria Park, but owing, to ' some dispute between the Old- Boys' secretary and the players, the pitch they usually play on was occupied by two other teams, and pre- vented this match from being played.</p>
        <p>'shooting. The game became fast and exciting, and each goal in turn was severely tested. A. Woodhouse hit the upright when a score ap- peared pertain. Sinclair offended by foul play, and Woodhouse, taking the free kick, forced a corner, which was unproductive. The Wesley-. ails now changed the scene of play, and had it not been for smart work by Hill the United goal would have fallen. From a free kick the visitors' goal was placed in danger, but Jacks°. caused the homesters to fall back to their own lines. S. Woodhouse by a good effort put his side in front a fetv minutes before time, and the remaining portion was keenlk contested, both goalkeepers being called upon, but no score was recorded, and when the whistle blew the result was: Fairfield United, two goals; Bewsey ,Wesleyans, one goal. ,</p>
        <p>play Bowdon Vale in the North Cheshire League at Thelvrall-lene with the following: Kite; Res- pell and Robinson; Downs, Webb and Carter; Smith, P. Webb, Lees, Fogg,. Siddall and Owen. The following players will represent Stockton Heath against Crosfield's Reserve: Simcock, Sankey and Gosling; Gifford, Denny and Marsh; Smith, Lockhart, Jones, Dunne and Stubbs.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Reserve will play Widnes St. Paul's, at Widnes, with the following; Harri- son; Jeffersonvand,„ Houghtori;, Magill, Wood- ward and Mortimer; Woodhead, Sankey, Welsby, Rigby and Isherwood.</p>
        <p>Great Sankey play Gossage's Recs., at Ditton New-road, Widnes, with: J. Bond; E. Glover and R. Stephens; H. Ditchfield, A. Gardner and H. Payne; E. Darlington, T. Illidge, J. Ditch- field, S. Poulter and A. Houghton.</p>
        <p>Padgate United play Army Service Corps. at Longford, with: Nork; Warburton and Sankey; Hodkinson, Wadsworth (capt.) and Williams; Critchley, Nunnerley, Gilbert, S. Hardman and James Houghton. Emmanuel Old Boys meet Orford St. John's at Orford with: O'Brien; Eccles and Harrison; .Wainwright, Hatton and Roberts; Brooks, Millington, Isherwood, Caldwell and Gregory. • Emmanuel Old Boys' Reserve 'play Elmwood- avenue Congregational, at Victoria Park, with a team selected from: Knight, Trainor, Brime- low, Day, Carroll. Robinson, Archer, Ashton, Balshaw. Green, Edelston, Hankinson, Rigby, Jones, Houghton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>AN ARRANGEMENT BROKEN.</head>
        <p>AN ARRANGEMENT BROKEN.</p>
        <p>In the earlier part of the season the arrange- ment was that the allotted playing pitches should be agreed to by a certain number of clubs, who arranged to play alternately, but latterly this rule has been rudely broken, and the present custom with regard to plying pitches in the Park appears to be first com€l first served, which means that some clubs in order to obtain a pitch have to pay some lads a few coppers to take a ball on to a certain playing piece; and thus take possession of it until they come later in the afternoon.</p>
        <p>Great Sankey were also amongst the disap- pointed ones ,last , week, They had a friendly fixture with Muspratt's. from `Widnes, and the latter wired their match off on the Saturday morning. The Warrington Adult School should have made the journey to play the Liverpool Farm School team, but as only three or four turned aP at the railway station, and not being sure of the remaining plaYers following, there ,was no match played.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Crosfield's v. Oughtrington United.</head>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Crosfield's v. Oughtrington United.</p>
        <p>Owing to the unfit state of the former club's ground at Folly-lane, this match was decided on the Thelwall-lane ground, the Latchford Com- mittee having offered the use of their ground to the Crosfield's Club in order to help them to play their League fixtures as arranged. The game started before a moderate attendance of supporters, and was evenly contested in mid- field, but in front of goal the United were more deadly with their shooting, while the "Soap" representatives failed lamentably when easy chances presented themselves. At. the interval the United were leading by three goals to nil. The second portion was more in favour of Crosfield's, but they could not penetrate the United defence, while the United added to their score by the aid of a penalty hick, which was converted by Thoinasson. It Was to be re- gretted that during the closing stages the United resorted to shady play, and the specta- tors showed_ their disapproval of the tactics the United ,adopted whenever they were beaten by an opponent or their goal was in danger of , falling. This ;spoilt what had in the earlier stages been a well-contested game. The result was: Oughtrington United, four goals; Cros- field's, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>WIDNES MINOR MEDAL COMPETITION. Semi-Final Tie. Bank Quay Recs. v. St. Barnabas'.</head>
        <p>WIDNES MINOR MEDAL COMPETITION. Semi-Final Tie. Bank Quay Recs. v. St. Barnabas'.</p>
        <p>_ The above met on the Orford St John's ground to decide their tie before a very fair number of spectators. The opening exchanges were evenly contested, after which the Recs• gained possession' in the Barnabas' terrifory, but were met by. a good defence, green, on the left, being the most prominent forwaid for the Recs. at this time, and his centres were well met by Long, who planted the ball well up the field. The Barnabas' forwards could make no head- way against Worrell and Lagar, and the Recs.' forwards were soon in a dangerous position. A free kick for obstruction relieved the Barnabas' defence. After a spell of even play, Deakin passed out to Eccleston, who worked for posi- tion and opened the score with a good shot, which completely beat Shaw. St. Barnabas' now played up with more energy, and Vernon, after beating ,his opponent, placed out to War- ham, and from his centre Smith conceded a corner. The flag kick was poorly taken, and the play was transferred to the Barnabas' end again. Green placed their goal in danger, but Long and then Spruce cleared with some fine kicks. The Varnabas' forwards nearly got through owing to Smith miskicking, when wor_ rall nipped in and robbed Vernon at the critical moment, and a long shot by Maddock went wide of the uprights. Following this the Recs. made another attack on their ,opponents' goal, and a fine shot from Deakin was tipped over the bar by Shaw. The ensuing corner proved of no 'ad- vantage, and half-time arrived with the Recs. leading by one goal to none. The Recs. got into the Barnabas' goal area in surprising fashion; Long camp to the rescue, and the ball travelled very quickly to the oppo- site end. Berry gave a corner away, and this proved of no use to St. Barnabas', but the play was now chiefly at the Recs.' end, and corner kicks were very frequent. Maddock, on several occasions, placed some very fine flag kicks for St. Barnabas', and from one of these Warham headed a fine goal and made the scores equal. Play now beceine very keen and exciting., the Recs. trying hard to force the St. Barnabas' defence, but Long was in fine form and always held the upper hand. At the opposite end Bushell and his back S were soon busy again, and from a long shot by Vernon the Recs.' goal had a narrow escape, Bushell just managing to put the ball round the post. This was got away, and the scene of action was transferred to the opposite end. Green forced a corner off Glover, which came to nothing. Off-side against St. Barnabas' spoiled a good chance to them, and several ineffectual attempts at both goals proved 'fruitless, 'and the game ended hr.a draw of one. goal each. Players—Bank Quay Recs.: Bushell; Berry and Smith; Worrell, Lagar and Chesters; Eccleston, Deakin, Bradshaw, Davies and Green. St. Barnabas': ShaW; Spruce and Long; Glover, Appleton and Forster; Maddock, Wood, Brown, Vernon and Wareham. Referee, J. Smith.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES. Fairfield United v. Bewsey Wesleyan. The following players turned out in this friendly fixture, on the ground of the former, in , charge of Referee Essenhigh :—Fairfield United: IIill; Rowlands and Jackson; GleiLve, Bebbing- ton. and Skelmardine; S. Woodhouse, A. Wood- house, Allen, Williaras, and. Cole. Bewsey Wes- leyans: Winstanley; Sinclair and Morrison; Howard, Mellor and Howard; T. Kendrick, !Hughes, Ashworth, E. Kendrick and Nurse. The game was 4Rven until the home forwards broke away, but when well placed off-side spoiled their efforts. This relieved the visitors' defence, and following even play E. Kendrick opened the score for the Wesleyans. This reverse roused the United players and the visitors' goal was strongly attacked, but the defense held the fort. At the other end Hill made a very smart save, and the home forwards transferred play to the Bewsey goal, where A. Woodhouse made the scores equal. The visitors now tried hard to regain the lead, and after Hill had saved a good shot, one of the home backs handled inside the area. E. Kendrick took the kick, but failed to score, and at half-time the score_was one goal each. On re-starting the visitors made the first at- tack, and chances were thrown away by bad</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE. Stockton Heath Reserve v. Widnes Wesley Institute.</head>
        <p>WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE. Stockton Heath Reserve v. Widnes Wesley Institute.</p>
        <p>On the West Avenue ground. The home for- wards were the first to become aggressive, and from a good centre by Woodhead, Welsby, the home centre, opened the score. Even play fol- lowed, and for a while the visitors had their full share of the game. From a grand centre by the extreme right, Jefferson failed to clear, and the ball was put in the net, the visitors thus equalising. From now both teams played keenly, and each defence was called upon to clear. Just before half-time Welsby scored again for the home team. The second portion was evenly contested, and each side missed op- portunities of scoring. Result: Stockton Heath Reserve, two goals; Widnes Wesley Institute, one goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <head>CUP AND MEDAL COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>CUP AND MEDAL COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>- - At a sub-committee meeting of the Warring- ton and District League, held in the Working Men's Cafe, Horsemarket-street, the Secretary reported that he had received a communication from the Liverpool County Assosiation, sane- tioning the rules for the Minor Medal Competi- tion, which the coinmittee proposed' to have for the junior clubs in Warrington and district area. The rules and regulations governing the competition may be had by sending application to the Secretary, Mr. It. Drandreth, Orford, Warrington, and the next meeting will be held in the Cafe, Horsemarket-street, on Thursday evening, the 19th inst., when all applications will be considered and the draw for the first round will be made, the matches taking place on the 21st and the 28th February. The rules for the open competition for clubs within ten miles of Market Gate, for which there will be a silver cup and medals, are tit the hands of the committee and will, be sent out as early as pos- sible. The cup has been pregentee by' Mr. Starkey, of Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>ASSOCIATION ITEMS.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION ITEMS.</p>
        <p>,n Bank Quay Recs. and St. Barnabas' played a Mrawn game in the Minor Medal semi-final tie at Orford. The result was a fair criterion of the game._ . . _ _</p>
        <p>Both teams made the mistake of keeping the ball too close, instead of swinging it to the wings. Long, of St. Barnabas', was the best player on the field. ("men was the best forward for the nem</p>
        <p>It was from his centre that Eccleston scored their goal. Maddock, for St. Barnabas', was their most dangerous forward. Be is an adept at taking corner kicks. The John's, for preference, would like to meet the Recs. in the final.</p>
        <p>They would then be able to settle their argu- ments as to which has the best junior forward line in Warrington. The Weaver Refiners are following in the foot- steps of another League club by failing to keep their engagements. They caused much disappointment at Weston by not turning up, as arranged at the League meeting. Poulter, the popular inside left of the Great Sankey Club, is now playing with Witton. The Latchford Club's Workshop Competition will soon be in operation. Some of the workshop teams • are getting anxious about it.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. North Cheshire League. Latchford v. Bowdon Vale. Warrington and District League. Adult School v. Daresbury ; J. T. Hadkiss. Crosfield's Recs. v. Stockton Heath; S. Breese. Great Sankey v. Weaver Refiners; G. Rushton. Orford St. John's v. Emmanuel 0.B.; T. Pit- tam. St. Barnabas' v. Bank Quay Bees.; B. Howell. Fairfield. v. Wilderspool Brewers; A., Holt, Widnes and District Alliance. Widnes St. Paul's v. Stockton Heath Reserve.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Tbe Latchford and Lostock Gralam Clubs should have met at Thelwall-lane in the Altrinc- ham Amateur Cup Competition, but owing to the latter club.being.engaged in the Northwich Cup Competition on that date, the tie has been postponed until the first Saturday in March. The Latchford Club secretary has arranged to</p>
