      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD'S HEAVY DEFEAT.</p>
        <p>The first eleven, in their return match with Manchester University at Fallowfield, were de- feated by 7-0. Up to the interval the game was fairly even, though the Manchester for- wards did more attacking-, and Boscolo and Lester scored; Bruhl had also made some good saves: Immediately after half-time the visitors' right hack had to leave the field injured and took no further part in the game. This quite disorganised the Latchford defence and the University forwards pressed continuously. Boscolo played very finely at centre forward, and taking full advantage of many splendid centres by McGill at outside left, and passes from Lester and O'Grady, scored five goals with fast, clean drives. There is no doubt he is a perfect shot. 13rnhl had a busy afternoon and made many grand saves. He had no chance with the scoring shots. Wilkinson and Knight were the best of the Latchford defence, aad Newton and Dawn were the pick of the University defence. The Latchford forwards, for once, were completely subdued.</p>
        <p>Reserve's Fine Win.</p>
        <p>The second eleven met University "A" at Latchford and won by eight goals to two. In fairness to the University team, it should be stated that they played a man short throughout. Furness opened the scoring for Latchford, and. shortly after added a second, after a smart run through the University defence. Up to this point Latchford had monopolised the play, but University came away and soon after scored a rather "tame" goal. Latchford's right wing got going, and a fine run and centre by McCau- ley resulted in Minards netting. Before half- time Furness added a fourth. In the second half Latchford added four more goals, 'the score's being Furness, Marsden, Minards and Banks. Marsden at left inside, and McCauley at outside right, both played a good game,, the latter especially getting in some , fine runs and centres. For the University their centre-half and right inside were the most pro- minent men._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <head>Trowm for- To-day.</head>
        <p>Trowm for- To-day.</p>
        <p>To-day the first eleven meet Mid -Cheshire ai Knutsford, with the following team: I Brnhl; W. Fleteher and C Billington; F. C Newbold, J. B. Wilkinson and A. Knight; F.- W. Isher- wood, .W: S. Furness, J. E. Pearson, R. A. Dale, and H. R. Colton. Umpire, Mr. F. Grounds. 'Bus leaves Bridge Foot 1.30. The second eleven are away to Brooklands, with: G. Mynekyme •. W. T. Eccles and P. Allan- son; W. Starkey, J.Albinson and T. Banks; J. J. McCauley, A. B. Starkey, W. H. Innards, A. G. Marsden, and C. Newbold. Train leaves Arpley Station LIG. Umpire, Mr. H. Eccles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0282" n="art0282">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH BEAT ALDERLEY EDGE.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH BEAT ALDERLEY EDGE.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath visited Alderley Edge at their new ground, Chelford, on Saturday last. The ground was in splendid condition, and efter a - very fast game Stockton Heath 'son by three goals against one. Alderley, playing with a very strong wind, were seen in the visitors' circle, E. Platt shoot- ing wide. The home team continued to press, C. Platt opening the scoring after 10 minutes. The visitors now -got going, Richardson net- ting, but was penalised for "sticks." From the free-hit Hatton intercepted and passed to Bain- bridge, who equalised. Play continued vary fast and both goalkeepers were troubled, Bain- bridge scoring a second. From the bully-ol the visiting forwards started a fine passing game, Bainbridge getting through, but was un- lucky to overrun the ball just on the gcal.iine. Half-time: Stockton Heath, two goals; Alderley Edge, one. Play. in the second half continn.l very fast, both sides playing up strong. Obtaining pos- session near the home "25," E. L. Platt made a fine run, but was unfortunate in overrunning the ball close against the post. After SO Pan- utes Baxter scored a third for Stockton Heath. Alderley now pressed hard, but could not add to their score. Final: Stockton Heath, three goals; Alderley Edge-, one. Last season Stockton Heath won by four goals to oil. Both teams have an open date to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0283" n="art0283">
