      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0288" n="art0288">
        <head>EXAMINER. SATURDAY. FEBRUARY 14, 1914. PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>EXAMINER. SATURDAY. FEBRUARY 14, 1914. PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>Miscellaneous.</p>
        <p>10,000 ROSE TREES, 50,000 Oval Privet, 20,000 Mixed Shrubs. Forest and Fruit Trees, to be Sold Cheap. What offers?—Powell, Nurseryman, Fearnhead. Tel. 320 y.</p>
        <p>"HINDLE WAKES" TRIUMPH. Success at the Royal Court Theatre.</p>
        <p>"Hindle Wakes" proves a great draw at the Royal Court Theatre this week. The plot is .well known, but still is not hackneyed, and the play teems with brilliant character pictures. Mr. Heys has sent a most talented tcmpany. Fanny Hawthorn, the heroine and unfortunate 'mill lass, is admirably portrayed by Miss Flor- ence Rutter. The other members do full justice to their parts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0289" n="art0289">
        <head>"THE QUEEN MOTHER "-A STIRRING</head>
        <p>"THE QUEEN MOTHER "-A STIRRING</p>
        <p>PLAY.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Campbell's latest and greatest suc- cess, "The Queen Mother," will commence a week's visit to the Royal Court Theatre next Monday. The scene of the story is laid in one of those imaginary countries in which romance is abundant. The play is beautifully mounted and will prove to be one of the most magnificent productions that has ever been seen here. The company has been carefully selected, and is headed by Miss Mary Fulton in the title role.</p>
        <p>"WHAT HO! RAGTIME" AT HIPPODROME, The Mirthquake Draws Big Houses.</p>
        <p>The Hippodrome this Week is quaking with merriment, for "What Ho ! Ragtime" is there, and when it is about there is a super-abundance of mirth. Sparkling music, daring dances, and good gags are all combined in the revue. The piece runs smoothly, and is one of the most attractive sketches ever seen at this popular house. The company is talented and each mem- ber does his or her work admirably. Those who have not yet seen the revue should visit it to-night, for it is one of the best. Other variety artistes contribute excellent turns.</p>
        <p>GREAT HEBREW COMEDIAN NEXT WEEK A powerful bill is promised for next week. Dave Samuels, the great American ragtime Hebrew comedian, tops the bill, and Dave will have some good songs to sing. Alfredo, the vagabond violinist, is a particularly funny fellow and usually keeps his audiences merry. The Yukitos present a sensational act, "A Naval Bombardment." The Five Joy Belles come round with quaint frivolity sketch, and other good turns are provided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0290" n="art0290">
        <head>VARIETY AT THE THEATRE ROYAL.</head>
        <p>VARIETY AT THE THEATRE ROYAL.</p>
        <p>After a most successful week of pantomime, variety turns will occupy the boards of this theatre next week. The artistes will be: Loch and Glen, comedy bar act; Lilian Stone, vocal- ist; and Victoria and Ramoo, equilibrists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0291" n="art0291">
        <head>PICTURE FEATURES</head>
        <p>PICTURE FEATURES</p>
        <p>"SATAN'S CASTLE" AT PICTUREDROME. The great attraction for the first three days next week at the Picturedrome is the thrilling film •" Satan's Castle," in which startling re- sults ensue. On the subsequent three days a military picture, "The Boomerang," will be the star film. .</p>
        <p>STRIKING PICTURES AT STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>Two striking films are promised at the Stz.r, Kinema next week. One depicts the impres- sive State opening of what historians will record as a most momentous Session of Parliament. The other shows the unveiling of the Antarctic Memorial to Scott and his ill-fated comrades.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0292" n="art0292">
        <head>"CAPTAIN KIDD" AT NEW PICTURE</head>
        <p>"CAPTAIN KIDD" AT NEW PICTURE</p>
        <p>HOUSE; Two stirring pictures will be on view at the new Picture House next week. On the first three nights the star film will be "Captain Kidd," a thrilling story. The last three days of the week will see the production of an excit- ing picture, "The Burning Train:'</p>
        <p>ATTRACTIVE FILMS AT PARK PALACE.</p>
        <p>"Why Girls Leave Home" is a startling film due at the Park Picture Palace heat week for the first three nights. "Between Man and Beast" will be there the last three nights of the week. Other tip-top pictures will also be on view.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0293" n="art0293">
        <head>FOOD ADULTERATION.</head>
        <p>FOOD ADULTERATION.</p>
        <p>Do Preservatives Produce Disease ?</p>
        <p>. " An Old Journalist" writes In a forcible letter to the editor, " T.C." writes decidedly to the point on this topic, which some of my readers may recognise as one of my favourites. A Derby correspondent told some years ago how one of the big railway oompanies discovered that large quantities of ground gypsum (plaster of Paris) were being forwarded —chiefly to Birmingham—for distribution to bakers and confectioners. In order to dis- guise the nature of the traffic and to secure the benefit of a low rate for conveyance, this min- eral was consigned as "meal," and passed as flour. lam glad to think that adulteration as a whole is steadily decreasing; and personally I am satisfied that the crude form just indicated no longer exists. All the same, it is disquieting to read that the natural bleaching of flour which used to occupy several weeks has been entirely diiplaced by the use of the fumes of peroxide of nitrogen. For colouring or preservative pur- poses chemicals are added to milk, cream, bacon, ham, coffee, sugar, bread, and practically all we eat; and they are not added, as Professor ITalliburton pointed out some time ago, by the spoonful, but literally by the shovelful. Naturally, the analyst condemns flour "im- provers," remarking that it is no satisfaction to the ordinary buyer of bread to learn that preparations are sold to enable bakers to get 10 to 15 more loaves per sack of flour than before, the obvious result being that the resulting loaves have less nutriment in them. Concern- ing preservatives in food, he. observes that while the use of these .in milk-, &amp;c., for the young is quite prohibited, the principle seems to be established that, within limits, they are admissible in food for adults. The result is that foreign chemical /antiseptics or germicides are present in numerous comestibles of general consumption. Boric acid, salicylic acid, and formaldehyde are the chief preservatives, and some of us may be said to get them at every meal. Starting the day with bleached bread, borated bacon and butter, and jam or marma- lade cum salicylic acid, not to mention the silicated egg, one ploughs bravely through formalised meat, coppered peas, talc-faced rice pudding, and later engages with shrimps, or sausage, or meat pie of even deeper dye, and more butter, bread, cream, and cheese of the same failing as above, not forgetting sine. beer, or other liquid scarcely innocent of a like in- dictment. Boric preservatives are of course used to arrest putrefaction, and this leads to the reminder that when man eats food so pre- served the boric ingredients will arrest the pro- cesses of Nature as regards himself. To boric preservatives authorities have attributed to a great extent the appalling prevalence of Bright's disease. No doubt unscrupulous manufacturers and retailers do this sort of thing solely from the desire to make money, but that is only one of the chief causes of adultera- tion, the other being the mania for cheapness on the part of the purchaser.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0294" n="art0294">
        <head>Shops, Businesses, etc.</head>
        <p>Shops, Businesses, etc.</p>
        <p>_ A LL PERSONS HAVING BUSINESSES TO ZA. SELL are advised to consult Mr. Cowden. 20, Museum-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Money.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0295" n="art0295">
        <head>EducatiOn.</head>
        <p>EducatiOn.</p>
        <p>PAYMENT FOR SERVICE.</p>
        <p>Insurance Committee Want is. for Each</p>
        <p>Attendance.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S JOKING PROPOSAL.</p>
        <p>Wanted: Gentlemen of knowledge and ability, to sit on Insurance 7 Committee and sub-committees. Remuneration: 7s. for each</p>
        <p>meeting. Four meetings per night. Possibly such an advertisement may appear, if, as the Warrington Insurance Committee de- sire, members who sit on the Insurance Com- mittee and the sub-committees should receive is. per meeting. Then there will be some clamouring after the positions. The question was the chief topic discussed at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Insur- ance Committee, presided over by Councillor Dr. Joseph, in the Council Chamber, on Thursday evening.</p>
        <p>The discussion arose on the Finance Com- mittee's instruction to the Clerk to make in- quiries as to what scale of payment had been fixed by other committees. The Mayor said that seeing the. Insurance Commissioners had already granted £25,000 to cover the payment of members at those meet- ings, and that everyone in connection with the insurance world received payment, it was time members of that committee received payment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0296" n="art0296">
        <head>OBJECTION TO PAYMENT.</head>
        <p>OBJECTION TO PAYMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. S. Hill objected to the payment of members, but at the same time said if the other committees did so, Warrington could not stand outside. Alderman Bennett said the matter raised a wide public principle, and, in his Opinion, it was the thin end of the wedge. Mr. Brocklehurst said that in regard to those men who had to leave work to attend the meet- ings', someone must have thought they were qualified, or they would not be appointed. (Hear, hear.) The doctors and chemists got money out of the Act, and the only individuals who got nothing were those who attended those meetings. Mr. Penketh said it was, only fair to give a man something back for losing time. There was then considerable discussion as to the amount to be asked for as payment.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said some Committees had sug- gested 55., is. 6d. and 10s. per meeting.</p>
        <p>7s. PER MEETING.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edes : I movb we make application to the Commissioners for 7s. for each 'meeting. Mr. Binks moved 10s., "to test the feeling of the meeting."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0297" n="art0297">
        <head>10s. A MEETING SUGGESTED.</head>
        <p>10s. A MEETING SUGGESTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Delaney thought that members on sub- committees, where the major portion of the work was done, ought to be paid the 108., as well as those attending the general meeting. Mr. Binks : Very well, 10s. for each meeting. The Clerk: There have been four meetings to- night. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0298" n="art0298">
        <head>MAYOR'S JOKE.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S JOKE.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: I am on all the sub-committees, and I shall be tempted to give up the Mayoralty for these big salaries. (Laughter.) I think the larger sum for this committee and something less for sub-committee meetings, unless you have a low average all round. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Binks : I say 10s. for each meeting.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: That Would be 12 for to-night. (Laughter.) Mr. Forster: Can we make any more com- mittees? (Laughter.) • • The Mayor: We shall have special meetings. After further discussion it was decided to recommend is. for each meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0299" n="art0299">
        <head>FARMERS IN THE PEERAGE.</head>
        <p>FARMERS IN THE PEERAGE.</p>
        <p>-- It is interesting, to note that there are a forge number of members of the Upper House who are actively engaged in agriculture. Of Vile Dukes, their Graces of Bedford, Portland, Marlborough, and Westminster are the most distinguished practical agriculturists. A friend of the Duke of Portland once de- clared that "His Grace would be a first rate fellow only for his crops;" the Duke's enthu- siasm about his "crops" was not shared by his friend who knew nothing about the especial difficulties encountered in the culti- vation of heavy soils such as are found about Welbeck, or the triumph experienced by ihe agridulturist who overcomes them as the Duke has done. The tillage fields at Welbeek are indeed a marvel of cleanliitess; there is not a sign of a weed anywhere whilst the fine "tilth of the soil tells of much careful farming. The " Duke of Portland does not go in much for cattle breeding but his crops are perhaps the finest in England and he has won numbers of prizes for potatoes, carrots and turnips. A ViTAGEIL</p>
        <p>arlborough on till, other • Tkl9 Duke of in lithe rearing of high brad hand specialist's - thered together a Jersey cattle la.nid has Vinwasely valuable; some that averaged Jerseys wereo years waghoichat Tare ring some of Lord Rothschild's £35 a head. at dealer rom ht th Blenheim wagered a five pound note with soil member of the cattle dealing frater- t the figure high though it was would ben:°nitu°Yntbitletellailarten before six months. The Blenheim dealer won his het for some of the Duke of Marlborough's Jerseys realised .g 45 per head few months later, probably the biggest igure ever paid for the sort of cattle The Marquess of Winchester was the chief Emyer at the sale in question and paid £l2O for "Charley's Pride" (me of the most famous Jersey bulls in the world. The Duke of Bedford's exploits in the world 34r agriculture are probably better known than those of any other peer. The Dukes of Bedford 14ave blen famous as rarmers for generations and English agriculture owes a great deal to their efforts to improve the science and knowledge of farming. The fifth Duke was one of th: founders of like Smithfield club and was a pioneer of the nitxteri CattLe Show. There is an experi- mental farm at Woburn the Duke's seat in Bedfordshire where many interesting and useful experiments in agriculture have been 3arried out directly under the present Duke a supervision. AN AMUSING STORY.</p>
        <p>A rather amusing story is told about the fate Duke who went to see some of his cattle sold at a certain market. His Grace was standing near the animals who were in charge of a herd when he was approached by a certain cattle dealer, "Are you selling thee° cattle, my man ?" asked the dealer. yes,' replied the Duke of Redford. % " They are the Duke of Bedford's." "Yes. Then began Oh -usual bargaining which takes place over every deal at a cattle mar- ket when suddenly the farmer exclaimed. "Look here, my man, the Duke is a fair man, and if he were to ask me £l5 a head himself for these cattle I would give that money for them, but clang me if I will give it to you." His Grace smiled, as be replied, "Well, [ am the Duke and you can have the cattle at £l5 a head but not for one farthine• lees. ' The farmer stuck to his word and duly paid the money.</p>
        <p>Apart from the Dukes mentioned there are of course many peers who farm extensively and personally supervise the conduct of their agricultural operations. °Lord Barrymore, Lord Rayleigh, Lord Rothschild. Lord Sefton, Lord Lonsdale and Lord Rosel)ery are some of the most notable titled agriculturists. A GIGANTIC DAIRY FARM.</p>
        <p>Lord Rayleigh, began his farming opera- tions in a very email way; after various ten- tativg operations in diVerent directions Ee came to the conclusion that the most remun- erative branch of agriculture was dairy farm- ing and he began by supplying the needs of quite a small local market; slowly and surely however the dairy business at terling grew until it deve!oped into one of the most gigan- tic dairy farms in the country. Hundreds of huge highly nolished coron-</p>
        <p>FARMERS IN THE PEERAGE—continui</p>
        <p>sten milk cans leave the farm twice a 4 kr the railway station, the contents of wb are consumed by thousands of persons over the country. Lord Rayleigh is by way married to a sister of Mr. Arthur f four who has been over the farm at Tenl on several occasions and was greatly jut ested in the details of its management. Lord Rosebery farms over 2,000 acres Dalmeny where as the result of certain periments made some years Ago the theories regarding the growing of grain Cr were completely upset and a system adop that now is more or less generally followed all agriculturists in this country. Lord Rosebery exports seeds from his I .neny farms to all parts of the world; am( the large buyers from his lordship being farmer in Southern Russia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-02-14_WidnesExaminer_art0300" n="art0300">
        <head>FARM AT TRING.</head>
        <p>THE FARM AT TRING.</p>
        <p>Lord Buth€ellild's dairy cattle at Tring perliapB the inobt noted of their class ia.'l country. Buyers from all parts of the 'Tr, come to pick up some of the Trine" whenever there is a. chance of cloin:6o a' these</p>
        <p>prkes. Lord Rothschild was of course founder of the Tring Agricultural Sociel under whose auspices one of the most not! agricultural shows in the kitgdom ke annnallv. The farm at Tring extends over 1,500 act; and the dairy is supplied altogether by t' milk from tke splendid Jersey cows. Until the dairy at Sandring,ham all-modern and u to-date dairying machinery is to be u°4 there and any new idea that appears to Ili. something in it gets a trial at Tring. dairy house is a detached building scrup lously clean and devoid of all unnecessary nament either inside or out; a TOW of .tr:e runs by one side of the building 'CO affords sheltei• from the sun in the sumul for nothing is /40 fatal to good dairy manag meat as undue heat. A gigantic separator used which removes the cream from nine' gallons of milk in the space of forty minute The milk of Jersey cattle is by far the riche of any other dairy stock and has a .pecull' brignt golden colour which to the uninitiat' does not always commend itself as was ti case once with a visitor to Tring who want! to know "Why the milk. wasn't white This Jersey milk is the class of milk tt commands the largest price in the mallk and large quantities of it are despatched London twice a day from Tring..</p>
