        <p>ANCIENT FOREST REMAINS IN NEW ZEALAND. A remarkable discovery has recently been dis- closed in Auckland, where the Railway Depart- ment is making a site for new engine sheds. In digging away the hillside some 20 feet thick, they have come on a 12-feet seam of lignite, which represents a primieval forest wiiiclr flour- ished several thousand years ago. The deposit is valueless from all but a scientific standfoint, since it is of the consistency of cheese or yellow soap and cannot be shattered by dynamite or displaced by picks, the men having to get rid of it very slowly by digging into it with wet spades. Specimens of kauri gum and complete logs of well-preserved kauri and other woods have been found in the lignite, and it is as- sumed by geologists that Oie whole is the rem- nant of a very ancient forest which was over- whelmed by one of the volcanic disturbances which deluged New Zealand thousands of years before the country was inhabited.</p>
