        <p>A ELENINISCERE Or THE WARVECTON ELECTIn 41 GOT THAT VOTE; DID I NOT DESERVE IT?" The cartoonist was a factor at as early a day as 1868, and we are able to reproduce whdt was perhaps the best hit of the Election. It 15 said that Mr. Greenall, when canvassing, found a woman at work at an old-fashioned mangle. He gallantly doffed his coat and offered his help, at the same time getting the woman's promise that her husband should be made to vote Tory. The label that bangs from the coat Pocket sug- gests that the candidate was angling for a baronetcy, and the brewer's dray of the picture on the wall tells its own story. It is worth while to reproduce the picture if only to show how clothes were mangled in the long ago.</p>
        <p>_ • ELECTION POETRY, • . The poet was much more prominent in the elections of former days; and his outpourings, go,o,dl:lobgagdinagn,odi indifferent, were sprinkled over the town. Two sample's will be sufficient. . , They mark the distance we have travelled in good manners. and poetry, since 1868. refers to Hogging in the Army. , ..• • To the • PETER'S THE MAN I FREE AND INDEPENDENT . ELECTORS Come all ye brave men, of the , Who for Rylands contend, Good Old Pocket Borough of Warrington. , Come, give him your votes, LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, He's the Workingmen's friend. In cold, dark November, about the third Week, Like true British subjects I'll come on my knees, yoar suffrage to seek. Let's conquer our foe, Then Rylands with Gladstone , * ***** 0 • To Parliament will go. [Some allusions to " The 'Prade" and its effect Up, up, and be doing, lads, upon the town are too strong to copy even at Come bold on that day; this distance of time.] And the true-hearted lib'ral , Will sure win his way. Our cause is a good one; . All the town will soon know, • , That Rylande with Gladstone My trade I'll pursue, and destruction I'll spread , To Parliament will go. Until my best patrons with the "blues" are all dead. . I Will build you a church, and endow it you see, "But," says the old Tory, If you wont send Peter Rylande to be your BI,P. "Peter's head is so big." I give you a vote, and make you pay rates, Why, those Tory old fools And bring down destruction on all your poor pates. Don't know he's a W(b)ig; But there's Peter Ryland, you very well know, And under that wig Whenever he comes with tile 'tis no go; He's got sense,—they must know He brings a strong wire, and twitches me up, That Peter's the right man, And makes my poor face like a bit of catgut. • And to Parliament must go. He's canvassed the town for many a day, And for Peter the Great I've got to give way. For the Warrington Tories On my old age have pity—have pity on me, Have near had their day, And let me once more be your Tory M.P: In sending a member I'll vote with Old Dizzy, and take a firm stand That's got nothing to say, ' Against all the Gladstones and Brights in the land. But will vote for taxation, . When away iii the highlands, seeking repose, . And flogging of men,— The anguish I suffer'd, why nobody knows, Thou must stay at home, Gilbert f In thinking of Peter—oh, how he haunts me, We wont have thee again. He is so determined to be your M.P. Then to Walton Hall I suppose I must go, "But," says the old Tory, And spend my last days in misery and woe. ' " Peter's the gift of the gab." But I'll come for the tin of you poor drunken wretches, Yes, yes, tny old friend, And send you to gaol from the Magistrate's benches. - He's just the man we must have. WALTON ARCHES. For poor simple Gilbert Can say nothing, you know; So Peter's the right man, , And with Gladstone will go. C. H. . , .</p>
        <p>that I shall be able to make all my own clothes, and my sister's. - BEATRICE MARSHALL. 75, Folly-lane, Warrington. AS A MUSICIAN. Dear Uncle Jack,—My greatest ambition is to become .a first-class musician. lam taking lessons on the piano, and my tutor says I am going on very well, so I hope before long I shall be able to boast the cap and gown. My reasons are as follow: I think music is one of the most ennobling and fascinating arts, and I feel that I am sensible to its charms. Cissiz MEREDITH. 99, Longford-street, Warrington. TO FIGHT LIFE'S BATTLES. Dear Uncle Tack,—l should like to inform youl as to, what I should like to become when I grow up to manhood. First of all, I should like to make myself a character which will enable me to face the world as an honest citizen, and I consider that will assist me to fight "the battle of life." I should like to become a responsible male shorthand typist and. qualify myself to make my ability felt, and to be a great help to all I may come in contact with. I should like to be a clerk because I am undergoing special training at Wood's Tutorial College for the mentioned post. REGINALD GALLOP. 72, Norris-street, Orford-avenue.</p>
